\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18I wasn\u8217\'20\'19t allowed to get the healthcare I needed\u8217\'20\'19: the women suing Tennessee for being denied abortions\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 1,615 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 February 2024\par 15:38 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 K Monica Kelly had to travel to Florida for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after her fetus was diagnosed with trisomy 13 \u8211\'20\'13 now she\u8217\'20\'19s part of a group suing her state\par \par \uc2 When K Monica Kelly saw that women in Texas had {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/nov/28/texas-supreme-court-hears-extreme-abortion-ban-lawsuit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filed a lawsuit}}}\uc2  challenging the contours of their state\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, she posted on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  to cheer them on.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI shared how terrible I thought it was, that they weren\u8217\'20\'19t able to get the proper healthcare they needed in their state,\u8221\'20\'1D Kelly said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt never crossed my mind that that was actually going to happen to me soon.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Kelly and her husband spent a year trying to have a second baby. So when they discovered in February 2023 that Kelly was pregnant, the couple was ecstatic. They taught their son, who was then two years old, to describe their family as: \u8220\'20\'1CMama, dada, me, baby, all four!\u8221\'20\'1D After an ultrasound looked promising, they drove more than 10 hours from their home in northern Tennessee to announce the news to their family in Florida.\par \par \uc2 Only days later, after they\u8217\'20\'19d returned home, in late March, the pair drove back to Florida. This time, though, the drive was \u8220\'20\'1Csurreal and devastating\u8221\'20\'1D, Kelly said. A series of catastrophic fetal diagnoses had led Kelly to decide to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 a procedure she could not legally get in Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s awful and agonizing to just even drive that far to go do something that you just really don\u8217\'20\'19t want to do,\u8221\'20\'1D Kelly said. \u8220\'20\'1CEven up until the last second, I wished that I could change my mind. But I just knew that that was the best decision.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 More than a dozen states have implemented near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans since the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade in June 2022. Although {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2023/nov/10/state-abortion-laws-us"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 every state with an \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ban}}}\uc2  has some kind of exception that should theoretically allow for abortions in medical emergencies, doctors and patients across the country have said that the bans are worded in such a way as to be unworkable in reality. Misinterpreting the exceptions could lead providers to shoulder not only hefty fines, but also lifelong prison sentences.\par \par \uc2 As a result, dozens of women from across the country have come forward with stories of being denied medically necessary abortions. Now, Kelly, like the women in Texas, is one of them: earlier this year, she joined a lawsuit in Tennessee that was filed by multiple women who say they were turned away from abortions they desperately needed.\par \par \uc2 After Roe fell, Tennessee rushed to implement what was believed to be one of the strictest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in the country. Lawmakers have since carved out extremely narrow exceptions in the law \u8211\'20\'13 but advocates say they are nowhere near enough to ensure doctors can provide what is often lifesaving care.\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18 I was trying to make a compassionate decision for my baby\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Kelly\u8217\'20\'19s fetus was diagnosed with trisomy 13, a chromosomal disorder that can result in a range of grave issues, according to Kelly\u8217\'20\'19s lawsuit. The fetus had large growths on the neck and back, as well as elevated swelling in the tissues and organs.\par \par \uc2 The fetus also had a condition, associated with trisomy 13, that had led the brain to fail to split correctly, Kelly said: \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s associated with really severe complications, like facial deformities, like only having one eye and really terrible things like that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Many fetuses diagnosed with trisomy 13 do not make it to birth; if they are born, the infants need extensive medical care and still likely only live for hours or days.\par \par \uc2 If Kelly had continued the pregnancy, the fetus could have started experiencing pain, according to the lawsuit. Kelly faced the risk of developing medical complications herself, too, such as preeclampsia, a risky blood pressure condition.\par \par \uc2 But Tennessee bans almost all abortions after conception. Under the state\u8217\'20\'19s ban, Kelly\u8217\'20\'19s doctor and genetic counselor told her, Kelly could not get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Tennessee, according to her lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI felt confused and stressed about where to go and what to do when I wasn\u8217\'20\'19t allowed to get the healthcare I needed in Tennessee,\u8221\'20\'1D Kelly said. \u8220\'20\'1CJust, the whole thing was drawn out even longer than it needed to be because I was trying to figure out where to go and I was trying to make a compassionate decision for my baby.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She considered getting an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Illinois, a state that has become an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  haven since Roe\u8217\'20\'19s downfall; she made and cancelled appointments. She didn\u8217\'20\'19t feel right going somewhere she had never been for such a personal procedure. \u8220\'20\'1CI was seeking any comfort I could possibly grab onto,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Finally, Kelly was able to get an appointment at a Florida hospital, with an OB-GYN she knew and trusted. But going to Florida came with its own challenges.\par \par \uc2 Kelly realized she was running out of time: Florida bans abortions past 15 weeks of pregnancy, and she was coming up against that limit. In addition, patients in Florida must visit an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider in person for counseling, wait 24 hours, and return for the procedure itself.\par \par \uc2 Kelly and her husband also had to make the 10-hour-plus trek to north-west Florida with their toddler in tow. Just remembering that drive, Kelly said, gives her goosebumps. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s hard for me to put myself back in that state of mind sometimes, because it was so traumatic for me that I think my brain is trying to protect itself and forget,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t remember what I did. I just went forward.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Kelly and her husband saved money by staying at a friend\u8217\'20\'19s place, but the whole trip set the couple back \u8220\'20\'1Cat least several thousands of dollars\u8221\'20\'1D, Kelly estimated. \u8220\'20\'1CIt was a lot more stressful and confusing than it would have been had we been able to just stay in Tennessee.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 At the hospital, Kelly cried before she slipped under anesthesia. The nurses held her hand.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey were really compassionate and kind,\u8221\'20\'1D Kelly said, her voice choking up with tears. \u8220\'20\'1CThat was the hardest part \u8211\'20\'13 just not wanting to go through with it or be there but knowing it had to be done, and then having someone just tell me it was gonna be OK. They made me feel comfortable.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 By the time of her \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , on 31 March 2023, Kelly was roughly 15 weeks pregnant, according to her lawsuit \u8211\'20\'13 just about the legal limit in Florida. But \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws in Florida may soon shift again: the Florida state supreme court is weighing a case that may lead to a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, which would outlaw the procedure before many people know they\u8217\'20\'19re pregnant.\par \par \uc2 After her procedure, Kelly reached out to Allie Phillips, a Tennessee mom who went public on social media with her experience of having to travel out of state for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Phillips, who is now {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/oct/16/tennessee-abortion-ban-lawsuit-allie-phillips-representative"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 running for a seat in the Tennessee state legislature}}}\uc2 , was one of the first women to sue Tennessee over the exceptions in its \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban \u8211\'20\'13 months later, Kelly decided to join the lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 Seven women who say they were denied abortions in Tennessee, including Kelly and Phillips, are a part of the lawsuit. One ended up traveling to Virginia for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and was later diagnosed with sepsis due to an infection that, her doctors said, had gotten worse because her \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been delayed. Another woman learned that her pregnancy was unlikely to result in a healthy baby, but had to continue it against her will. She delivered a stillborn son 31 weeks into her pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI hope that it makes a difference. I feel like I\u8217\'20\'19m proud to be a part of it, although it\u8217\'20\'19s something I never wanted to be a part of,\u8221\'20\'1D Kelly said. \u8220\'20\'1CI just hope that I can make a difference for women\u8217\'20\'19s rights.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A hearing in the case is set for April.\par \par \uc2 In April 2023, Tennessee\u8217\'20\'19s Republican governor signed into law a bill that slightly expanded \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers\u8217\'20\'19 legal protections. Under the original version of the law, all abortions had technically been felonies \u8211\'20\'13 but doctors who performed them could claim in court that the abortions had been performed to save a patient\u8217\'20\'19s life or prevent a serious injury. Under the new law, doctors could use their \u8220\'20\'1Creasonable medical judgment\u8221\'20\'1D to perform abortions in severe emergencies, a standard that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates say still opens doctors up to prosecution.\par \par \uc2 Lawyers for the women, who are being represented by the \uc2 Center for Reproductive Rights\uc2 , are asking the courts to issue a temporary injunction that would allow doctors instead to use their \u8220\'20\'1Cgood faith medical judgment\u8221\'20\'1D to perform abortions when a patient has a \u8220\'20\'1Ccritical or emergent physical medical condition\u8221\'20\'1D that threatens their health, future fertility or life.\par \par \uc2 The Texas lawsuit, meanwhile, is under deliberation by the state supreme court. A similar case, filed in Idaho last year, continues.\par \par \uc2 Kelly is pregnant again. She\u8217\'20\'19s due in June 2024.\par \par \uc2 The fear of something happening to this pregnancy and, in particular, the fear of being pregnant again in Tennessee lingers. Kelly didn\u8217\'20\'19t buy any new baby outfits, or tell her son about the pregnancy, until after testing confirmed her fetus had no medical issues.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe did not start trying until my previous baby\u8217\'20\'19s due date had passed. I wasn\u8217\'20\'19t mentally or physically ready,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe actually ended up conceiving this baby the weekend of our former baby\u8217\'20\'19s due date. Things just felt right.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240201ek21002e5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
