\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Pennsylvania case challenging ban on Medicaid abortions back in court\b0\par\par\uc2 Erum Salam \par \uc2 404 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 January 2024\par 00:49 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Supreme court rules lower court must hear case arguing decades-old law barring coverage for procedure should be overturned\par \par \uc2 Pennsylvania\u8217\'20\'19s supreme court ruled on Monday that a lower court must hear a case challenging a ban on the use of government-funded healthcare to pay for abortions, raising hopes among reproductive rights advocates for an expansion of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the state and the establishment of a constitutional right to the procedure.\par \par \uc2 The case, brought by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in the state, challenged a decades-old state law barring Medicaid from covering abortions, arguing that it should be overturned because it violates the broader rights guaranteed by the state constitution\u8217\'20\'19s Equal Rights Amendment.\par \par \uc2 Monday\u8217\'20\'19s 3-2 ruling both overturns a lower court decision to dismiss the case and puts aside a 1985 state supreme court court decision that upheld the law banning the use of state Medicaid dollars for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , except in cases of rape, incest or to save the life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 The majority said the lower court must reconsider whether Medicaid can legally draw a distinction \u8220\'20\'1Cbetween pregnant women on Medical Assistance who would seek to obtain abortions and pregnant women on Medical Assistance who would seek to carry their pregnancies to term\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Two of the justices also wrote that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a \u8220\'20\'1Cfundamental right\u8221\'20\'1D under Pennsylvania\u8217\'20\'19s constitution, signaling the possibility of a broader ruling if the case makes its way back up to the state\u8217\'20\'19s highest court.\par \par \uc2 In 2022, the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned the landmark Roe v Wade decision, ending the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  across the country and leaving the decision up to individual states. Since then, a number of state courts have been forced to contend with legal questions around access the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Abortions remain legal in Pennsylvania until 24 weeks, but a 1982 law barred the use of Medicaid to pay for the procedure. A lower court will now decide the constitutionality of that law.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Court gave our clients an enormous victory this morning,\u8221\'20\'1D said Susan J Frietsche, co-executive director of the Women\u8217\'20\'19s Law Project and one of the lawyers who argued the case. \u8220\'20\'1CWe are still determining next steps, but we are confident the Medicaid \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban will be consigned to the scrapheap of history very soon.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Associated Press contributed to this report\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240130ek1u0002v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
