\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  rights are Biden\u8217\'20\'19s most powerful re-election issue. He should act like it\b0\par\par\uc2 Moira Donegan \par \uc2 1,291 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 January 2024\par 18:38 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a crucial issue in this election \u8211\'20\'13 and a powerful motivator for voters. Yet the president is still tiptoeing around it\par \par \uc2 For years, the beltway set had a standard line of advice for Democratic candidates: stick to the economy. The idea was that white, male, blue-collar voters \u8211\'20\'13 those magical creatures, somewhere out there in the windswept lands of the upper midwest, who always qualify in the pundit imagination as \u8220\'20\'1Creal Americans\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 would be turned off by so-called culture-war issues.\par \par \uc2 These guys, we were told, didn\u8217\'20\'19t want to hear about civil rights or social equality: they wanted to hear about economic growth. According to this advice, Democrats could be pro-choice, pro-racial justice, or pro-LGBTQ rights, but not openly, avowedly so. They had to play their progressive social positions in a minor key.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s not clear that this advice ever really paid off for Democratic candidates. At any rate, you don\u8217\'20\'19t hear it much any more. That\u8217\'20\'19s because, for the past two years, Democratic electoral victories up and down the ballot have been driven disproportionately by one of those culture-war issues that candidates were typically told to avoid: \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 American women\u8217\'20\'19s anger over the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Dobbs ruling is the single most potent political force in America right now, and if \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  wins re-election \u8211\'20\'13 a distinct if imperiled possibility \u8211\'20\'13 it will be because his campaign succeeded in making the election a referendum on Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. There is no one issue with greater importance; there are few issues that have ever motivated voters so dramatically.\par \par \uc2 You would think that this would be a gift to the Biden campaign. On paper, Republicans are almost solely responsible for the overturning of Roe and the draconian, morbid, and dangerous \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans that have followed.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  continually brags about appointing three of the six justices who ruled to eliminate the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  right; Republican politicians nationwide, not content with being able to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , have sought to eliminate life and health exemptions, to further restrict gestational age limits, and to impose criminal and civil penalties for things like advocating for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights or transporting a patient across state lines. These are hateful, bigoted, invasive, and lawless moves, ones that degrade women\u8217\'20\'19s citizenship and are hated by the public. And they\u8217\'20\'19re Republican moves.\par \par \uc2 But the new prominence of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in electoral politics presents something of a conundrum for the Biden campaign: because while Republicans are vehemently anti-choice, Biden himself is not a particularly convincing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocate.\par \par \uc2 He is, at best, unenthused about the issue. Biden speaks of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in stilted, euphemistic terms, talking about \u8220\'20\'1Crestoring the protections of Roe\u8221\'20\'1D or \u8220\'20\'1Ca woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to choose\u8221\'20\'1D more than \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D. (He did not use the word in public remarks until he was forced to after facing pressure from activists.) On the stump, he frequently ad libs, straying from prepared remarks to make his dislike of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clear. In one set of remarks last year, he unhelpfully offered that he was \u8220\'20\'1Cnot big on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In remarks this past week, he characterized his own position using anti-choice buzzwords, saying he was opposed to \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on demand.\u8221\'20\'1D Most of the campaigning on the issue has been passed off to \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , admittedly a more comfortable messenger for a women\u8217\'20\'19s rights platform. But outsourcing such a prominent issue to the vice-president is itself fraught with symbolic dangers: the campaign risks signaling that they consider \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be a second-tier issue by assigning it to their second-tier principal. And Harris is limited in what she can say by the somewhat narrow extent of the president\u8217\'20\'19s comfort.\par \par \uc2 And so Biden has taken on the task of marketing himself as a champion of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights with all the relish of a third-grader told to eat his broccoli: he has been informed that doing so is good for him, but he really, really doesn\u8217\'20\'19t want to. This week, as the Biden administration launched a series of policy and public relations efforts meant to frame the stakes of the elections for voters invested in reproductive freedom, things got off to something of a rocky start.\par \par \uc2 Last Monday, on what would have been Roe\u8217\'20\'19s 51st anniversary, Biden held a task force meeting in which he said that his administration would defend laws legalizing things like the \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval of mifepristone, which is being challenged by anti-choice lawyers in court. He said he would create a team to educate the public about when emergency abortions are legal in hospitals \u8211\'20\'13 a growing need in an era when more and more pregnant women are facing disastrous health risks because of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans that prohibit the procedure from being used to spare them from catastrophic harm. He said he would encourage access to birth control.\par \par \uc2 It was a tepid announcement, one where Biden seemed self-satisfied for doing the bare minimum. It was a policy agenda, too, that leaves all the agenda-setting power in the anti-choice movement\u8217\'20\'19s hands: what the Biden campaign is offering American women \u8211\'20\'13 the ones who are angry and distraught, the ones that have suffered a blow to their dignity and an endangering of their safety \u8211\'20\'13 is that his administration might be willing to make minimal efforts to stop the people who are working maximally hard to make it worse.\par \par \uc2 At a rally in Wisconsin the next day, Harris seemed more interested in describing the post-Dobbs landscape as one of a \u8220\'20\'1Chealthcare crisis\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 emphasizing, as Biden has, the stories of women denied life \u8211\'20\'13 and health-preserving abortions in moments of medical emergency. And it is true that the post-Dobbs world is one where it has become dramatically more dangerous to be pregnant, one where a capricious law, or a doctor\u8217\'20\'19s fear of one, could cost you your life, your health, or your fertility in the event that something goes wrong. And it is true, too, as Harris told the crowd, that a Republican victory would almost certainly result in a national ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 something a Republican president could effect in practice even without a filibuster-proof majority in Congress.\par \par \uc2 But the campaign\u8217\'20\'19s focus on these aspects of the Dobbs catastrophe \u8211\'20\'13 the women suffering complications from wanted pregnancies, the potential that things could get worse \u8211\'20\'13 does too little to grapple with the harm that\u8217\'20\'19s happening right now, to women who simply do not want to be pregnant, and who deserve to be treated with the respect and dignity of citizens, not talked down to like children who cannot be trusted to act as custodians of their own bodily functions.\par \par \uc2 Biden was not wrong when he said that women who were forced to become sicker and sicker during miscarriages before they were allowed to obtain abortions were subjected to an indignity. But so, too, are those who the law treats as de facto incompetent or suspicious: those who want and deserve their abortions, in Biden\u8217\'20\'19s contemptuous phrasing, \u8220\'20\'1Con demand\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 If anything, Biden is talking like he believes that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains a delicate issue, as if it is something he thinks he will lose by being too strong on. But that advice, which maybe never quite worked, was from another time. It is not advice for this moment. Biden needs to change his strategy on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to bring it more in line with both the sentiments of voters and the demands of our era. It is time for him to grow up, and eat his vegetables.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Moira Donegan is a Guardian US columnist\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240129ek1t001mo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
