\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Biden hopes  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  will keep him in the White House. But has he done enough to protect rights?\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 1,483 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 January 2024\par 14:25 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The president has made big promises and held the line against legal challenges, but activists say he could be doing more\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s re-election campaign has made a big bet that outrage over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will keep the president in the White House come November.\par \par \uc2 Over the last several days, the Biden administration has unleashed a blitz of ads and events to spotlight the devastation wrought by the overturning of Roe v Wade. Biden met with a reproductive health task force, while his vice-president, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 who he has entrusted to lead this effort \u8211\'20\'13 embarked on a national tour to talk about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They even devoted their first joint campaign stop of 2024 to the issue.\par \par \uc2 From the podium, Biden promised to sign any bill that would codify Roe\u8217\'20\'19s protections into law and to fight back efforts by Congress to diminish \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Maga Republicans, including the speaker of the House, are hellbent on going even further,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2021/12/30/opinion/abortion-pills-biden.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Biden said}}}\uc2 , a reference to the hard-right Republican speaker, Mike Johnson. \u8220\'20\'1CAs long as I have power of the presidency, if Congress were to pass a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, I would veto it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Congress is unlikely to ban or protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  anytime soon. Not only is Congress largely frozen \u8211\'20\'13 it passed just 27 bills last year \u8211\'20\'13 but both political parties seem wary of tackling national legislation around a third-rail topic like \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now that Roe is gone, the question of if and how to regulate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access is largely up to state governments to answer. But the executive branch of the \uc2 US government\uc2  still maintains several powers to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access \u8211\'20\'13 and undermine it.\par \par \uc2 What has Biden done to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access?\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration\u8217\'20\'19s ability to enforce remaining federal laws that touch on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is perhaps its greatest weapon in the fight over the procedure. Shortly after Roe\u8217\'20\'19s demise, the Biden administration announced that it believed a 1986 federal law that protects people\u8217\'20\'19s access to emergency care at hospitals also applies to emergency abortions. The administration later sued Idaho, arguing that the state\u8217\'20\'19s near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban flew in the face of that law, the Emergency Medical Treatment and Labor Act (Emtala).\par \par \uc2 That case has now made its way to the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 . The supreme court justices are also set to hear arguments in a case involving the availability of a major \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill \u8211\'20\'13 a case in which the Biden administration is, once again, arguing in favor of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBeing a check on the supreme court is pretty significant,\u8221\'20\'1D said Mary Ziegler, a \uc2 University of California Davis\uc2  school of law professor who studies the legal history of reproduction. The \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  is dominated 6-3 by conservatives. \u8220\'20\'1CIf the supreme court says that you can or should enforce rules against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, I don\u8217\'20\'19t think a Biden administration is going to do that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Since Roe fell, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists have also begun to argue that the federal government could enforce a de facto national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban through the Comstock Act, a 19th-century anti-obscenity law that bans the mailing of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related materials.\par \par \uc2 However, the Biden administration has issued guidance declaring that they do not believe the Comstock Act can or should be used to enforce a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. According to the Biden administration, as long as someone does not intend to break the law when they mail \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related materials, they are not violating the Comstock Act.\par \par \uc2 What more could Biden be doing?\par \par \uc2 The answer depends on who you ask. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates have long been dissatisfied with Biden\u8217\'20\'19s approach to the procedure; Biden has supported Roe\u8217\'20\'19s protections but also said that, as a Catholic, he is personally {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/joe-biden-abortion-catholic-faith-roe-v-wade-got-it-right/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 not \u8220\'20\'1Cbig on \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 \u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2 . During his campaign and the first several months of his presidency, he seemed wary of even saying the word \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D, leading reproductive justice advocates to launch a website devoted to answering the question \u8220\'20\'1CDid Biden Say \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Yet?\u8221\'20\'1D (He has now said it multiple times.)\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/sep/16/face-act-anti-abortion-threaten-clinics"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pursued several cases}}}\uc2  under the Freedom of Access to Clinic Entrances Act, or Face Act, a federal law that penalizes people for threatening, obstructing, or injuring someone who is trying to access a reproductive health clinic, or for vandalizing a clinic. But \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers have long complained that the law is not being enforced enough.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters have also proposed a litany of other, experimental ways to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, such as by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2021/12/30/opinion/abortion-pills-biden.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 leasing federal land to \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  providers}}}\uc2  or {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2024/01/22/opinions/abortion-threat-comstock-act-must-be-repealed-cohen-donley-rebouche/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 advocating for the repeal}}}\uc2  of the Comstock Act. Biden could also loosen regulations around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, although Ziegler cautioned that such actions run the risk of politicizing the \uc2 US Food and Drug Administration (FDA)\uc2  to a dangerous degree.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates have also said that the Biden administration could take steps to lessen the impact of the Helms Amendment, a decades-old law that has been used to block the use of federal funding to pay for abortions. Advocates have accused Biden of inappropriately over-enforcing the Helms Amendment, to the point that the \uc2 US Agency for International Development\uc2  in 2021 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.rcog.org.uk/news/us-helms-amendment-is-misogynistic-legislation-preventing-women-from-accessing-safe-abortion-around-the-world-says-rcog/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cancelled a conference session}}}\uc2  on the provision of safe telemedicine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 However, in Ziegler\u8217\'20\'19s view, the threat of the supreme court tamps down on Biden\u8217\'20\'19s ability to innovate. Rather than pursuing novel, national ways to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and run the risk of litigation, the administration may want to stay out of federal court entirely.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think Biden has been really cautious,\u8221\'20\'1D Ziegler said. \u8220\'20\'1CBut I do also think that had he not been as cautious, it could have ended up the same or worse anyway, just because the supreme court is so conservative.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 What could \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  do to further restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 If Trump wins the presidency in November 2024, he may reverse course on many of the Biden administration\u8217\'20\'19s decisions around how and if to enforce federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law. He could try to implement the Comstock Act to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in some form, including in states that haven\u8217\'20\'19t passed bans. He could also decrease Face Act prosecutions, or tighten regulations on mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 Unlike Biden, he likely wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t worry about politicizing the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , Ziegler said. \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s a lot of asymmetry that hurts Democrats, but also Democrats do value some of these institutional separations that Republicans don\u8217\'20\'19t.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s first four years in the White House also offer a blueprint for how he may further dismantle access to both \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and contraception if he returns to power.\par \par \uc2 Since the 1980s, whenever a Republican becomes president, he has implemented what is known as \u8220\'20\'1Cthe Mexico City policy\u8221\'20\'1D or the \u8220\'20\'1Cglobal gag rule\u8221\'20\'1D, as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters call it. This policy typically blocks foreign NGOs that receive US family planning funding from providing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related services or even advocating for increased access to the procedure. (Historically, whenever a Democrat replaces a Republican as president, he has rescinded the Mexico City policy.)\par \par \uc2 Trump, however, turbocharged the Mexico City policy during his presidency. Rather than stripping funding only from family planning assistance, in 2017 his administration expanded it to apply to all US global health assistance. Rather than impacting $600m worth of funding, by 2018 it impacted $12bn, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/gpr/2020/04/unprecedented-expansion-global-gag-rule-trampling-rights-health-and-free-speech"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to estimates by the Guttmacher Institute}}}\uc2 , which supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 As president, Trump also implemented a \u8220\'20\'1Cdomestic gag rule\u8221\'20\'1D, which blocked members of Title X, the nation\u8217\'20\'19s largest family planning program, from even referring people for abortions. Rather than comply with this rule, a quarter of Title X-funded health centers simply left the program. Six states were left with zero Title X providers, who offer low-cost access to family planning services like birth control.\par \par \uc2 If Trump wins in 2024, he will likely reinstate this rule, said Robin Summers, vice-president and senior counsel for the \uc2 National Family Planning and Reproductive Health Association\uc2 . And that\u8217\'20\'19s just the beginning of Summers thinks he might do.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think it only gets worse,\u8221\'20\'1D Summers said.\par \par \uc2 Trump could, Summers suggested, legally label certain forms of hormonal birth control \u8211\'20\'13 such as IUDS \u8211\'20\'13 as abortifacients, suggesting that they cause abortions. (Medical experts widely believe that they do not.) The \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  has previously supported a similar move. In a 2014 decision, issued when the court\u8217\'20\'19s makeup was far less conservative, the justices ruled that a corporation did not have to cover certain forms of birth control for employees because the corporation\u8217\'20\'19s religious owners believed them to be abortifacients.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe bottom line here is that advocates sounded the alarm for years that Roe was at significant risk of being overturned. And we were dismissed by many as catastrophizing the whole thing,\u8221\'20\'1D Summers said. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd look where we are.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240126ek1q001s0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
