\par\par \uc2 Society\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18I didn\u8217\'20\'19t know what I was supposed to do\u8217\'20\'19: US women who miscarry are in dangerous legal limbo post-Roe\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 2,287 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 January 2024\par 19:28 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 With near-total bans on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 14 states, the laws on miscarriage are vague \u8211\'20\'13 stoking fears of criminal consequences and leaving women desperate\par \par \uc2 For a long time after her miscarriage, Rebecca sat on the toilet, trying to figure out what to do.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI thought about fishing it out of the bloody water,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CI didn\u8217\'20\'19t really want to hold it in my hand. I also didn\u8217\'20\'19t know what I would do with it afterwards. Am I gonna put it in the trash? Am I gonna dig a hole in the backyard? What the hell am I supposed to do? I had no idea.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It was around 3 or 4 in the morning; her husband was trying to get some sleep before work. She was exhausted and in shock.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CUltimately, I flushed it. I didn\u8217\'20\'19t want to,\u8221\'20\'1D Rebecca said. As she thought about it all again, she kept repeating herself: \u8220\'20\'1CI didn\u8217\'20\'19t know what I was supposed to do.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Rebecca, who is from Ohio, experienced that miscarriage in fall of 2014. Nine years and one supreme court decision later, another distraught woman would also miscarry into a toilet in Ohio \u8211\'20\'13 but after she tried to flush, she would have a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/dec/26/ohio-jury-woman-miscarriage-abuse-corpse"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 very different experience}}}\uc2 . Her case would ultimately land a national spotlight on the anguish, uncertainty and even danger that millions of women, like Rebecca, have experienced in the minutes and hours after a miscarriage.\par \par \uc2 In September 2023, a 33-year-old woman named Brittany Watts went to the hospital, where doctors found that her water had broken roughly 22 weeks into her pregnancy, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/nation/2023/12/15/ohio-woman-miscarriage-abuse-of-corpse-grand-jury/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to the Washington Post}}}\uc2 . It was too early for Watts to deliver a healthy baby. Hospital providers debated whether they could help her; at the time, Ohio had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban on the books. Watts reportedly left the hospital multiple times, at least once against medical advice. Within days, she miscarried into her home toilet.\par \par \uc2 Police ultimately charged Watts with the fifth-degree felony of \u8220\'20\'1Cabuse of a corpse\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 a charge that rang alarm bells among reproductive rights groups, since it suggested that a fetus can be legally defined as a human body. On 11 January, months after an initial hearing and after news outlets across the country had covered Watts\u8217\'20\'19 story, a Trumbull county {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/jan/11/ohio-woman-miscarriage-not-indicted-abuse-corpse-brittany-watts"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 grand jury declined}}}\uc2  to indict Watts, ending the harrowing possibility of a trial and one-year prison stint.\par \par \uc2 But as the 2022 overturning of Roe v Wade continues to roil laws around reproductive health and the legal status of fetuses, someone else may soon face criminal consequences over their handling of a miscarriage. The law can be startlingly vague on the topic of miscarriages and their aftermath, while the persistent miasma of misinformation about pregnancy loss compounds people\u8217\'20\'19s confusion and terror. What should Watts have done after her miscarriage? What should any woman do, at a time when any pregnancy can be twisted into a crime scene?\par \par \uc2 A murky legal landscape\par \par \uc2 For years after Rebecca\u8217\'20\'19s miscarriage, she tried to simply move on, but the news stories about Watts have made that impossible. She keeps thinking \u8220\'20\'1Cabout how Republicans would want me to be in prison for the miscarriages\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Rebecca, 44, asked to go only by her first name, because she\u8217\'20\'19s afraid of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists and authorities coming after her. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s just not worth endangering my family,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CThat just doesn\u8217\'20\'19t feel worth it any more \u8211\'20\'13 which is completely insane. We\u8217\'20\'19re supposed to live in a free country.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She is far from the only person to feel that way. After the Guardian published a story about Watts\u8217\'20\'19 case, more than 100 people reached out to share their stories of miscarriages and their fears for the future.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWithout knowing it, these people are criminalizing their mothers, their sisters, their aunts, their friends,\u8221\'20\'1D said Mary Morgan, an Alabama woman who had five miscarriages within six years. \u8220\'20\'1CThey have no idea how many women have had miscarriages \u8211\'20\'13 and I\u8217\'20\'19m just gonna say it, have had them on the toilet, because I\u8217\'20\'19m telling you, that\u8217\'20\'19s a thing.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 About {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.marchofdimes.org/find-support/topics/miscarriage-loss-grief/miscarriage"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 10% to 20%}}}\uc2  of known pregnancies end in miscarriage, according to March of Dimes, a leading non-profit that focuses on maternal and infant health. Most of those miscarriages occur before 12 weeks of pregnancy. After 20 weeks, a pregnancy loss is known as a stillbirth (although fetuses are typically deemed viable \u8211\'20\'13 that is, able to survive outside the womb \u8211\'20\'13 at about 24 weeks).\par \par \uc2 Under normal circumstances, Dr Alexandria Wells might recommend that patients who are miscarrying earlier in pregnancy do as Rebecca did and flush. (It is, as Morgan said, common for people to miscarry into toilets.) If they want genetic testing to diagnose what went wrong, Wells might also recommend that they bring the miscarriage remains to her.\par \par \uc2 But many people in the United States are no longer living under normal circumstances.\par \par \uc2 In the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2023/nov/10/state-abortion-laws-us"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 14 states with near-total \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  bans}}}\uc2 , there\u8217\'20\'19s no good way to deal with the remains of pregnancy, according to Wells, an OB-GYN in Seattle, Washington and a fellow with Physicians for Reproductive Health. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a question that is particularly difficult for providers to answer, particularly when you get into higher gestational ages where you have recognizable fetal parts.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Many miscarrying patients, Wells said, are probably no longer showing up at hospitals at all. Instead, they are potentially making decisions about fetal remains at home, alone.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHonestly, we don\u8217\'20\'19t know what\u8217\'20\'19s safe\u8221\'20\'1D legally, she said. \u8220\'20\'1CI can\u8217\'20\'19t necessarily tell someone in a restrictive state that it\u8217\'20\'19s safe to bring their remains to a hospital or it\u8217\'20\'19s best to bring them to a mortuary service. I genuinely don\u8217\'20\'19t know. We\u8217\'20\'19re in this kind of uncharted territory.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 State law, rather than federal, dictates definitions of \u8220\'20\'1Chuman remains\u8221\'20\'1D and how to dispose of them \u8211\'20\'13 but it\u8217\'20\'19s not very clear, said Tanya Marsh, a professor at the Wake Forest University School of Law who teaches the only course in a US law school on funeral and cemetery law.\par \par \uc2 Historically, the law recognizes a \u8220\'20\'1Cbody\u8221\'20\'1D when the body possesses both a birth and death certificate, according to Marsh. But before the overturning of Roe, several anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  state legislatures moved to implement requirements around the handling of fetal remains \u8211\'20\'13 which can also be known as products of conception \u8211\'20\'13 including in cases of miscarriages, stillbirths or \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While those remains might have been previously classified as medical waste, medical facilities such as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics were suddenly legally tasked with treating them like human bodies and thus cremating or burying them. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates attacked these laws, passed in states including Indiana and Texas, as veiled attempts to impose onerous, expensive restrictions on clinics.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re not all on the same page about the answers to two of the most fundamental questions about the human condition: when do you exist and when do you no longer exist?\u8221\'20\'1D Marsh said. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd so then of course we\u8217\'20\'19re not going to be on the same page about all the questions about: \u8216\'20\'18OK, now what do you do with that which remains?\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The restrictions on handling fetal remains apply only to medical providers and facilities, not individuals who may be getting an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or experiencing a miscarriage. (Similarly, US \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans typically impose criminal penalties on providers, not patients.) But while pregnant people may not be technically subject to those rules, that doesn\u8217\'20\'19t mean they face zero legal risk when having miscarriages \u8211\'20\'13especially in the post-Roe United States.\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Who do we trust?\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Ling serves as senior counsel for the helpline run by the reproductive justice organization If/When/How, which also provided guidance to Watts\u8217\'20\'19 legal team. American callers to the helpline can get free, confidential legal advice about their reproductive experiences, including miscarriages.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople definitely do contact us with concerns about the fact that they may have experienced a miscarriage, or they think that they have experienced a miscarriage, and being worried about that information \u8211\'20\'13 if given to the wrong person \u8211\'20\'13 might be used against them,\u8221\'20\'1D Ling said.\par \par \uc2 Between 2000 and 2020, at least 61 people were criminally investigated or arrested for allegedly ending their own pregnancies or helping someone else do so, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/oct/30/self-managed-abortions-arrest-investiagtion-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 If/When/How found in a report released last year}}}\uc2 . Most of the adults whose cases proceeded through the court system were living in poverty.\par \par \uc2 Many of those cases were charged under statutes that were not originally meant to address the end of a pregnancy, such as child abuse, felony assault or assault of an unborn child, even homicide and murder. The \u8220\'20\'1Cabuse of corpse\u8221\'20\'1D charge that Watts faced hinged on the question of whether someone had treated a corpse in a way that would \u8220\'20\'1Coutrage reasonable community sensibilities\u8221\'20\'1D. Part of the problem, Marsh said, is that it\u8217\'20\'19s no longer always clear what constitutes a \u8220\'20\'1Ccorpse\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 For anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists, that may be something of a feature, rather than a bug: their opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rests on the belief that a fetus is a person, and legally redefining fetuses as people \u8211\'20\'13 complete with the ability to leave behind a corpse \u8211\'20\'13 is a long-term goal of many in the movement.\par \par \uc2 Now that more than a dozen states ban almost all abortions, doctors who treat miscarriages can also get caught in a legal bind. From a medical provider\u8217\'20\'19s perspective, a miscarriage and an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  can look deeply similar; in medical lingo, miscarriages are even known as \u8220\'20\'1Cspontaneous abortions\u8221\'20\'1D. Under \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, providers have at times been reluctant to offer \u8220\'20\'1Cmiscarriage management\u8221\'20\'1D, out of a fear that authorities may think they\u8217\'20\'19re performing illegal abortions. Even if a patient\u8217\'20\'19s pregnancy is over, they might be unable to get the miscarriage care they need.\par \par \uc2 All \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans permit abortions in medical emergencies \u8211\'20\'13 but doctors have widely said that the exceptions are too vague to be workable.\par \par \uc2 When Watts\u8217\'20\'19 water broke early, she technically experienced what is known as \u8220\'20\'1Cpreterm premature rupture of the membranes\u8221\'20\'1D, or PPROM. In cases of PPROM, someone\u8217\'20\'19s pregnancy may be effectively over, because she\u8217\'20\'19ll be unable to carry the pregnancy to term \u8211\'20\'13 but fetal cardiac activity may continue. In the months since Roe fell, many women who have had PPROM have said that they were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jun/22/post-roe-abortion-women-of-color"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 denied procedures}}}\uc2  to treat it.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPPROM happens routinely, at every hospital,\u8221\'20\'1D Dr Ghazaleh Moayedi, an OB-GYN in Texas, told the Guardian in an interview last year. \u8220\'20\'1CIt has been one of the scenarios that has really highlighted the inaccuracy of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law, and really the fallacy in trying to define a medical procedure as one thing or trying to describe pregnancy in one way.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Watts was reportedly turned into police by a nurse. Of roughly 1,400 cases where people were criminalized for their pregnancies between 2006 and 2022, one in three cases were instigated by a medical professional, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pregnancyjusticeus.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/9-2023-Criminalization-report.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  an analysis of the cases}}}\uc2  by the reproductive justice group Pregnancy Justice.\par \par \uc2 Erica Freeman, a full-spectrum doula, says she\u8217\'20\'19s hearing from people who worry they will not be able to get care if they miscarry. \u8220\'20\'1CIt definitely puts people in a hard place,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CWho do we trust in these circumstances, that they\u8217\'20\'19re gonna believe me, they\u8217\'20\'19re gonna listen to me, that they\u8217\'20\'19re gonna actually hear what\u8217\'20\'19s coming out of my mouth and really diagnose me properly?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Calls for open conversation\par \par \uc2 Everybody who talked to the Guardian about their miscarriages agreed: people don\u8217\'20\'19t talk about miscarriages enough. Left in the dark about how common it is, people often feel like failures over their inability to stay pregnant.\par \par \uc2 Freeman recommended that people look for providers with whom they can have open conversations, both before and after pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have to normalize those hard conversations and not for everyone to continue to think that pregnancy is just unicorns and rainbows,\u8221\'20\'1D said Freeman, who is also CEO of Sisters in Loss, a company that helps Black women speak out about pregnancy, infant loss and infertility. \u8220\'20\'1CBecause it\u8217\'20\'19s not, especially not in the US.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 When Roberta Nordheim-Wallace got unexpectedly pregnant around 1995, people seemed resentful that her pregnancy made her so nauseous. \u8220\'20\'1CIt was all about stiffening my spine, and being grateful that I was going to be bringing new life into the world,\u8221\'20\'1D Nordheim-Wallace said.\par \par \uc2 That pregnancy ended in a miscarriage. She spent several hours in pain, then started to bleed heavily. Finally, she decided to go to the hospital, where she said she waited for hours. No one spoke directly to her, Nordheim-Wallace recalled.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt was a Twilight Zone movie. The overarching feeling I had from the entire experience was that the entire thing made people extremely uncomfortable,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 The way she was treated haunted her for years afterward. \u8220\'20\'1CIt was an experience that frightened me so badly that when I reached the point of needing hip replacement, I was very nearly hysterical. I was at one point on the floor of my GP\u8217\'20\'19s office, curled in a ball, because I could not accept the idea of going into a hospital again.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In her view, Republicans are now telling women: \u8220\'20\'1COK, you are no longer a thinking human being, you are now an incubator. And if you fail in your role as an incubator, you will be punished.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 In the US, you can call or text the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://mahotline.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 M+A Hotline}}}\uc2  at 1-833-246-2632 from 8am to 1am ET for guidance on miscarriage\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240124ek1o002pa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
