\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 US single people under 50 having less sex since Roe overturned, study finds\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 882 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 January 2024\par 14:47 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Annual study from \uc2 Match Group\uc2  of 5,000 single people finds shift in behavior since supreme court ruling on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 More than one in 10 single people under 50 say they are having less sex because the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade, according to a new study.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, \uc2 Match Group\uc2 , which operates Tinder, Hinge and a slew of other matchmaking platforms, released the latest findings from its long-running Singles in America survey, a snapshot of more than 5,000 single Americans\u8217\'20\'19 thoughts and experiences around dating and sex. For the second year in a row, Match has found that the demolition of the national right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is affecting how Americans say they date and have sex with one another.\par \par \uc2 Since Roe fell, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2023/nov/10/state-abortion-laws-us"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than 20 states}}}\uc2  have significantly restricted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. Thirteen per cent of singles under 50 said that they are now more afraid of getting pregnant or getting someone else pregnant, including 21% of gen Z singles between the ages of 18 and 26. Twelve per cent of singles under 50 also said that Roe\u8217\'20\'19s demise has made them more hesitant to date, while 11% said they have casual sex less frequently and the same number reported having less sex overall. Seven per cent said that they are more likely to have sex in ways that lower the risk of pregnancy, such as non-penetrative sex.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt has become a litmus test for people that it\u8217\'20\'19s too much trouble to date,\u8221\'20\'1D one survey respondent said of Roe. Another added: \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m bisexual, so it makes me think I should strictly date women.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 One in 10 say that, when they do have sex, they are more nervous or anxious.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNot only is it impacting behavior, it\u8217\'20\'19s also impacting the meaning and the quality of the experience that people are having. It\u8217\'20\'19s really remarkable that legislation is making people feel more nervous or worried or less comfortable with their sexual lives,\u8221\'20\'1D said Justin Garcia, the executive director of the Kinsey Institute and a scientific adviser to \uc2 Match Group\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This kind of anxiety, Garcia said, threatens people\u8217\'20\'19s ability to form partnerships: \u8220\'20\'1CYou\u8217\'20\'19re not gonna connect that well if you\u8217\'20\'19re feeling anxious the whole time.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 These statistics provide a rare peek into the link between \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and people\u8217\'20\'19s sex lives, as there is relatively little research into the connection. Although the Singles in America survey is not longitudinal \u8211\'20\'13 new people are surveyed with every iteration \u8211\'20\'13 worries about the end of federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections seem to be on the rise: last year, 78% of survey singles said that Roe\u8217\'20\'19s fall had affected their sex and dating lives. This year , the number jumped to 87%.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are often vocal about their support for particular kinds of sex \u8211\'20\'13 specifically, the heterosexual, married and procreative kind \u8211\'20\'13 and have pushed policies to discourage sex among single people, including slashing access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particular types of birth control, and comprehensive sex ed in schools. The Match survey suggests their efforts might be succeeding.\par \par \uc2 At the National Pro-Life Summit, held over the weekend, \uc2 Kevin Roberts\uc2 , the head of the leading conservative thinktank \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2 , urged attendees to build \u8220\'20\'1Ca culture of life\u8221\'20\'1D through \u8220\'20\'1Cthe heroic spiritual adventure of falling in love, getting married and, yes, having lots of kids\u8221\'20\'1D. The room erupted in applause.\par \par \uc2 But Roe\u8217\'20\'19s demise has contributed to more use of birth control, the Singles in America survey found. Thirteen per cent of singles under 50 said that they now use condoms more often. Meanwhile, 7% of women said that they got an IUD and 5% of men said they got a vasectomy in response to the ruling.\par \par \uc2 The survey arrives at a time when \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is increasingly on the ballot. The vice-president, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , this week kicked off a tour to spotlight how a Republican administration may further devastate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, as Democrats believe that outrage over Roe may win them votes in the 2024 elections.\par \par \uc2 Seventy per cent of singles said that a candidate\u8217\'20\'19s views on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have some impact on their vote. Nearly a quarter said that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation will completely determine their vote, including 30% of Democrats and 20% of Republicans and libertarians. Women were more likely to say that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would determine their vote: 26% of women said so, compared to 18% of men.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m furious and will only vote for Democrats for any elected office,\u8221\'20\'1D one respondent said.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, many singles may not vote at all. Fourteen per cent said they do not plan to vote in the upcoming election, including 20% of gen Z singles, according to the survey.\par \par \uc2 There are more than 100 million single people in the United States. They\u8217\'20\'19re an enormous and overlooked demographic, Garcia said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CRelationships status can impact people\u8217\'20\'19s views and their commitments to certain issues,\u8221\'20\'1D Garcia said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a group that someone can scoop up. Those aren\u8217\'20\'19t negligible numbers in a tight election. That\u8217\'20\'19s a very large group of singles that don\u8217\'20\'19t feel engaged. What an opportunity for politicians to think about relationship status and singlehood and to engage those people.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240124ek1o0028l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
