\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Many Republicans support  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2 . Are they switching parties because of it?\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 1,858 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 January 2024\par 11:03 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 GOP leadership has floundered on the issue, and members have conflicting answers on party loyalty\par \par \uc2 The first time Carol Whitmore ever had sex, she got pregnant.\par \par \uc2 It was 1973, and Whitmore was a teenager. Whitmore\u8217\'20\'19s parents were in and out of trouble with the police, Whitmore said. When they told Whitmore they would help her raise the child, she thought, nope.\par \par \uc2 Instead, Whitmore got an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That same year, the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide in Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI made that choice myself, and to this day, I don\u8217\'20\'19t regret it,\u8221\'20\'1D Whitmore told the Guardian. A half-century later, Whitmore is still staunchly supportive of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. She\u8217\'20\'19s recently taken to collecting petitions in support of a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/jan/05/florida-proposal-abortion-rights-constitution-ballot"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Florida ballot measure}}}\uc2  to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 Whitmore is also a deeply committed Republican, who has held multiple positions in Florida local government. For her, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are part and parcel of her Republican worldview.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDo you want more government overreach to tell us how to take care of ourselves?\u8221\'20\'1D Whitmore said. She\u8217\'20\'19s not the only conservative who feels this way, she said: \u8220\'20\'1CEverybody I talked to says: \u8216\'20\'18Well, we aren\u8217\'20\'19t coming out publicly, but we are definitely going to sign that petition, we are definitely going to vote for that amendment to be passed.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In the year and a half since the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe in June 2022, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/dec/29/republicans-democrats-abortion-plans-election-2024-trump-biden"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Republicans have floundered}}}\uc2  over how to handle \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The issue is widely thought to have cost them the promised \u8220\'20\'1Cred wave\u8221\'20\'1D in the 2022 midterms, as well as control of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/nov/07/virginia-election-voting-results-house-delegates-senate"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Virginia state legislature}}}\uc2  in 2023. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters have triumphed on every \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related ballot measure since Roe\u8217\'20\'19s demise, including in states that are traditionally believed to be conservative strongholds like Kansas, Kentucky and, most recently, Ohio.\par \par \uc2 Experts still have questions about the driving forces behind these victories. Was it a surge in Democratic turnout? Or Republicans breaking with their party platform on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? And will \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  convince Republicans to leave the GOP behind entirely?\par \par \uc2 The outcome of the 2024 elections, when roughly a dozen states may vote on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  referendums, could hinge on the answers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt may be in the narrowest sense possible to win with only Democrats, but that\u8217\'20\'19s not even on our radar,\u8221\'20\'1D Jodi Liggett, the senior adviser for \uc2 Reproductive Freedom for All\uc2  Arizona, said late last year. Liggett\u8217\'20\'19s organization is championing a proposal for a 2024 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related ballot measure in Arizona. \u8220\'20\'1CI think if anything, you\u8217\'20\'19re for sure gonna need independents,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd we think people actually agree across parties, on the pure issue of who should be deciding: physicians, medical professionals and families, not politicians.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Democrats, who once avoided the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in election campaigns, are now banking on the issue to amplify anger and turnout overall. Yet in interviews with eight Republican or formerly Republican women who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, hailing from six states across the country, a complex portrait emerged, suggesting Republicans might not be the silver bullet that Democrats are hoping for in November.\par \par \uc2 When the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  first legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide in Roe v Wade, neither party was unified in its position on the issue. Over the next five decades, Republicans grew increasingly opposed to the procedure. Although \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are broadly popular in the United States today, support is sharply split by party: while 80% of Democrats say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or most cases, just 38% of Republicans say the same, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2022/06/13/about-six-in-ten-americans-say-abortion-should-be-legal-in-all-or-most-cases-2/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 polling from the Pew Research Center}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Still, that is a significant fraction of Republicans. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2022/06/17/a-closer-look-at-republicans-who-favor-legal-abortion-and-democrats-who-oppose-it/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Men and women make up equal shares}}}\uc2  of these \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights-supporting Republicans \u8211\'20\'13 but women consistently vote at higher rates than men, making them a critical voting bloc for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights supporters to win.\par \par \uc2 For Stephanie Tyler, a longtime Republican in Nevada, Roe\u8217\'20\'19s overturning \u8220\'20\'1Cwas the proverbial last straw\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI believe so fundamentally in a woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to choose,\u8221\'20\'1D Tyler said, adding: \u8220\'20\'1CAny party that would make that a key piece of its platform, and then support a supreme court that obviously pushed that as a primary agenda, is not my party.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Weeks after Roe fell, Tyler dropped her affiliation with the Republican party and registered as an independent.\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18There will be no Republicans left\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Some prominent Republican women have started to urge the GOP to stop focusing on banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  so much \u8211\'20\'13 including Ann Coulter, the conservative firebrand not generally known for moderation. \u8220\'20\'1CThe demand for anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation just cost Republicans another crucial race,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://jezebel.com/ann-coulter-makes-one-1-good-point-about-abortion-1850305384"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Coulter tweeted in April}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CPro-lifers: WE WON. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not a \u8216\'20\'18constitutional right\u8217\'20\'19 anymore! Please stop pushing strict limits on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or there will be no Republicans left.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 This week, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  similarly warned that Republicans have been \u8220\'20\'1Cdecimated\u8221\'20\'1D over extreme \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  stances, even as he took credit for what he called the \u8220\'20\'1Cmiracle\u8221\'20\'1D of overturning Roe.\par \par \uc2 Many of the interviewed women shared Coulter\u8217\'20\'19s concern about what the GOP\u8217\'20\'19s hardline stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would mean for the party\u8217\'20\'19s future.\par \par \uc2 Sandy Senn, a Republican state senator from South Carolina, , believes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be outlawed after the first trimester of pregnancy (with exceptions). But last year, she banded together with the four other female senators in South Carolina\u8217\'20\'19s state legislature \u8211\'20\'13 two Republicans, a Democrat and an independent \u8211\'20\'13 to stop a total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban from passing in South Carolina. They called themselves the \u8220\'20\'1Csister senators\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 At one point, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/may/23/south-carolina-abortion-ban-six-weeks"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sister senators}}}\uc2  filibustered for three days. Their efforts worked, for a while. By the end of the year, though, South Carolina had banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  past six weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou cannot have laws that are going to thumb a group of people down, because they\u8217\'20\'19re ultimately not going to listen, and they\u8217\'20\'19re going to find a workaround,\u8221\'20\'1D Senn said. \u8220\'20\'1CIf we continue down this path \u8211\'20\'13 we hate gays, we hate women, we hate transsexuals \u8211\'20\'13 if we become the party of hate, we lose independents. We\u8217\'20\'19re gonna lose young voters.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She continued: \u8220\'20\'1CYou\u8217\'20\'19re gonna see moderate Republicans walk away from the party on certain issues, or maybe not even vote top-ballot or in some elections, just because they feel like they can\u8217\'20\'19t.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Gauging how many Republicans will break away from the party to support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, though, is complicated by much of the GOP\u8217\'20\'19s recent tack to the far right. Kelly Dittmar, the director of research and a scholar at the center for American women and politics at \uc2 Rutgers University\uc2 \u8211\'20\'13Camden, said that it\u8217\'20\'19s become increasingly difficult to even locate moderate Republican women, either as voters or as elected officials. So many have simply left the party.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWomen in general have been more likely to be liberal, and that includes among Republicans,\u8221\'20\'1D Dittmar said. \u8220\'20\'1CIn surveys, it\u8217\'20\'19s hard to compare Republicans over time, because you\u8217\'20\'19re actually talking about a really different group of people. Instead of seeing Republican women say, \u8216\'20\'18Oh, we don\u8217\'20\'19t agree with the party\u8217\'20\'19, they just don\u8217\'20\'19t even come up in the count, because they don\u8217\'20\'19t identify at the beginning of a survey as Republican.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Mirabel Batjer is one of those women. She left the GOP around 2010, after supporting \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  for president in 2008.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI just decided that it was silly for me to pretend any longer that it was a party for me, because it certainly was not,\u8221\'20\'1D said Batjer, who now calls herself a \u8220\'20\'1Crabid liberal Democrat\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CI was almost a single-issue voter when it came to being concerned about Roe v Wade and what the supreme court could do. And I guess my fears were right.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Some Republicans, such as Kellyanne Conway, a former Trump adviser, have suggested the GOP should pivot to emphasizing contraception or risk losing in 2024. At a GOP presidential debate, candidate Nikki Haley, who has urged \u8220\'20\'1Cconsensus\u8221\'20\'1D on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , asked the audience: \u8220\'20\'1CCan\u8217\'20\'19t we all agree contraception should be available?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But that plan might not get far in the modern Republican party. Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists, who have long been wedded to the GOP and have helped propel it to electoral victories across the country, have mixed views on hormonal birth control. The powerful Students for Life of America, for example, has labeled oral contraceptives and IUDs \u8220\'20\'1Cabortifacients\u8221\'20\'1D, meaning that they cause abortions, which is false.\par \par \uc2 This year, Senn was enraged by the introduction of a South Carolina bill to require that parents give consent before doctors can fulfill minors\u8217\'20\'19 requests for medication, including birth control \u8211\'20\'13 in apparent defiance of a federal program, Title X, that allows minors to receive confidential family-planning help.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHere they are, making an attempt to basically keep women barefoot and pregnant, because now they don\u8217\'20\'19t even want them to have birth control,\u8221\'20\'1D Senn said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe bill is asinine, and women aren\u8217\'20\'19t going to put up with that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But multiple Republican women who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights said they prefer to stick with the GOP come November, even if they vote for access to the procedure in states that are holding referendums. Even those who were undecided on or opposed to Trump said that they couldn\u8217\'20\'19t bring themselves to vote for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Yuripzy Morgan, a Republican who ran to represent Maryland\u8217\'20\'19s third congressional district in 2022, supports some access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But, she said, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not a \u8220\'20\'1Cprimary issue of mine\u8221\'20\'1D when she enters the voting booth.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen I say it is a complicated topic, I don\u8217\'20\'19t say that because it\u8217\'20\'19s the speaking point. I\u8217\'20\'19ve felt the baby kick in my tummy. I\u8217\'20\'19ve given birth. I know what that feels like,\u8221\'20\'1D Morgan said. \u8220\'20\'1COne of my biggest pet peeves is when people make this a black-and-white issue.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Melissa Deckman, the CEO of the Public Religion Research Institute, doesn\u8217\'20\'19t believe women are ditching the Republican party in significant numbers because of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . According to her organization\u8217\'20\'19s research, Republican women remain much more likely to think that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be illegal in all cases.\par \par \uc2 Instead, it\u8217\'20\'19s Democrats who now feel more strongly than ever about supporting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. Before Roe fell, most Democrats did not consider \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  their top issue; post-Roe, it\u8217\'20\'19s become a litmus test for half of Democrats, according to research from the Public Religion Research Institute. If anything, Deckman says, Democrats are likely to benefit from boosted turnout among independents or relatively weak Democrats .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CGenerally speaking, most Republicans are opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That\u8217\'20\'19s become the party line and party voters really feel that way,\u8221\'20\'1D Deckman said. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t think there\u8217\'20\'19s any indication that there\u8217\'20\'19s mass exodus from the GOP because of the decision that happened.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Even Whitmore, the Florida woman who had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1973, said that access to the procedure will not be a factor in her vote for president.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe president or whoever is not going to decide this issue,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s going to be the citizens of Florida.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240113ek1d00107\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
