\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Law protecting women seeking emergency abortions is target in \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  case\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 1,258 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 January 2024\par 21:26 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Emergency Medical Treatment and Labor Act is at the heart of the court\u8217\'20\'19s latest blockbuster \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case, which comes out of Idaho\par \par \uc2 Mylissa Farmer\u8217\'20\'19s pregnancy was doomed. But no one would help her end it.\par \par \uc2 Over the course of a few days in August 2022, Farmer visited two hospitals in Missouri and Kansas, where doctors agreed that because the 41-year-old\u8217\'20\'19s water had broken just 18 weeks into her pregnancy, there was no chance that she would give birth to a healthy baby. Continuing the pregnancy could risk Farmer\u8217\'20\'19s health and life \u8211\'20\'13 yet the doctors could not act.\par \par \uc2 Weeks earlier, the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  had overturned Roe v Wade and abolished the national right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It was, legal counsel at one hospital determined, \u8220\'20\'1Ctoo risky in this heated political environment to intervene\u8221\'20\'1D, according to legal filings.\par \par \uc2 In immense pain and anguish, Farmer ultimately traveled several hours to Illinois, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal. There, doctors were able to end her pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Farmer\u8217\'20\'19s account is detailed in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nwlc.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/11/2022.11.08-Mylissa-Farmer-EMTALA-complaint.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 legal complaint}}}\uc2  she filed against the hospitals, arguing that they broke a federal law that requires hospitals to treat patients in medical emergencies. In a first-of-its-kind investigation, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://missouriindependent.com/2023/05/01/hospitals-in-joplin-kck-cited-for-denying-emergency-abortion-to-missouri-woman/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US government sided with Farmer}}}\uc2  and declared that the two hospitals had broken the law.\par \par \uc2 The future of the government\u8217\'20\'19s ability to invoke that law to protect women seeking emergency abortions is now in question. The law, the Emergency Medical Treatment and Labor Act (Emtala), is at the heart of the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s latest blockbuster \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case, which comes out of Idaho.\par \par \uc2 On Friday evening, the supreme court announced it would hear oral arguments in a case involving Emtala. Under the law, which dates back to 1986, hospitals that receive federal Medicare dollars \u8211\'20\'13 the vast majority of hospitals in the US \u8211\'20\'13 must stabilize people in emergencies, regardless of their ability to pay. The Biden administration has argued that the law protects access to emergency abortions, while \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  foes contend that it does not.\par \par \uc2 Emergency abortions, like Farmer\u8217\'20\'19s, have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/dec/16/abortion-ban-lawsuits-pregnancy-complication-emergency-kate-cox"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 taken center stage}}}\uc2  in the battle over post-Roe v Wade \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, as dozens of women across the United States have stepped forward to say that they were denied medically necessary abortions. The Emtala case, which the justices will hear in April, is set to pour gasoline on that fight, as it raises the question of whether the federal government has any role in protecting physicians and patients who feel they\u8217\'20\'19re at war with state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are a lot of doctors who, despite the state\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, still want to go back and they still want to serve the communities that need the care,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dr Alexandria Wells, an OB-GYN in Washington state and a fellow with Physicians for Reproductive Health. \u8220\'20\'1COne of the arguments that they had was Emtala, and the ability to say to your hospital administration that \u8216\'20\'18There is this federal standing that we have to provide this level of care\u8217\'20\'19. And so they were able to provide the care that they knew was necessary to patients.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWithout Emtala, I think a lot of providers are afraid that there\u8217\'20\'19ll be less fear from institutions about saying \u8216\'20\'18no\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D when abortions are needed in emergencies, Wells added.\par \par \uc2 As part of its decision to take the case, the supreme court agreed to let Idaho enforce a law that would ban almost all abortions. Under the state\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, Idaho doctors who perform the procedure may face criminal consequences. In court, they can use an \u8220\'20\'1Caffirmative defense\u8221\'20\'1D to argue that they only performed the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to save a patient\u8217\'20\'19s life.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTo separate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  out from the rest of healthcare that is protected by Emtala is to treat pregnant people differently from everyone else,\u8221\'20\'1D said Alison Tanner, senior counsel at the \uc2 National Women\u8217\'20\'19s Law Center\uc2 , which is representing Farmer.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  can be necessary to save someone\u8217\'20\'19s life. It\u8217\'20\'19s also sometimes necessary to save someone\u8217\'20\'19s health and future fertility,\u8221\'20\'1D Tanner continued.\par \par \uc2 In Oklahoma, another woman, Jaci Statton, has also filed a complaint accusing a hospital of violating Emtala. In her complaint, Statton said that she was diagnosed with a partial molar pregnancy \u8211\'20\'13 a condition that would not result in a healthy baby and put her life at risk. An \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would stabilize Statton, the complaint said, but an Oklahoma hospital failed to offer the procedure to Statton.\par \par \uc2 Instead, providers at the hospital \u8220\'20\'1Ctold Jaci that they could not provide an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until she was actively crashing in front of them or on the verge of a heart attack\u8221\'20\'1D, the complaint said. \u8220\'20\'1CIn the meantime, the best that they could offer was to let Jaci sit in the parking lot so that she would be close to the hospital when her condition further deteriorated.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Researchers from the \uc2 University of California, San Francisco\uc2 , are currently at work on an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ansirh.org/sites/default/files/2023-05/Care%20Post-Roe%20Preliminary%20Findings.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ongoing study}}}\uc2  into how Roe\u8217\'20\'19s downfall has affected healthcare providers\u8217\'20\'19 ability to meet the US standard of care. One doctor in a state with an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban described to researchers a case in which medical providers refused to even get near a patient who went into labor too early to deliver a healthy pregnancy. The situation was so bad, the doctor said, that they were considering leaving the state.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI will never forget this case because I overheard the primary provider say to a nurse that so much as offering a helping hand to a patient getting onto the gurney while in the throes of a miscarriage could be construed as \u8216\'20\'18aiding and abetting an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\u8217\'20\'19 Best not to so much as touch the patient who is miscarrying,\u8221\'20\'1D the doctor said, according to preliminary findings from the study. \u8220\'20\'1CA gross violation of common sense and the oath I took when I got into this profession to soothe my patients\u8217\'20\'19 suffering.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is no way to do healthcare,\u8221\'20\'1D said Carole Joffe, a professor at the \uc2 University of California, San Francisco\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s department of obstetrics, gynecology and reproductive sciences. Joffe is one of the authors of the study\u8217\'20\'19s preliminary findings. \u8220\'20\'1CAll these medical emergencies are making a very powerful case that pregnancy can be very dangerous and of course it has to be treated within the healthcare system.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 When he helped overturned Roe, \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  wrote in a concurring opinion that Roe\u8217\'20\'19s demise \u8220\'20\'1Creturns the court to a position of neutrality\u8221\'20\'1D on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and permits states to make their own decisions about how to handle the procedure.\par \par \uc2 That outcome, however, has not come to pass. Instead, a flurry of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related lawsuits has drawn federal courts, including the supreme court, back into the fray of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars. Last week, the famously conservative US court of appeals for the fifth circuit affirmed a ruling out of Texas that found that the Biden administration had overstepped when, shortly after Roe\u8217\'20\'19s overturning, the administration issued guidance asserting that Emtala covered emergency abortions. (That case is not connected to the Idaho case.)\par \par \uc2 Emtala, Kurt Engelhardt wrote, \u8220\'20\'1Cdoes not mandate any specific type of medical treatment, let alone \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In December 2023, the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  also agreed to take up another \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case, which involved the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/aug/17/what-abortion-pill-supreme-court-case-access-mifepristone"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 availability of \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  pills}}}\uc2 . The justices will likely rule in both cases by summer \u8211\'20\'13 just months ahead of the 2024 presidential election, in which \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is set to be a major issue.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240109ek19002f2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
