\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 More Americans are stockpiling  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  pills without pregnancy \u8211\'20\'13 study\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 968 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 January 2024\par 18:45 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 From September 2021 to April 2023, Aid Access tracked roughly 48,400 advance provision requests for mifepristone\par \par \uc2 More Americans are now stockpiling \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills in case they get pregnant, according to new research published Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Before Roe v Wade was overturned in June 2022, Aid Access, an organization that mails \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to people across the US, received an average of 25 requests a day from people seeking the pills despite not being pregnant. After the leak of the supreme court decision to overturn Roe, that average shot up to 247 requests each day, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jamainternalmedicine/article-abstract/2813618?utm_campaign=articlePDF&utm_medium=articlePDFlink&utm_source=articlePDF&utm_content=jamainternmed.2023.7291"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the research published on Tuesday}}}\uc2  found.\par \par \uc2 That number fell after the actual decision, but rose again to 172 a day in April 2023, as US courts signaled a willingness to restrict the availability of a major \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill.\par \par \uc2 People have been turning to Aid Access for \u8220\'20\'1Cadvance provision\u8221\'20\'1D pills since September 2021, after Texas enacted a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban but long before the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe and abolished the national right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Now, with wide swathes of the US south and midwest under \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, an online market to request and obtain \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills is thriving.\par \par \uc2 The study tracks requests between the beginning of September 2021 and the end of April 2023. In December 2023, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/dec/13/abortion-pill-us-supreme-court-mifepristone"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the US supreme court announced}}}\uc2  that it would hear arguments in a case regarding {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/aug/17/what-abortion-pill-supreme-court-case-access-mifepristone"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the future of mifepristone,}}}\uc2  a major \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill. That case is expected to be decided by this summer.\par \par \uc2 In total, over the study\u8217\'20\'19s time frame, Aid Access tracked roughly 48,400 advance provision requests. It received more requests for advance provision pills from states that were anticipated to enact bans \u8211\'20\'13 even more than the requests from states that did enact bans.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt seems to suggest that what people are reacting to is the threat of reduced access, the threat of curtailment of reproductive rights,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dr Abigail Aiken, an associate professor at the \uc2 University of Texas at Austin\uc2  and a co-author of the study. \u8220\'20\'1CWhen you think about what advanced provision is, that makes sense, right? Advanced provision is getting out ahead of things. Advanced provision is advanced planning. Advanced provision is a way to protect a potential need you might have in the future if you think access to the service that would fulfill that need is going away.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Over the study period, Aid Access also received more than 147,00 requests from people seeking to end their existing pregnancies. Medical experts widely agree that it is safe to \u8220\'20\'1Cself-manage\u8221\'20\'1D your own \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  , or perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  outside of the formal US healthcare system, using pills within the first trimester of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Compared with the people who wanted to terminate their existing pregnancies, people who sought advance provision pills were more likely to be white, child-free and living in urban areas. Choosing from a list of reasons, they most frequently told Aid Access that they wanted the pills to \u8220\'20\'1Censure personal health and choice\u8221\'20\'1D and to \u8220\'20\'1Cprepare for possible \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Aid Access was launched in 2018 by Dr Rebecca Gomperts, a Dutch physician and one of the most visible \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in the world. Gomperts, who co-authored the study published Tuesday, previously founded Women on Web, an organization that, like Aid Access, shipped \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills. However, Women on Web didn\u8217\'20\'19t provide pills to the United States . Ultimately, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theatlantic.com/health/archive/2018/10/women-on-web-safe-abortion-mail/573322/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Gomperts decided}}}\uc2  that the state of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the country was too dire to ignore.\par \par \uc2 Advance provision pills cost $150 and should arrive within a few days of ordering, according to Aid Access\u8217\'20\'19s website. During the time frame of the study, most of the pills were being shipped by overseas pharmacies, Aiken said.\par \par \uc2 Now, to send \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, US-based physicians associated with Aid Access have begun to rely on what are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/jul/23/shield-laws-provide-abortion-care-aid-access"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 known as \u8220\'20\'1Cshield laws\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  : protections in Democratic states for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers who prescribe pills for patients in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -hostile states. This transition to focusing on using US providers was part of the reason for the study\u8217\'20\'19s conclusion in April, Aiken said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt made sense to look at a time period where the service was entirely outside of the formal US healthcare setting,\u8221\'20\'1D Aiken said. \u8220\'20\'1CNow, I think a lot of people would argue that it\u8217\'20\'19s happening within the formal healthcare setting, because it\u8217\'20\'19s US provider-led and -based.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But while the US providers in blue states may be operating with the formal healthcare system, their patients in red states are not necessarily afforded the system\u8217\'20\'19s protections and guidance. Someone who wants to get a check-up after an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or even just talk to their doctor about their experience, may not feel able to.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn terms of the experience of the person actually using the pills, it may still look a lot more like a self-managed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D Aiken said. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat that means for the nature of the service is an ongoing, interesting question that we\u8217\'20\'19re thinking about now in the research field.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 There was not much data available on what people ended up doing with the advance provision pills, Aiken said, since only a fraction followed up with Aid Access. However, of that fraction, most people still had the pills on standby months later.\par \par \uc2 Last year, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/feb/20/abortion-pills-us-ruling-rebecca-gomperts-interview"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Gomperts}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/feb/20/abortion-pills-us-ruling-rebecca-gomperts-interview"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told the Guardian}}}\uc2  that she wanted people to stock up on pills to protect themselves.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDon\u8217\'20\'19t wait for the decision. Just get the medication now, get it in your house, get it in your hands,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CIf you\u8217\'20\'19re in a war zone and the war is coming, you also make sure you have enough food in your house. This is how it feels. It really is a war. It\u8217\'20\'19s a war on women.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240103ek13002h1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
