\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 The fight for  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rights: what to know going into 2024\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman in New York \par \uc2 1,311 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 December 2023\par 15:21 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 A presidential election and another major supreme court case is on the horizon, after a dramatic year in which pro-choice and foes have waged a state-by-state war\par \par \uc2 More than a year after the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade, the dust from the landmark decision\u8217\'20\'19s collapse has yet to settle.\par \par \uc2 It has been a dramatic year of fallout, with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters and foes now waging a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2023/nov/10/state-abortion-laws-us"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 state-by-state skirmish}}}\uc2  for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. They are sparring in state legislatures, courtrooms, voting booths and hospitals, with each side racking up victories and losses.\par \par \uc2 With a presidential election and another major supreme court case on the horizon, the coming year promises to be at least as eventful. Here\u8217\'20\'19s what you need to know about the fight over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2023 \u8211\'20\'13 and what it means for 2024.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters keep winning at the ballot box\par \par \uc2 In 2022, Republicans underperformed in the midterms and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists won a string of ballot measures to preserve \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, even in conservative states. This year, activists extended their winning streak \u8211\'20\'13 and they hope to replicate their successes in 2024.\par \par \uc2 In November, Ohio became the first reliably red state since Roe fell to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/nov/07/ohio-issue-1-abortion-rights-vote-result"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 vote in favor of proactively enshrining \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  rights}}}\uc2  in the state constitution, while Virginia Democrats successfully fended off Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 attempt to retake the state legislature by campaigning on a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/nov/01/republicans-abortion-ads-virginia-election"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 15-week \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ban}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 For activists and Democrats, these victories were proof that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/nov/08/abortion-winning-issue-rights-victories-2024-election"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  is an election-winning issue}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 and, potentially, an issue that can draw in voters from across both sides of the ideological spectrum. Activists are already at work on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/nov/10/democrats-ballot-measures-protect-abortion-access"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2024 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 -related ballot measures}}}\uc2  in roughly a dozen states, including swing states like Arizona and Nevada.\par \par \uc2 Abortions are on the rise\par \par \uc2 After \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics across the south and midwest were forced to shutter, patients overwhelmed the country\u8217\'20\'19s remaining clinics. In the first year after Roe\u8217\'20\'19s demise, the average number of US abortions performed each month rose rather than fell. Clinics and their advocates are now {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/sep/22/us-abortion-funds-run-out-of-money-demand-surges"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 struggling to keep up}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CWhat actually is happening is a complete disruption,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/oct/24/us-abortion-rates-post-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one expert told the Guardian}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 There is also a gaping hole in the data, which was released in October by the Society of Family Planning: it does not include abortions performed at home, a practice known as \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2023/nov/09/plan-c-documentary-mail-abortion-pill-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 self-managed \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D. Medical experts widely agree that it is safe to self-manage an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  using pills early on in pregnancy, and a number of services shipping \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills have increased in visibility since Roe\u8217\'20\'19s overturning. But while evidence suggests that self-managed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is on the rise, the lack of concrete data about the practice reflects a growing problem in the post-Roe United States: as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves further into the shadows of US life, we will know less about it.\par \par \uc2 Legal battles over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans are ongoing\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans continued to cascade across the country in 2023, with near-total bans taking effect in Indiana, North Dakota and South Carolina. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/may/16/north-carolina-abortion-ban-veto-override#:~:text=North%20Carolina%20bans%20abortion%20at,veto%20%7C%20North%20Carolina%20%7C%20The%20Guardian"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 North Carolina }}}\uc2  and Nebraska, meanwhile, enacted laws to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 12 weeks of pregnancy. In total, 24 states or territories have now banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before viability, or roughly 24 weeks of pregnancy, which would have been illegal under Roe.\par \par \uc2 Litigation over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions is still unfurling in many of these states, and court cases have frozen bans in states like Wyoming and Iowa. Wisconsin \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, meanwhile, found themselves in a unique position this year: after a judge ruled that an 1849 law that had been interpreted to ban abortions instead only banned feticide and did not apply to what she called \u8220\'20\'1Cconsensual abortions\u8221\'20\'1D, providers resumed performing the procedure \u8211\'20\'13 even though the ban is still technically on the books.\par \par \uc2 Lawsuits may force other hardcore anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  states to soften their bans in 2024 to clarify exceptions when abortions are permitted in medical emergencies. While Tennessee and Texas carved out narrow exceptions in their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters have still {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/sep/12/women-denied-abortions-medical-emergencies-challenge-bans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filed lawsuits}}}\uc2  in those two states, as well as in Idaho, that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/dec/16/abortion-ban-lawsuits-pregnancy-complication-emergency-kate-cox"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 challenge the language}}}\uc2 . One Texan mother of two {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/dec/12/kate-cox-texas-abortion-case-explained"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filed a lawsuit}}}\uc2  seeking an emergency \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  while she was still pregnant. (She ultimately {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/dec/11/texas-abortion-kate-cox-lawsuit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fled the state}}}\uc2  for the procedure.)\par \par \uc2 Theoretically, people in medical emergencies should be able to access the procedure even in states with bans \u8211\'20\'13 but doctors say that, in reality, these bans are so vaguely worded that they {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/sep/13/idaho-extreme-abortion-ban-medical-emergency"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 block doctors from helping sick patients}}}\uc2 . This summer, one of these lawsuits led women to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jul/20/texas-women-pregnancy-abortion-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 testify in a Texas court}}}\uc2  about their experiences of being denied abortions. It was the first time since Roe fell, if not the first time since Roe itself was decided, that women did so.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills are in peril\par \par \uc2 The most common method of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, is at the mercy of deeply conservative courts in 2024.\par \par \uc2 In April, a conservative judge in Texas {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/apr/07/abortion-pill-ruling-mifepristone-trump-judge-matthew-kacsmaryk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruled to suspend the FDA\u8217\'20\'19s approval of}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/apr/07/abortion-pill-ruling-mifepristone-trump-judge-matthew-kacsmaryk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  a key \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  pill,}}}\uc2  mifepristone, in response to a lawsuit brought by a coalition of rightwing groups determined to make the pill the next target in their post-Roe campaign against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A federal appeals court soon scaled back that decision, ruling to keep the pill, mifepristone, available but {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/aug/16/restriction-abortion-pill-mifepristone-access-fda-court-decision"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 impose significant restrictions}}}\uc2  on its use. The supreme court then stepped in and decreed that the \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s rules around mifepristone should stay the same while litigation plays out.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration and a manufacturer of mifepristone in September have asked the supreme court to formally hear arguments in the case. In December, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/dec/13/abortion-pill-us-supreme-court-mifepristone"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the justices agreed}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Although the justices indicated that they will only rule on the restrictions imposed by the appeals court, rather than on the overall legality of mifepristone, the case could still have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/aug/17/what-abortion-pill-supreme-court-case-access-mifepristone"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 enormous consequences}}}\uc2 . Rolling back the \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s rules could allow future lawsuits against other politicized medications, like gender-affirming care, HIV drugs or vaccines. Plus, the supreme court will probably rule by summer 2024 \u8211\'20\'13 just months before the presidential election.\par \par \uc2 Mifepristone is used in more than half the abortions in the country. If access to the drug is curtailed, many \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics have said they will pivot to using doses of a different drug, misoprostol, to perform abortions, but misoprostol-only abortions are less effective and associated with more complications.\par \par \uc2 Doctors are fleeing states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans\par \par \uc2 With \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans endangering their patients and threatening to send doctors to prison, doctors are fleeing states where the procedure is banned. After Idaho banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , at least 13 reproductive health physicians left the state and at least two rural labor and delivery wards have closed. Doctors in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/oct/26/us-abortion-ban-providers-doctors-leaving-states"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tennessee}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://19thnews.org/2023/06/abortion-gender-affirming-care-bans-doctors-leaving-texas"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Texas}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abcnews.go.com/US/doctors-face-tough-decision-leave-states-abortion-bans/story?id=100167986"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 North Carolina}}}\uc2 , Ohio and Florida have also told reporters that they are leaving states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans or planning to do so.\par \par \uc2 OB-GYNs are already in short supply in the United States. About half of US counties do not have a practicing OB-GYN, according to the \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2 . The US maternal mortality rates are also worsening, particularly for Black and Native people, at a time when the United States already has the worst maternal mortality rate among industrialized countries.\par \par \uc2 Doctors are now even afraid to get trained in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. Applications to OB-GYN residencies in states with near-total bans fell by more than 10% the year after Roe\u8217\'20\'19s demise, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ama-assn.org/medical-students/preparing-residency/after-dobbs-m4s-face-stark-reality-when-applying-residency"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 data from Association of American Medical Colleges}}}\uc2 . Applications to US OB-GYN residencies overall dropped by about 5% \u8211\'20\'13 indicating that fewer doctors are planning to become OB-GYNs at all.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 This article was amended on 31 December 2023. An earlier version said \u8220\'20\'1CSouth Carolina and Nebraska, meanwhile, enacted laws to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 12 weeks of pregnancy.\u8221\'20\'1D That meant to refer to North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231226ejcq001e3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
