\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Arizona Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 No 1 message: \u8216\'20\'18Republicans want to destroy our democracy\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Gambino \par \uc2 1,936 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 December 2023\par 17:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 State party chair Yolanda Bejarano says Biden\u8217\'20\'19s agenda of job creation and lowering family\u8217\'20\'19s costs is a winner and recent polling does not capture where voters will be come election day\par \par \uc2 Few states will have more influence over the country\u8217\'20\'19s political future than Arizona. Once a ruby-red Republican stronghold, it is now a south-western battleground and the stakes couldn\u8217\'20\'19t be higher for Arizonans \u8211\'20\'13 or Americans.\par \par \uc2 In 2020, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  carried Arizona by just over 10,000 votes, becoming the first Democratic presidential candidate to win the state in nearly a quarter-century. Two years later, Democrats won statewide races for governor, secretary of state and attorney general and re-elected the Democratic senator Mark Kelly to a full six-year term.\par \par \uc2 Now, Arizona is at the center of the battle for the White House and for control of Congress, with a marquee Senate race and a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cookpolitical.com/ratings/house-race-ratings"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pair of closely contested}}}\uc2  House races. At the state level, Democrats are attempting to wrest control of the Arizona legislature for the first time in decades. Republicans presently hold one-seat majorities in both the state senate and the state house.\par \par \uc2 To win in Arizona, Democrats say they must {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/phoenix-rising"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reassemble the coalition}}}\uc2  of moderate Republicans, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/oct/30/arizona-us-election-donald-trump-suburbs-maricopa"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 suburban women}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/oct/28/latino-voters-maricopa-county-phoenix-us-election"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Latinos and young people}}}\uc2  who powered {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/dec/30/democrat-kris-mayes-wins-arizona-attorney-general-recount"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 nail-biting}}}\uc2  victories for the party in recent election cycles. They also must confront widespread economic discontent in a state that saw some of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://cronkitenews.azpbs.org/2023/01/03/phoenix-led-the-nation-in-inflation-in-2022-but-rise-may-slow-in-2023/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 worst levels of inflation}}}\uc2  in the country last year. Frustration over high prices and housing affordability has hurt Biden\u8217\'20\'19s standing in Arizona, where he trails \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the Republican frontrunner, in a number of swing state polls.\par \par \uc2 As Democrats attempt a repeat of their successes in Arizona next year, Yolanda Bejarano, the state party chair, will play a crucial role in turning out the vote in what is expected to be another closely fought election.\par \par \uc2 In an interview, Bejarano, a longtime union organizer and Arizona native, previewed Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 strategy for winning her state. \uc2 The Guardian\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s interview with Bejarano has been edited for clarity and length:\par \par \uc2 Tell us how you got involved in politics.\par \par \uc2 I was a union organizer for 18 years and I got involved in politics after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2012/apr/24/arizona-immigration-law-hispanic-communities"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Arizona passed SB 1070,}}}\uc2  which was the racial profiling bill. I started volunteering for candidates that I believed in and I thought would make a difference in making sure that racial profiling was not something that we were OK with. I became vice-chair of the state party a couple years ago and then our chair [Raquel Ter\u225\'00\'E1n] finished her term and then I decided to run.\par \par \uc2 Recent polling {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://projects.fivethirtyeight.com/polls/arizona/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shows}}}\uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  struggling in Arizona, with voters unhappy over his handling of the economy and immigration. What can Biden do to improve his standing in the state?\par \par \uc2 The best predictor of how voters are feeling is how voters are voting. And we saw in the 2022 and 2023 elections when the Democratic party puts in the time, money and resources behind the president\u8217\'20\'19s agenda, it really is a winning message. President Biden is creating more jobs more quickly than the rest of the world. He\u8217\'20\'19s providing relief in Arizonans\u8217\'20\'19 pockets and it\u8217\'20\'19s our job to continue to do what we do best and to communicate that widely across the state \u8211\'20\'13 to communicate President Biden\u8217\'20\'19s accomplishments from bringing back good union jobs like manufacturing jobs, decreasing inflation, expanding broadband, lowering the cost of internet and lowering the cost of prescription drug prices. Folks care about these issues, these kitchen table issues and we\u8217\'20\'19re going to continue to communicate that across the state.\par \par \uc2 Democrats may find themselves in a three-way race for \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Senate seat. Sinema was elected to the seat {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2018/nov/12/kyrsten-sinema-beats-martha-mcsally-arizona-senator"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in 2018}}}\uc2  as a Democrat but {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/dec/09/kyrsten-sinema-independent-days-after-democrats-secure-senate-majority"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 left the party}}}\uc2  to become an independent after the 2022 midterms. She has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/apr/07/kyrsten-sinema-2024-us-election-independent"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 not decided}}}\uc2  whether to seek re-election. The Democratic congressman \uc2 Ruben Gallego{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jan/23/ruben-gallego-arizona-senate-kyrsten-sinema-democrat"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 launched his campaign}}}\uc2  for the seat last year and Kari Lake, the former local TV anchor who {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/sep/28/arizona-kari-lake-us-senate-run-announcement"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ran unsuccessfully}}}\uc2  for governor in 2022, is likely to be the Republican nominee.\par \par \uc2 In 2022, Democrats portrayed Lake as a threat to American democracy. In a three-way race, could Arizona end up electing a\par \par \uc2 Senator Lake?\par \par \uc2 When we talk about Kari Lake and just everything she stands for \u8211\'20\'13 she calls \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  the \u8220\'20\'1Cultimate sin\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 she\u8217\'20\'19s dangerous for our state, dangerous for Arizonans. Now she\u8217\'20\'19s trying to rebrand herself, trying to appeal to moderate Republicans and it\u8217\'20\'19s just not working. She\u8217\'20\'19s just a failed candidate. She kept saying she was the governor even though she\u8217\'20\'19s running for Senate. Arizonans don\u8217\'20\'19t like her and we\u8217\'20\'19re going to make sure she doesn\u8217\'20\'19t win.\par \par \uc2 What\u8217\'20\'19s your best guess: does Sinema run for re-election?\par \par \uc2 I don\u8217\'20\'19t know if she runs again. I haven\u8217\'20\'19t spoken to her in years. Who knows what she\u8217\'20\'19s going to do. We are laser-focused on making sure that our Democratic nominee wins in November 2024.\par \par \uc2 The state is roughly evenly divided between Democratic, Republican and independent voters. What is Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 message to moderate Republicans, independent and swing voters who they will need to persuade in order to win in 2024?\par \par \uc2 The number one thing is making the persuasive argument that Democrats want to protect our democracy; Republicans want to destroy our democracy. Democrats are helping working families bringing back jobs, Democrats are supporting small businesses, Democrats are bringing back manufacturing jobs, fixing our infrastructure, lowering the price of prescription drug prices that affects everybody, regardless of party affiliation. So I think it\u8217\'20\'19s that we Democrats believe in our democracy, Democrats believe in protecting our institutions and Republicans are trying to tear down everything and privatize things and it\u8217\'20\'19s just not good for our state.\par \par \uc2 Arizona is a border state and immigration is top of mind for voters. How are Arizona Democrats navigating concerns over border security with the concerns of immigrant communities?\par \par \uc2 I grew up near a border town in a place called Roll, Arizona. It\u8217\'20\'19s in Yuma county. My dad was a farm worker. We were pretty poor. We didn\u8217\'20\'19t have healthcare so we would drive across the border to San Luis R\u237\'00\'EDo Colorado and go to the doctor, see the dentist. Americans would go there for the pharmacies or to go eat. Mexicans would come across the border to work. It was like an exchange of commerce and people.\par \par \uc2 That being said, I 100% agree that the border needs to be a safe, secure and welcoming place. Our Arizona Democrats from Governor [Katie] Hobbs to our congressional delegation, they\u8217\'20\'19re multi-focused on this issue. It\u8217\'20\'19s a complicated issue. And Democrats are working towards a solution that prioritizes the economics, the safety, the humanity, treating people humanely, people who are suffering and trying to find a better life.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are trying to find a solution while Republicans are weaponizing the border and they\u8217\'20\'19ll continue to weaponize the border. I truly do not believe that they want a solution because they will use the border to dehumanize people, to scare people.\par \par \uc2 It appears likely that a measure enshrining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights into the state\u8217\'20\'19s constitution will appear on the ballot next year. At the same time, the state\u8217\'20\'19s supreme court is considering the legality of a territory-era ban that could effectively outlaw the procedure in the state. How are Democrats working to leverage the issue in next year\u8217\'20\'19s election?\par \par \uc2 This is a mobilizing issue for us. When \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is on the ballot, like we saw in Kansas, we saw it in Ohio, what happened in Virginia, people do not want the government interfering with their decisions to grow or start a family. People in Arizona and across the country believe that everyone should be free to decide how and when to start and grow a family free from political interference. This is a big issue. It\u8217\'20\'19s going to get on the ballot and we\u8217\'20\'19re going make sure that a woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is enshrined in our constitution.\par \par \uc2 How central is \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 campaign message?\par \par \uc2 In 2022, we elected pro-choice Democrats up and down the ticket: our attorney general, our secretary of state, our governor. They were talking about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and it is what got them across the finish line. So it\u8217\'20\'19s huge.\par \par \uc2 The economy and inflation are top of mind for voters in Arizona and across the country. How are your candidates confronting frustration over the economy and Biden\u8217\'20\'19s handling of it?\par \par \uc2 We are starting to see the economic progress made with Bidenomics. In our wallets we\u8217\'20\'19re seeing decreased energy prices, and there\u8217\'20\'19s a consistent drop in inflation. And it wasn\u8217\'20\'19t just the United States that had an inflation problem. It was something across the entire world.\par \par \uc2 So what we\u8217\'20\'19re doing is letting folks know that the reason why they\u8217\'20\'19re seeing their energy bills decreasing is because of our Democratic policies. The cap on insulin at $35 a month for Medicare recipients, that\u8217\'20\'19s because of Democratic policies. Our infrastructure, our improved roads are thanks to a Democratic policies. So we\u8217\'20\'19re messaging that to Arizonans across the state.\par \par \uc2 In 2020 Arizona became {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/20/rusty-bowers-interview-trump-arizona-republicans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cground zero\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s stolen election lies. Though Arizona voters largely rejected election-denying candidates in 2022, two Republican officials were recently {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/nov/25/cochise-county-arizona-election-denial"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 indicted on charges}}}\uc2  of conspiring to delay the election. How is the party confronting pervasive and ongoing election denialism in the state?\par \par \uc2 Arizona is a testing ground for Republican election conspiracy theories. We saw in 2022 militia men hanging outside of ballot drop boxes with their rifles to try to intimidate people. We saw Maga mobs showing up to our county recorders\u8217\'20\'19 offices when they were counting ballots in a very transparent manner. People can see how they were counting the ballots but we have mobs outside.\par \par \uc2 Our state where Democrats have been working diligently to protect our democracy, making it clear that if you hold up a certification of our elections, you will be indicted. That just happened to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/nov/29/arizona-officials-charged-election-2022"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 two Cochise county board of supervisors}}}\uc2 . Our statewide Democrats are laser-focused on making sure that our everybody\u8217\'20\'19s fundamental right to vote is protected. We are prepared to communicate clearly about what\u8217\'20\'19s at stake in this upcoming election.\par \par \uc2 I think this is something that crosses party lines. People need that assurance, that stability, that when you vote that your elections are fair and they are transparent and that is what is happening. And we have people on the Republican side that are still perpetuating this \u8220\'20\'1Cbig lie\u8221\'20\'1D and the election conspiracy theories that hurt our democracy and hurt our democracy.\par \par \uc2 Several recent polls show {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.noblepredictiveinsights.com/post/update-on-the-2024-presidential-election"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump ahead in Arizona}}}\uc2 . With the caveat that much can change in a year, why do you think Trump is winning over some of the Arizonans who turned against him in 2020?\par \par \uc2 People know that Trump\u8217\'20\'19s judgment is totally compromised. He is not someone who Arizonans will support. Ah. What can I say about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? He is dangerous. Arizonans will want progress over chaos, they want stability, and they don\u8217\'20\'19t get that with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . They get more chaos.\par \par \uc2 So you don\u8217\'20\'19t think the polls capture where Arizona voters will be come\par \par \uc2 election\par \par \uc2 day 2024?\par \par \uc2 I do not think the polling captures where people will be in a year, correct.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231220ejck002jz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
