\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Democrats won Virginia on  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2 . Can it also win them the White House?\b0\par\par\uc2 Joan E Greve in Fredericksburg \par \uc2 1,453 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 December 2023\par 17:04 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 A year and a half after the supreme court struck down Roe v Wade, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to weigh heavily on voters\u8217\'20\'19 mind\par \par \uc2 Days before Josh Cole won his toss-up race, the Democratic candidate for Virginia\u8217\'20\'19s house of delegates predicted that his party would perform well on election day, largely because the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  had motivated many voters to turn out at the polls.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are people who are absolutely passionate about reproductive freedom and making sure that an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban doesn\u8217\'20\'19t come to Virginia,\u8221\'20\'1D Cole said.\par \par \uc2 Four days later, Cole was proven right, defeating the Republican candidate Lee Peters to represent house district 65 in Richmond, the capital of Virginia. Cole\u8217\'20\'19s victory reflected Virginia Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 broader success on election day, as the party flipped control of the house of delegates and maintained their majority in the state senate.\par \par \uc2 Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 wins in Virginia may now offer some helpful lessons for the party heading into a crucial presidential election. A year and a half after the supreme court overturned Roe v Wade, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to weigh heavily on voters\u8217\'20\'19 minds, helping to lift Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 prospects at the polls. Even as Biden {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://projects.fivethirtyeight.com/biden-approval-rating/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 remains unpopular}}}\uc2  and voters {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/11/20/upshot/economy-voters-poll.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 express pessimism}}}\uc2  about the state of the economy, Republicans have struggled to translate that dissatisfaction into electoral success.\par \par \uc2 House district 65 in particular represents a fascinating example of how Republicans failed to win the support of swing voters who helped elect \uc2 Glenn Youngkin\uc2 , the Virginia governor, two years earlier. The district, which was newly redrawn following the 2020 census, lies roughly halfway between Washington and Richmond and encompasses the small city of Fredericksburg, as well as parts of Stafford and Spotsylvania counties.\par \par \uc2 The battleground district supported Biden by 11.7 points in 2020, according to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://centerforpolitics.org/crystalball/articles/the-race-for-virginias-legislature-part-two/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 University of Virginia\u8217\'20\'19s Center for Politics}}}\uc2 . Just one year later, the district went for Youngkin by 2.8 points. Both parties had targeted the seat, with Youngkin himself appearing alongside Peters at a get out the vote rally in Fredericksburg the day before polls closed.\par \par \uc2 Republicans had hoped Peters\u8217\'20\'19 biography as a sheriff\u8217\'20\'19s captain and a former marine would help him defeat Cole, a local pastor and former delegate who narrowly lost his re-election race in 2021. But Cole ultimately won the seat by 6 points.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis was in no way a predetermined result. It\u8217\'20\'19s not a solid blue district at all. It was a winnable one [for Republicans],\u8221\'20\'1D said Mark Rozell, dean of the Schar School of Policy and Government at \uc2 George Mason University\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CAnd probably among the house of delegates districts, it best represents what went wrong for the Republicans when it should have been a better year for them in the legislative races.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 This was in no way a predetermined result. It\u8217\'20\'19s not a solid blue district at all\par \par \uc2 Mark Rozell\par \par \uc2 Democrats credit their success in the district and elsewhere to one issue: \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Democrats consistently reminded voters of Virginia\u8217\'20\'19s status as the last remaining state in the US south without severe restrictions on the procedure, warning that Republicans would enact an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban if they took full control of the legislature.\par \par \uc2 Those warnings appeared to resonate with Virginians; according to a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2023/10/20/abortion-virginia-election-poll-youngkin-approval/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Washington Post-Schar School poll}}}\uc2  conducted in October, 60% of voters in the state said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a \u8220\'20\'1Cvery important\u8221\'20\'1D factor in their election decisions. More than half of Virginia voters, 51%, said they trusted Democrats more when it came to handling \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies, while 34% said the same of Republicans.\par \par \uc2 In this year\u8217\'20\'19s race, Cole kept relentless attention on the issue, citing his support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in nearly all of his ads and mailers while {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=H20Gr_Q-EaE"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 attacking}}}\uc2  Peters over his \u8220\'20\'1Canti-choice extremism\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt was very interesting because it seemed as if people were showing up on one issue,\u8221\'20\'1D Cole said after election day. \u8220\'20\'1COf course, we did talk about kitchen-table issues when we\u8217\'20\'19re on the doors and different things like that, but our message was simple. We need to trust women and we need to protect a woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to choose and we need to make sure that the government doesn\u8217\'20\'19t interfere with that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Virginia Republicans were clearly aware that their stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could become a liability in the legislative races, particularly after the party\u8217\'20\'19s disappointing performance in the 2022 midterms. To address voters\u8217\'20\'19 potential concerns over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Youngkin chose to deploy a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/nov/01/republicans-abortion-ads-virginia-election"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 new and untested messaging tactic}}}\uc2 . He proposed a \u8220\'20\'1Creasonable 15-week limit\u8221\'20\'1D on the procedure, rejecting the label of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cban\u8221\'20\'1D and instead accusing Democrats of being out of step with voters on the issue.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMost people believe that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the moment of birth is wrong, far beyond any reasonable limit. Not Virginia Democrats,\u8221\'20\'1D the narrator said in one Republican ad. \u8220\'20\'1CThey fought to make {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/sep/27/abortion-myths-republicans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 late-term abortions}}}\uc2  the rule, not the exception.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Republicans also attempted to downplay the significance of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the legislative races, insisting Virginia voters were more focused on other issues. Peters himself made this argument at a September debate, saying, \u8220\'20\'1CEverybody is not concerned or worried about women\u8217\'20\'19s rights, even though there are many, many women who are. Some people worry about public safety. Some people worry about their schools.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But in the end, Virginia Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 efforts to redefine and minimize the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate were unsuccessful. Democrats maintained a majority of 21-19 in the Virginia senate while flipping control of the house of delegates with a majority of 51-49.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey tested some new messages around this issue \u8211\'20\'13 with the intention of getting to the same result, which was an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. And voters just outright rejected them,\u8221\'20\'1D said Heather Williams, president of the Democratic Legislative Campaign Committee. \u8220\'20\'1CRepublicans are still scratching their head on how to talk about an issue that voters don\u8217\'20\'19t want.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It really doesn\u8217\'20\'19t take a rocket scientist to quickly analyze why Republicans did not perform better at the polls\par \par \uc2 Bill Bolling\par \par \uc2 Even fellow Republicans have acknowledged that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has become a persistent problem for the party\u8217\'20\'19s electoral prospects. Bill Bolling, a Republican and the former lieutenant governor of Virginia, attributed the party\u8217\'20\'19s losses to three factors: \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and a lack of a clear policy vision.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt really doesn\u8217\'20\'19t take a rocket scientist to quickly analyze why Republicans did not perform better at the polls,\u8221\'20\'1D Bolling {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bearingdrift.com/2023/11/08/bolling-election-day-2023-another-bad-day-for-the-gop/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote}}}\uc2  last month. \u8220\'20\'1CDemocrats successfully argued that Republicans wanted to \u8216\'20\'18ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 in Virginia. While this argument was certainly not truthful, it was effective, especially with suburban women who have grown increasingly Democratic in their voting patterns in recent years.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In Cole\u8217\'20\'19s view, his message to voters spread beyond \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access to encompass other rights, allowing his campaign to embrace a central theme of safeguarding fundamental freedoms.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis election was about protecting rights, whether it\u8217\'20\'19s the right to education, women\u8217\'20\'19s rights, the right to live safely in the streets, or whatever have you. This race was about rights,\u8221\'20\'1D Cole said. \u8220\'20\'1C[Voters] understood that we definitely have to have people fighting for us on every level, who are looking out for us and our rights.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 That theme was similarly present in the messaging of other Democratic candidates in Virginia, Williams said. She suggested that their success could offer a framework for candidates running next year, when Democrats will be fighting to hold the White House and the Senate and flip control of the House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 Republicans have just constantly squandered whatever advantage that they have by focusing on divisive social issues\par \par \uc2 Mark Rozell\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe way that that [message] shows up in an individual community or state may look different. One community may gravitate much more towards having good safe schools and a planet to live on,\u8221\'20\'1D Williams said. \u8220\'20\'1CBut that arc is still true \u8211\'20\'13 that fundamental freedoms matter and voters want to see their freedoms protected and not rolled back.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 For Republicans, the results in Virginia present the latest in a series of warning signs about how the party is suffering because of its stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Youngkin\u8217\'20\'19s failure to take control of the legislature may signal that Republicans must find a way to shift the conversation away from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , although that strategy risks angering their rightwing base.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt seems to me that Republicans have just constantly squandered whatever advantage that they have by focusing on divisive social issues where the voters are not aligning with their position,\u8221\'20\'1D Rozell said. \u8220\'20\'1CSo they need to find a way out of that trap that they\u8217\'20\'19ve made for themselves. Otherwise, they\u8217\'20\'19re going to keep losing winnable districts.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231220ejck001p8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
