\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Kate Cox case reveals toll of US  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  bans on women in medical emergencies\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 1,433 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 December 2023\par 11:26 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 25\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Lawsuits from women denied the procedure despite health risks shows how bans don\u8217\'20\'19t allow for complexities of pregnancy\par \par \uc2 When Kate Cox got the news that her baby would probably only live for a few days, she went online to figure out her options. A 31-year-old mother of two living in Texas, Cox could not get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but she also knew that she did not want to make her baby suffer.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s when Cox came across the news that 20 Texas women had come forward to tell a court that they, like her, had been unable to get abortions in medical emergencies. Within days, Cox went public too: she became the first woman since the fall of Roe v Wade to sue for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  while actively pregnant.\par \par \uc2 Dozens of women across the United States have now launched lawsuits alleging that they were denied abortions even in the midst of pregnancy complications that put them at often severe risk. Their voices have redefined the conversation around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the US since the fall of Roe v Wade, casting a spotlight on the unique circumstances that can lead to unexpected abortions, the complicated reality of pregnancy and the medical and ethical difficulty of legislating both.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, Cox fled Texas for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , her lawyers said earlier this week. Hours after their announcement, the Texas state supreme court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/dec/11/texas-supreme-court-overturns-ruling-kate-cox-emergency-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturned a lower court order}}}\uc2  that would have let her terminate her pregnancy in her home state.\par \par \uc2 In the wake of \uc2 Cox\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s lawsuit, dozens of people have reached out to \uc2 Cox\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s lawyers to say that they want to find some way to help \u8211\'20\'13 or that what happened to Cox happened to them, too.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think it\u8217\'20\'19s touched a nerve. People think the laws are too extreme, which they are,\u8221\'20\'1D said Nick Kabat, a staff attorney at the \uc2 Center for Reproductive Rights\uc2 , which represents Cox as well as several other women who have launched lawsuits after their abortions were blocked despite medical emergencies. \u8220\'20\'1CPeople recognize how dangerous it is. Pregnancy is complicated already, as it is, and having the state get in the way of doctors providing medical care to patients \u8211\'20\'13 it\u8217\'20\'19s just a bad idea.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Technically, all \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in the US have some kind of carve-out that should allow people to get abortions in emergencies. But countless doctors have said that these exceptions don\u8217\'20\'19t reckon with the complexities of pregnancy. Due to the bans\u8217\'20\'19 vague and politicized language, as well as the severe criminal penalties for providers who run afoul of the law, doctors say that they are confused about when they can step in to help patients and hamstrung by the fear of prosecution.\par \par \uc2 Instead, doctors say , they have been forced to wait and watch until patients are on the brink of death. Then they have to pull them back.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CA person gets to decide for themselves: what is a medical emergency for them?\u8221\'20\'1D said Dr Ghazaleh Moayedi, a Texas OB-GYN. For a pregnant person, \u8220\'20\'1Cit is an emergency, it is imminent harm, to be forced to continue a pregnancy even one more minute than needed, when they know that their baby is suffering and will suffer and will die and that they will be forced into a major surgery with potential life-impacting complications.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Originally, five women sued Texas over its medical emergency exceptions, which the lawsuit hopes to clarify. The suit is named for Amanda Zurawski, whose water broke too early in pregnancy for her to give birth to a healthy baby. Because her doctors could still detect fetal cardiac activity, she was unable to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Days later, she was diagnosed with sepsis. She ended up spending three days in the ICU, and, thanks to scarring on her reproductive organs, she may never be able to get pregnant again.\par \par \uc2 As the months have passed, more and more women have signed on to Zurawski\u8217\'20\'19s lawsuit. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/nov/28/texas-supreme-court-hears-extreme-abortion-ban-lawsuit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Dr Danielle Mathisen joined in November}}}\uc2 . She had fled to New Mexico for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after learning that her baby had a condition that meant she would probably die soon after birth.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are so many things that can harm you in pregnancy, apart from the birth itself,\u8221\'20\'1D said Mathisen, who is an OB-GYN. \u8220\'20\'1CThe birth itself \u8211\'20\'13 you can end up with major abdominal surgery, you can hemorrhage, you can pass an embolism. The pregnancy itself \u8211\'20\'13 you can develop pre-eclampsia, you can develop diabetes. It\u8217\'20\'19s not a health-neutral event.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Kabat declined to say what the future of \uc2 Cox\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s lawsuit might look like. The Texas lawsuit involving Zurawski and Mathisen is awaiting a ruling from the Texas state supreme court.\par \par \uc2 Similar lawsuits are under way in Idaho and Tennessee. One of the women in the Tennessee lawsuit, Allie Phillips, is running for office as a member of the first generation of female candidates to run post-Roe. After her fetus was diagnosed with several lethal conditions, Phillips had to travel to New York for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI can scream on TikTok, I can scream at marches, I can scream at the Capitol every day for the rest of my life. But that\u8217\'20\'19s not going to change the people inside who are voting,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/oct/16/tennessee-abortion-ban-lawsuit-allie-phillips-representative"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Phillips told the Guardian}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CI have to be inside. I have to be the one that\u8217\'20\'19s voting \u8211\'20\'13 on the actual bills, not on the ballot.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In Oklahoma, a woman has also filed a federal complaint against two hospitals. Desperate for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as her non-viable pregnancy worsened, the woman was told to wait in a parking lot until she was \u8220\'20\'1Ccrashing\u8221\'20\'1D, according to the complaint.\par \par \uc2 Her complaint centers around a federal law that requires providers to treat people in medical emergencies. Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates say that requirement does not extend to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while the Biden administration has argued that it does.\par \par \uc2 Now, the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  may soon get pulled into that battle, as the highest court has been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/nov/30/idaho-punish-abortion-provider-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 asked to review}}}\uc2  a case out of Idaho about the law and its link to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Fights over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are also being waged on several other legal fronts \u8211\'20\'13 as evidenced by a flurry of developments just in the last few days.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  announced this week that it would take up a different \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case that deals with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/aug/17/what-abortion-pill-supreme-court-case-access-mifepristone"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 restrictions around \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  pills}}}\uc2 . Arguments in that case have not been scheduled, but a ruling could be reached by summer 2024 \u8211\'20\'13 just in time to upend the 2024 US presidential election.\par \par \uc2 In addition, this week, state courts held hearings on multiple lawsuits, some of which were orchestrated by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates seeking to expand on the string of bans implemented since the fall of Roe. Arizona is weighing whether to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/dec/12/arizona-abortion-ban-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reinstate a 19th-century \u8220\'20\'1Czombie\u8221\'20\'1D law}}}\uc2  that would ban almost all abortions in the state. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  foes went to court in New Mexico in a case involving a different 19th-century law, which threatens to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/dec/13/abortion-ban-comstock-act-new-mexico"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unleash a de facto \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ban}}}\uc2  nationwide. A Wyoming judge is now deliberating over whether to let a first-of-its-kind ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills take effect.\par \par \uc2 And while \uc2 Cox\uc2  was the first pregnant woman to file a post-Roe lawsuit seeking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , she wasn\u8217\'20\'19t the last. Mere days after Cox went public, a Kentucky woman announced that she, too, was suing for the right to end her pregnancy in a state that outlaws almost all abortions. (It is unclear whether that lawsuit will continue as the woman has since learned {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/dec/13/kentucky-abortion-ban-lawsuit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 her fetus no longer has cardiac activity}}}\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 Regardless of their outcome, the lawsuits brought by patients over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans represent a \u8220\'20\'1Cremarkable grassroots movement\u8221\'20\'1D, said Nicole Huberfeld, co-director of the Boston University School of Law program in reproductive justice. \u8220\'20\'1CI do think it\u8217\'20\'19s important that patients are willing to speak out, to have a voice.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 US conservative, Christian organizations have long been skilled at finding plaintiffs and bringing lawsuits to dismantle statutes that run counter to their beliefs; the case that ultimately overturned Roe arose out of a law created in order to be challenged in court. Now, through these lawsuits, the reproductive rights movement is finally answering that effort with one of its own.\par \par \uc2 But, Huberfeld cautioned, it\u8217\'20\'19s probably not enough to calm the legal chaos.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese test cases grab headlines,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CBut the real answers are going to be in writing laws that make it so that there\u8217\'20\'19s a more stable landscape.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231218ejci000fx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
