\par\par \uc2 Law\par \b \uc2 Justice \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2  took 10 minutes to approve Dobbs  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  opinion \u8211\'20\'13 report\b0\par\par\uc2 Martin Pengelly in Washington \par \uc2 633 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 December 2023\par 16:19 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reports conservative supreme court justice had no changes to 98-page draft of opinion that removed right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 The conservative supreme court justice \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2  took just 10 minutes to approve without changes a 98-page draft of the opinion that would remove the federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that had been guaranteed for nearly 50 years, the \uc2 New York Times{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/12/15/us/supreme-court-dobbs-roe-abortion.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 According to the paper, \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , the author of the opinion in Dobbs v Jackson, the case that struck down Roe v Wade, from 1973, circulated his draft at 11.16am on 10 February 2022.\par \par \uc2 Citing two people who saw communications between the justices, the Times {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/12/15/us/supreme-court-dobbs-roe-abortion.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1CAfter a justice shares an opinion inside the court, other members scrutinise it. Those in the majority can request revisions, sometimes as the price of their votes, sweating sentences or even words.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBut this time, despite the document\u8217\'20\'19s length, Justice \uc2 Neil M Gorsuch\uc2  wrote back just 10 minutes later to say that he would sign on to the opinion and had no changes.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Three other conservatives \u8211\'20\'13 \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  and \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 signed on in the following days.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNone requested a single alteration,\u8221\'20\'1D the Times {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/12/15/us/supreme-court-dobbs-roe-abortion.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CThe responses looked like a display of conservative force and discipline.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In a statement to the Guardian, Caroline Ciccone, president of the watchdog group Accountable.US, said: \u8220\'20\'1CThese revelations offer yet another example of the current supreme court plowing through usual guardrails and tossing precedent aside to deliver an extreme decision ultimately impacting millions of Americans.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Alito\u8217\'20\'19s draft opinion was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/03/chief-justice-roberts-abortion-leak-investigation-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 leaked}}}\uc2 , then published by Politico in May. An {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2023/jan/19/us-supreme-court-leaked-dobbs-draft-opinion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 investigation}}}\uc2  did not name a culprit. Critics have postulated that the leak might have come from the right, as a way to lock in votes. Alito, who has complained that the leak made rightwingers on the court \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/apr/28/samuel-alito-draft-abortion-ruling-supreme-court-justice"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 targets of assassination}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D, has also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/apr/28/samuel-alito-draft-abortion-ruling-supreme-court-justice"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  the leaker was not a conservative and claimed to have a \u8220\'20\'1Cpretty good idea\u8221\'20\'1D who it was.\par \par \uc2 The full opinion was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/24/roe-v-wade-overturned-abortion-summary-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 passed down in June}}}\uc2 . Conservatives celebrated a great victory. Progressives protested. Since then, attacks on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights including stringent bans in Republican-run states have fueled a series of Democratic election victories.\par \par \uc2 High-level Democratic sources {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/dec/12/heather-williams-democrat-interview-abortion-republicans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have said}}}\uc2  the party will centre the issue in next year\u8217\'20\'19s elections, as a rare subject on which they enjoy a distinct polling advantage.\par \par \uc2 The court remains at the centre of debate over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, this week {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/aug/17/what-abortion-pill-supreme-court-case-access-mifepristone"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 saying}}}\uc2  it would hear a case about access to mifepristone, one of two pills typically used in the US for abortions by medication, a key part of maintaining access after Dobbs.\par \par \uc2 Ciccone said the Times report \u8220\'20\'1Cbegs serious questions as the high court takes up another critical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  decision this term \u8211\'20\'13 one pushed through the courts by far-right interests and influencers like \uc2 Leonard Leo\uc2  [of the \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2 ] who knew their agenda was too radical and unpopular to ever win at the ballot box\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/12/15/us/supreme-court-dobbs-roe-abortion.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 exhaustive reporting}}}\uc2  of how Dobbs came to be decided, and maneuvering among the justices, the Times said \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2 , a Catholic conservative appointed by Donald Trump to replace \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 , a liberal lion and protector of women\u8217\'20\'19s rights, ultimately voted not to hear the case. But, the paper said, four men \u8211\'20\'13 Gorsuch, Kavanaugh, Alito and Thomas \u8211\'20\'13 decided to move forward.\par \par \uc2 The conservative chief justice, \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , and the liberal \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2  then sought to find middle ground. Roberts voted with the liberals to uphold Roe but the ultimate decision went the other way, 5-4 as Barrett ended up voting to knock down Roe.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CGuided by Justice Alito\u8221\'20\'1D, the Times {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/12/15/us/supreme-court-dobbs-roe-abortion.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2 , the court \u8220\'20\'1Cengineered a titanic shift in the law\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231215ejcf002js\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
