\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 New Mexico supreme court to consider obscure 1873 law used to ban  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 1,024 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 December 2023\par 14:35 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 State is latest in which the Comstock Act is being used to try to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the fall of Roe v Wade\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists across the United States are convinced that a 19th-century anti-vice law could hold the key to banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide. Their strategy could be put to the test this week.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the New Mexico supreme court will hear arguments in a case over local ordinances passed in the towns of Hobbs and Clovis, in the east of the state, as well as the nearby Lea and Roosevelt counties. All four localities have passed similar ordinances claiming that the Comstock Act, an 1873 law meant to target vice, bans the mailing of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related materials. The ordinances also effectively ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the four areas overall, prompting New Mexico\u8217\'20\'19s attorney general to ask the supreme court to strike them down.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBecause those ordinances violate state law, our office had to get involved,\u8221\'20\'1D Aletheia Allen, solicitor general in the \uc2 New Mexico attorney general\u8217\'20\'19s office\uc2 , said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt flies in the face of what they\u8217\'20\'19re allowed to do procedurally, as local governments. They\u8217\'20\'19re just not allowed to regulate in this area, which is healthcare and medical licensing.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But while the \uc2 New Mexico attorney general\u8217\'20\'19s office\uc2  is arguing that the localities\u8217\'20\'19 ordinances violate New Mexico law and its protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists are hoping to ultimately make a much bigger play. They believe that the Comstock Act trumps any state-level \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws \u8211\'20\'13 to the point that the it could result in a total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban across the US.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese ordinances \u8211\'20\'13 they don\u8217\'20\'19t explicitly outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They\u8217\'20\'19re de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. They\u8217\'20\'19re simply requiring compliance with federal statutes,\u8221\'20\'1D said Mark Lee Dickson, who has made a career of traversing the country and urging localities to pass anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd people are leaning upon them because that is the strongest argument that exists in terms of the federal law, that can be used to see \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  come to an end in every state in America.\u8221\'20\'1D Dickson has championed the New Mexico ordinances and plans to attend arguments on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Named for anti-vice crusader Anthony Comstock, the Comstock Act originally banned \u8220\'20\'1Cevery obscene, lewd, lascivious, indecent, filthy or vile article, matter, thing, device, or substance\u8221\'20\'1D from being mailed, including \u8220\'20\'1Cevery article or thing designed, adapted, or intended for producing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D. Its scope narrowed by years of litigation and its ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related materials rendered meaningless by Roe v Wade, the Comstock Act was long regarded as a relic of a past, prudish era. But that ban was never formally repealed.\par \par \uc2 After the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  struck down Roe v Wade last year, some anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists began to insist that the Comstock Act, by federally banning the mailing of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related materials, was back in effect. Since \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics rely on the mail to receive the medical equipment necessary for their work, a ban on mailing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related materials would effectively stop them from operating.\par \par \uc2 Beyond citing it in the ordinances in New Mexico, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups are pursuing a number of other avenues in their efforts to revive the Comstock Act. References to the law have surfaced in an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  coalition\u8217\'20\'19s lawsuit seeking to suspend the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s approval of a major \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill. They also appear in an association of attorneys generals\u8217\'20\'19 warning to pharmacies not to dispense the pill, and in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://theintercept.com/2023/04/26/abortion-wrongful-death-texas-lawsuit/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrongful death lawsuit}}}\uc2  filed in Texas by a man who accused his ex-wife\u8217\'20\'19s friends of helping her get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (The FDA case is now before the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 , which has not yet said whether it will formally take up the case.)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  foes are hoping a court bites.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CClearly a lot of people with power in the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement think that the Comstock Act is really important,\u8221\'20\'1D said Mary Ziegler, a professor at the \uc2 University of California, Davis\uc2 , School of Law. \u8220\'20\'1CThey really want the conservative \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  to take their side in interpreting it, and they\u8217\'20\'19re just throwing up lots of opportunities.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration\u8217\'20\'19s justice department {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.justice.gov/olc/opinion/file/1560596/download"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has publicly said}}}\uc2  that it\u8217\'20\'19s not illegal to mail \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related materials as long as the sender doesn\u8217\'20\'19t intend to violate the law, guidance that\u8217\'20\'19s in line with several past courts\u8217\'20\'19 interpretations of the Comstock Act.\par \par \uc2 New Mexico is a haven for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  seekers fleeing bans in other southern states. In the year after Roe\u8217\'20\'19s demise, providers there performed an average of 700 more abortions each month compared with the months before, according to research from the Society of Family Planning.\par \par \uc2 Several \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics that were forced to close in states that enacted bans have also relocated to New Mexico. At least one, Whole Woman\u8217\'20\'19s Health, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vice.com/en/article/k7bmxn/future-of-abortion-war-is-in-new-mexico"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 initially considered moving}}}\uc2  to the area around Hobbs and Clovis, because it is close to the border between New Mexico and Texas.\par \par \uc2 Legal experts told the Guardian that it\u8217\'20\'19s unlikely that this particular case in New Mexico will serve as the justification to revive the Comstock Act. Not only is the New Mexico state supreme court stacked with Democrats, but none of the localities that passed ordinances have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic \u8211\'20\'13 so it\u8217\'20\'19s unclear why it needs to be resolved in court in the first place.\par \par \uc2 Allen, the solicitor general, disagreed. There is a live controversy, she said, that affects the future of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the state.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEven though there\u8217\'20\'19s not currently a clinic perhaps in those four local government areas, one couldn\u8217\'20\'19t be established with the ordinances in place,\u8221\'20\'1D Allen said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt also creates \u8211\'20\'13 and I think we know this and we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen this and we\u8217\'20\'19ve heard a lot of people talking about it \u8211\'20\'13 a lot of fear. That\u8217\'20\'19s a live issue. We don\u8217\'20\'19t want our own residents in our own state afraid to seek necessary healthcare because of some local ordinance that shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t exist.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231213ejcd001e3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
