\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Heather Williams, president of DLCC: \u8216\'20\'18Republicans are not listening to voters\u8217\'20\'19 on  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 David Smith in Washington \par \uc2 1,451 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 December 2023\par 11:46 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s crucial for Democrats to connect with voters on the topic and focus on state capitols where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  allies are pressing for sweeping bans\par \par \uc2 Democrats should hammer extremist Republicans as \u8220\'20\'1Cout of touch\u8221\'20\'1D with ordinary voters on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  rights}}}\uc2  ahead of crucial state legislature elections next November, a leading party fundraiser says.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://dlcc.org/heather-williams/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Heather Williams}}}\uc2 , whose appointment as president of the Democratic Legislative Campaign Committee (DLCC) was first shared with the Guardian on Tuesday, urged her party to stay focused on state capitols, where allies of former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  are pursuing sweeping \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C Republicans are not listening to the voters and they don\u8217\'20\'19t seem to care or feel any consequence for being on the wrong side of where not just voters in their state are, but where Americans are,\u8221\'20\'1D Williams, 42, said in an interview at the headquarters of the DLCC, the party\u8217\'20\'19s campaign arm focused on state legislative races.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t know if it\u8217\'20\'19s their values base, I don\u8217\'20\'19t know what they\u8217\'20\'19re doing, but whatever it is, it\u8217\'20\'19s very out of touch. They don\u8217\'20\'19t seem to connect to the consequences and so we\u8217\'20\'19re going to continue to tell that story. As people connect to the impact of it on their real lives and the way that their lives take shape and what it means for them, Republicans are going to continue to be in the hot seat on these issues.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Reproductive healthcare is a potential rallying point for Democrats in 2024 as \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  faces stiff headwinds on the economy, border security and his age. Last year\u8217\'20\'19s supreme court decision to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2022/jun/26/us-supreme-court-abortion-ruling-democracy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturn Roe v Wade}}}\uc2 , the 1973 ruling that enshrined a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has boomeranged on Republicans in a series of special elections and ballot measures.\par \par \uc2 The human cost has been exposed in a series of legal actions and harrowing cases. On Monday a Texas woman who sued the state for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after receiving a lethal fetal diagnosis {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/dec/11/texas-abortion-kate-cox-lawsuit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fled the state}}}\uc2  in order to obtain the procedure. A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/abortion-kentucky-aclu-lawsuit-14df6d5e8a5aaa36009557d080b1c779"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pregnant woman in Kentucky}}}\uc2  has also gone to court to seek immediate access to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 As people connect to the impact of it on their real lives \u8230\'20\'26 Republicans are going to continue to be in the hot seat on these issues\par \par \uc2 In the absence of a national law, state capitols have become frontline battlegrounds, making next year\u8217\'20\'19s elections for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ballotpedia.org/State_legislature"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 state legislative chambers}}}\uc2  in 44 states arguably the most important yet. Williams, who has spent a cumulative 13 years at the DLCC and was more recently interim president, reflected: \u8220\'20\'1CThe impact of legislatures and state government on one\u8217\'20\'19s life has never been clearer and it\u8217\'20\'19s a truly incredible time to be able to lead this organisation.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Last week the DLCC announced its first round of 2024 battleground state investments, sending nearly $300,000 to Michigan, Arizona, New Hampshire, Wisconsin, Pennsylvania and North Carolina. The committee is aiming to raise a record $60m for this cycle and as it seeks to continue a winning streak.\par \par \uc2 Last year it defied predictions of a \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/09/election-night-key-takeaways"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 red wave}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D by maintaining control of every state Democratic majority and flipping four chambers \u8211\'20\'13 the best midterm year for the president\u8217\'20\'19s party since at least 1934. The DLCC went on to overperform in last month\u8217\'20\'19s elections and win full control of the legislature in Virginia, where {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/oct/30/hes-pretty-smooth-glenn-youngkin-of-virginia-may-be-a-challenger-for-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Governor Glenn Youngkin}}}\uc2  had threatened to sign a 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban if Republicans won both chambers.\par \par \uc2 Williams recalled: \u8220\'20\'1CIn the lead-up to the elections in Virginia, voters knew that, if they went to the polls on Tuesday and woke up with a Republican trifecta in their state legislature, there would be {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/nov/01/republicans-abortion-ads-virginia-election"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ban}}}\uc2 . That very clear line of stakes has resonated but also motivated people and we\u8217\'20\'19re seeing more interest, engagement, enthusiasm, participation in legislative races.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Yet still \u8220\'20\'1CMake America great again\u8221\'20\'1D anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  extremism has been spreading in state legislatures. New Hampshire Republicans recently proposed an extreme ban at 15 days. Missouri Republicans are pushing a law that would {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/abortion-homicide-missouri-legislation-e192f15396b4cd82e593819c2692cd56"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 allow homicide charges}}}\uc2  against women who obtain abortions. Legislation banning most abortions after 12 weeks became law in North Carolina after Republicans overrode a Democratic governor\u8217\'20\'19s veto.\par \par \uc2 Opinion polls show such measures are wildly unpopular, even among Republican voters, and Democrats intend to continue shining an unforgiving light on them. Williams, originally from Moorhead, Minnesota, said: \u8220\'20\'1CA lot of people historically talked about the states as being sort of a laboratory for our democracy and a laboratory for policy ideas.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt was a lot easier for them to happen under the radar when there was less attention on the ballot level and when people assumed that the issues were more benign or more local \u8211\'20\'13 state taxes and how are we spending that money, state regulatory framework for businesses.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs the stakes have become clearer and as people have connected with what is happening in their state government, there\u8217\'20\'19s a lot more eyes right on the kind of policies that are coming out and a lot more attention and I don\u8217\'20\'19t think that Republicans are getting away with the undercover, nefarious things that they used to. There are just more alarms being sounded early.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Republicans came into the 2010 election with a national strategy that changed the landscape of state legislatures\par \par \uc2 In the past, Democrats were slow to recognise the significance of statehouses and senates. The party lost nearly 1,000 state legislative seats during the presidency of \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 . As of 5 December, Republicans controlled 54.75% of all state legislative seats nationally, while Democrats held 44.35%, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ballotpedia.org/State_legislative_elections,_2024"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to Ballotpedia}}}\uc2 . Republicans held a majority in 57 chambers and Democrats held the majority in 39 chambers.\par \par \uc2 State legislative chambers are uniquely influential \u8211\'20\'13 and potentially dangerous \u8211\'20\'13 because they can move much faster than the federal government in Washington. This has resulted in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/sep/23/florida-desantis-book-ban-school-student-parent"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 book bans}}}\uc2 , attacks on transgender children\u8217\'20\'19s rights, voter disenfranchisement and failure to tackle gun safety in schools.\par \par \uc2 Williams acknowledged: \u8220\'20\'1CRepublicans came into the 2010 election with a national strategy that changed the landscape of state legislatures. They flat out were better at bringing their national party, their national donors or national strategists into these state races. Democrats still had a very local focus and approach \u8211\'20\'13 what happened in the states was important but it was in the state. It was very localised and 2010 changed that.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI would argue that the silver lining in that was that the face of Democrats changed \u8211\'20\'13 the face of Democratic leadership, the face of Democrats running for office. We found diversity. There were women leaders, there were moms, there were people of colour and now we\u8217\'20\'19ve got this incredibly representative, diverse coalition of Democrats in the states and in state leadership and through that lens, you just get better policy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Now it is Republicans who are gambling by continuing to embrace Trump despite the string of defeats he and his endorsed candidates have suffered in recent years. Nowhere is his cult of personality more evident than in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/jan/30/republicans-radical-extreme-state-parties"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  state parties}}}\uc2 , where white nationalist ideas and far-right conspiracy theories have been allowed to thrive among his foot soldiers.\par \par \uc2 Williams said: \u8220\'20\'1CHe is their leader and he shifted the perspective of the Republican party. The Republican party that so many of us remember from before was one that cared about the budget and taxes and it was quite boring but you saw them through that lens. Sometimes voters agreed that it was time to buckle up and sometimes they were like, nope, that\u8217\'20\'19s not going to work.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNow you\u8217\'20\'19ve got a Republican party where it\u8217\'20\'19s like they\u8217\'20\'19re constantly trying to outdo each other. The impact on people has gotten lost in that and it\u8217\'20\'19s all now just, \u8216\'20\'18OK, well, if you\u8217\'20\'19re going to come across with an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban at 20 weeks, well, I\u8217\'20\'19m going to go to 15 and then I\u8217\'20\'19m going to go to five and suddenly and you know what, I\u8217\'20\'19m going to say 15 days.\u8217\'20\'19 It snowballs because they\u8217\'20\'19ve lost touch with what it actually means and who it actually impacts.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey fundamentally believe that if somebody in their life needed access to reproductive healthcare, they would just get it because they they don\u8217\'20\'19t think about anyone else. They just know that they\u8217\'20\'19ve got access to it. That lack of connection to actual people, and more trying to win praise and attention and position within the party, has contributed to their lost ways.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231213ejcd001jq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
