\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Kate Cox case: what led to the Texan fleeing the state for an  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2 ?\b0\par\par\uc2 Ava Sasani \par \uc2 730 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 December 2023\par 18:46 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Texas mother sued to obtain procedure after learning of non-viable pregnancy, but state supreme court overruled decision\par \par \uc2 A Texas woman {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/dec/11/texas-abortion-kate-cox-lawsuit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fled her home state}}}\uc2  to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after Texas\u8217\'20\'19s top court ruled last week that she cannot terminate her non-viable pregnancy despite risks to her life and future fertility.\par \par \uc2 What was the Kate Cox case\par \par \uc2 about?\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, Kate Cox, a 31-year-old mother of two young children, sued to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after learning that her fetus has trisomy 18, a fatal chromosomal condition, as well as other health issues, including a spinal abnormality.\par \par \uc2 Doctors informed Cox and her husband \u8220\'20\'1Cthere was virtually no chance that their baby would survive to birth or long afterwards\u8221\'20\'1D, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://reproductiverights.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/12/Cox-v.-Texas-original-petition-FINAL-1.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to court filings.}}}\uc2  Continuing the pregnancy would pose grave risks to \uc2 Cox\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s life and would probably jeopardize her future fertility.\par \par \uc2 Still, Cox was unable to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Texas because of the state\u8217\'20\'19s strict laws prohibiting the procedure. Earlier this month, Cox filed an emergency lawsuit asking a judge to allow her OB-GYN to provide an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  without threat of prosecution.\par \par \uc2 What are\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18medical exceptions\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 to Texas\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans?\par \par \uc2 Texas began enforcing a law banning virtually all \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year, shortly after the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  ruled that there is no constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the United States.\par \par \uc2 The state law technically includes exceptions for cases of medical emergencies, but Texas doctors have said that the exceptions are too vague, and force them to wait until their patients get sick enough to intervene.\par \par \uc2 Cox\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s story has grown increasingly common in conservative states like Texas.\par \par \uc2 In March, five women denied abortions in Texas, along with two doctors, sued the state after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/mar/07/texas-abortion-women-lawsuit-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 they were refused care despite suffering severe complications}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/mar/07/texas-abortion-women-lawsuit-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 with their pregnancies}}}\uc2 . Fifteen Texas women have since joined the lawsuit, which seeks to clarify the murkiness surrounding medical exceptions to the state\u8217\'20\'19s ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Why didn\u8217\'20\'19t Kate Cox\u8217\'20\'19s pregnancy qualify as a medical exception?\par \par \uc2 According to court filings, \uc2 Cox\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s doctors said that if the baby\u8217\'20\'19s heartbeat stopped, they could induce labor \u8211\'20\'13 but they cannot perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedure known as dilation and evacuation (D&E). Due to her prior C-sections, inducing labor means she faces a higher risk of rupturing her uterus. A D&E would be the best medical option for her health, but doctors in the state who might provide it fear prosecution {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/dec/05/texas-woman-emergency-abortion-lawsuit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 under Texas\u8217\'20\'19s criminal \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ban}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Texas doctors accused of violating the state\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law face up to $100,000 in fines or even life in prison.\par \par \uc2 Do similar provisions exist in other states\u8217\'20\'19 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans?\par \par \uc2 Texas is among the 21 states that ban or heavily restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the United States today. Most of these anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws include narrow exceptions to protect the life or health of the pregnant person.\par \par \uc2 But doctors and hospitals face serious punishment if they misinterpret these medical exceptions. In Texas and other conservative states, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers are threatened with hefty fines, loss of medical license or even prison time.\par \par \uc2 Many \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers said bans, like those effective in Texas, create a chilling effect among doctors who are scared to misinterpret what constitutes a medical emergency.\par \par \uc2 What happened to Kate Cox\u8217\'20\'19s lawsuit?\par \par \uc2 After a lower court judge ruled in favor of Kate Cox, the Texas attorney general, \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , a conservative serving his third term as the state\u8217\'20\'19s top prosecutor, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/dec/12/texas-abortion-ken-paxton-kate-cox"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 threatened to punish}}}\uc2  any doctor who provided an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to Cox.\par \par \uc2 Texas has a law on the books that permits private individuals to sue over suspected illegal abortions. Regardless of the lower court order, doctors could be held liable under that law, Paxton warned.\par \par \uc2 The Texas attorney general\u8217\'20\'19s threats underscored the limits of medical exceptions in a state that is hostile to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, sowing fear and confusion among doctors involved in Cox\u8217\'20\'19s treatment.\par \par \uc2 Hours later, Paxton and his office filed an appeal with the state\u8217\'20\'19s top court.\par \par \uc2 The Republican-stacked Texas state supreme court sided with Paxton, blocking Cox from obtaining an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in her home state. Her attorneys have since announced that, after the protracted legal battle, Cox fled Texas to obtain the procedure out of state.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231212ejcc002jv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
