\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Texas supreme court temporarily blocks woman from  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  for non-viable pregnancy\b0\par\par\uc2 Richard Luscombe and agencies \par \uc2 567 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 December 2023\par 21:20 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dallas woman was earlier allowed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  by judge, even as state attorney general threatened to sue doctors who perform procedure\par \par \uc2 The Texas supreme court stepped in late on Friday to temporarily stay a judge\u8217\'20\'19s ruling {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/dec/07/texas-judge-abortion-ruling-non-viable-pregnancy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 approving an \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  for a woman whose fetus has a fatal diagnosis, the latest development in a protracted legal wrangle over one of the most restrictive bans in the nation.\par \par \uc2 Kate Cox, a 31-year-old mother of two from Dallas, had been granted a temporary restraining order from a lower court on Thursday, allowing her to proceed with an emergency procedure at 20 weeks.\par \par \uc2 But in a one-page ruling issued on Friday night, the supreme court announced it was staying the earlier ruling \u8220\'20\'1Cwithout regard to the merits\u8221\'20\'1D. It gave no indication when it would pick up the case again.\par \par \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , the Republican attorney general of Texas, argued that Cox did not meet the criteria for a medical exception to the state\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. \u8220\'20\'1CFuture criminal and civil proceedings cannot restore the life that is lost if plaintiffs or their agents proceed to perform and procure an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in violation of Texas law,\u8221\'20\'1D his office told the court.\par \par \uc2 Doctors have advised \uc2 Cox\uc2  the pregnancy is non-viable, and proceeding with it carried significant risks to her own future health.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, Paxton warned three hospitals in Houston that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/dec/08/ken-paxton-texas-abortion-kate-cox"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 they could face legal consequences}}}\uc2  if they allowed Cox\u8217\'20\'19s physician to provide the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , despite the ruling from state district judge Maya Guerra Gamble, whom Paxton called an \u8220\'20\'1Cactivist\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Her order, he wrote in a letter, \u8220\'20\'1Cwill not insulate hospitals, doctors or anyone else from civil and criminal liability\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Molly Duane\uc2 , an attorney at the \uc2 Center for Reproductive Rights\uc2 , which is representing Cox, said she was concerned about the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s intervention. \u8220\'20\'1CWhile we still hope that the court ultimately rejects the state\u8217\'20\'19s request, and does so quickly, in this case we fear that justice delayed will be justice denied,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Cox\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s attorneys have said they will not share her \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  plans, citing concerns for her safety. In a filing with the state supreme court on Friday, her attorneys indicated she was still pregnant.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit is believed to be the first of its kind since the landmark \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  decision last year {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/24/roe-v-wade-overturned-abortion-summary-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturning the Roe v Wade}}}\uc2  ruling that had provided federal protections for a woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for almost half a century.\par \par \uc2 Cox learned she was pregnant for a third time in August and was told weeks later that her baby was at a high risk for a condition known as trisomy 18, which has a very high likelihood of miscarriage or stillbirth and low survival rates, according to her lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 Doctors told Cox that if the baby\u8217\'20\'19s heartbeat stopped, inducing labor would carry a risk of a uterine rupture because of two earlier cesarean sections, and that another C-section at full term would would endanger her ability to carry another child.\par \par \uc2 In Kentucky on Friday, in a case with striking parallels, another woman {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/dec/08/kentucky-woman-abortion-ban-lawsuit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filed a lawsuit}}}\uc2  demanding the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The unidentified plaintiff is about eight weeks pregnant and is challenging the state\u8217\'20\'19s near-total ban on a class-action basis including other Kentuckians.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231211ejcb001mg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
