\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Texas judge rules woman with non-viable pregnancy can have an  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 Susan Rinkunas \par \uc2 971 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 December 2023\par 20:52 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Kate Cox sued state over ban after being unable to receive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care on learning her fetus had fatal diagnosis\par \par \uc2 A Texas woman with a non-viable pregnancy will be able to get an emergency \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a judge ruled on Thursday, in a lawsuit against the state that was filed after the woman was denied care because of the state\u8217\'20\'19s strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 Kate Cox, a 31-year-old mother of two in Dallas, learned last week that her fetus has a lethal diagnosis and had been unable to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite going to the emergency room three times with severe cramping and fluid loss. Her lawyers said in court that, in the two days since {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/dec/05/texas-woman-emergency-abortion-lawsuit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filing the lawsuit}}}\uc2 , Cox had to go to the ER a fourth time.\par \par \uc2 During the hearing, Cox\u8217\'20\'19s lawyers asked the Travis county judge Maya Guerra Gamble to issue a temporary restraining order to allow Cox\u8217\'20\'19s OB-GYN, Dr Damla Karsan, to perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  under the medical exception to the state bans.\par \par \uc2 Cox\uc2  had been told that her only options were to carry the pregnancy to term and have a C-section or, if the fetal heartbeat stopped, have labor induced. Both options could threaten her life and fertility: because she has had two previous C-sections, she would face a higher risk of uterine rupture or hysterectomy. In her suit, Cox requested an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedure known as dilation and evacuation (D&E).\par \par \uc2 In granting the order, Gamble said: \u8220\'20\'1CThe idea that Ms Cox wants desperately to be a parent and this law might actually cause her to lose that ability is shocking and would be a genuine miscarriage of justice.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Cox and her husband Justin attended the virtual hearing and Cox wiped away tears after the judge ruled. However, hours after the ruling, Texas attorney general \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2  issued a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texasattorneygeneral.gov/news/releases/attorney-general-ken-paxton-responds-travis-county-tro"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 statement}}}\uc2  warning doctors that Gamble\u8217\'20\'19s order would not insulate them from civil or criminal penalties under Texas\u8217\'20\'19s overlapping \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 The state is likely to appeal Gamble\u8217\'20\'19s order.\par \par \uc2 Cox\uc2  is 20 weeks pregnant and her fetus has trisomy 18, a fatal chromosomal condition, as well as other health issues, including a spinal abnormality. Hers is the first case in which a pregnant person has asked a court for an emergency \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since Roe v Wade was decided in 1973. She is being represented by the \uc2 Center for Reproductive Rights\uc2 , which is also suing the state on behalf of 20 women and two OB-GYNs, including Karsan, in a separate case called Zurawski v Texas.\par \par \uc2 Notably, in neither suit are the plaintiffs asking for the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban to be overturned \u8211\'20\'13 they are seeking clarification on the exceptions for emergency medical conditions.\par \par \uc2 Molly Duane\uc2 , an attorney with the \uc2 Center for Reproductive Rights\uc2 , argued that, in its filings against \uc2 Cox\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s suit, the state of Texas was second-guessing her physicians and saying that she was not sick enough.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe state says that Ms Cox has no injury, the harms to her health are not imminent but are \u8216\'20\'18purely hypothetical\u8217\'20\'19 and her legal claims are not yet ripe. These arguments are frankly stunning. The state goes as far to characterize her claims as \u8216\'20\'18a frivolous assertion of harm\u8217\'20\'19,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. Duane said the state\u8217\'20\'19s argument amounted to a patient having to be about to die before they can have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Stone, the lawyer representing Texas, said: \u8220\'20\'1CThe only party that\u8217\'20\'19s going to suffer an immediate and irreparable harm in this case if the court enters a [temporary restraining order] is the state.\u8221\'20\'1D He claimed this was because Texas would not get to make its legal arguments in a regular hearing.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  once performed is permanent and cannot be undone,\u8221\'20\'1D Stone said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe plaintiffs are going to obtain permanent relief in this case through this TRO application without any evidence being considered by this court and in full-blown evidentiary hearing.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Duane countered that claim with a searing response.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe state says that the only harm at issue in this motion is harm to the state and I would just note that the harm to Ms Cox\u8217\'20\'19s life, health and fertility are very much also permanent and cannot be undone,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Even before laying out the state\u8217\'20\'19s arguments, Stone attempted to raise questions about the Cox\u8217\'20\'19s petition being signed by a virtual notary in Florida rather than Texas. Duane said Texas suggested in its filings that the couple was on vacation in Florida, when in fact they have been at home because Cox felt so unwell and wanted to stay close to the emergency room.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe take notarized documents from other states all the time,\u8221\'20\'1D Gamble said to Stone, also noting that the couple attended the hearing and could attest in person. \u8220\'20\'1CI think we should focus on what\u8217\'20\'19s really going on.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Duane said in a statement after the hearing: \u8220\'20\'1CToday\u8217\'20\'19s decision underscores what we already know \u8211\'20\'13 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is essential healthcare. While we are grateful that Kate will be able to get this urgent medical care, it is unforgivable that she was forced to go to court to ask for it in the middle of a medical emergency.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMost women are not able to do what Kate has done \u8211\'20\'13 many Texans have been forced to continue pregnancies that put their lives at risk. That is happening every day across Texas. As long as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned, pregnant people will suffer. As we await the Texas supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s ruling in Zurawski v State of Texas, our hearts go out to the countless people in similar situations who \u8211\'20\'13 for so many reasons \u8211\'20\'13 aren\u8217\'20\'19t able to sue the state of Texas for an emergency hearing.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231207ejc7002e6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
