\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  clinics saw record surge in out-of-state patients post-Roe, report says\b0\par\par\uc2 Ava Sasani \par \uc2 667 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 December 2023\par 13:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 One in five patients crossed state lines to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2023, compared with one in 10 patients in 2020\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers witnessed a record surge in out-of-state patients since Roe v Wade was overturned last year, according to newly released data from the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 . The report, offering the first analysis of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related travel since the supreme court decision, revealed that one in five patients crossed state lines to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2023, compared with one in 10 patients in 2020.\par \par \uc2 As \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans have rippled across the country, providers in the states such as Illinois \u8211\'20\'13 where the procedure is protected by the state constitution \u8211\'20\'13 have been inundated with appointment requests. Illinois\u8217\'20\'19s clinics doubled the proportion of abortions provided to out-of-state patients, according to the Guttmacher report \u8211\'20\'13 in 2020, 21% of the patients who received \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care in Illinois came from out of state; in 2023, the figure jumps to 42%.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople will go to great lengths to access care, and those are the people we need to care for the most,\u8221\'20\'1D said Julie Burkhart, co-owner of Hope Clinic, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider in Granite City, Illinois. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now almost entirely banned in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2023/nov/10/state-abortion-laws-us"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 14 states}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Hope Clinic sits strategically close to Illinois\u8217\'20\'19s border with Missouri, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is prohibited. In the months after the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  stripped away national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections, the clinic\u8217\'20\'19s waiting room started to fill with patients who had driven from places like Tennessee and Arkansas.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not just Illinois and Missouri any more, we\u8217\'20\'19ve started seeing patients from across the deep south, people who have come from very far away to get care,\u8221\'20\'1D Burkhart told the Guardian.\par \par \uc2 The patchwork of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans enacted since Roe\u8217\'20\'19s demise means Trust Women, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic in Wichita, Kansas, is now the closest provider to patients in Houston, Texas. Kansas saw a 79% overall increase in abortions this year compared with 2020, with most of those abortions provided to out-of-state patients, according to Guttmacher.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a healthcare crisis that is entirely manufactured,\u8221\'20\'1D said Zack Gingrich-Gaylord, communications director of Trust Women. \u8220\'20\'1CNobody should be forced to travel 12 hours just to get essential healthcare.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Like Hope Clinic, the staff at Trust Women worked to ramp up the number of appointments offered each week, a desperate effort to keep pace with this year\u8217\'20\'19s spike in out-of-state patients. The Kansas clinic tripled its staff and stopped offering other reproductive health services, like contraceptives and gender-affirming care. Despite their best efforts, hundreds of patients are still turned away each week.\par \par \uc2 Providers like Burkhart worry that, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/dec/05/us-abortion-clinics-close-after-roe-overturned"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 as independent clinics are closing across the country,}}}\uc2  any additional legislative efforts to chip away access will make the resulting patient caseload unmanageable.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAnd we are all bracing ourselves for what might happen in Florida,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 The Florida supreme court is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/sep/08/florida-abortion-ban-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 currently reviewing}}}\uc2  the state\u8217\'20\'19s ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  past 15 weeks of pregnancy. If the state\u8217\'20\'19s high court upholds the 15-week ban under consideration, a separate, stricter law would take effect prohibiting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks, before many people know they are pregnant.\par \par \uc2 Five of the seven justices on the current state supreme court were selected by the conservative governor, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , fueling the concerns of Burkhart and other \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates.\par \par \uc2 A six-week ban would have dramatic effects outside the state, one of the last bastions of access in the south. In the first six months of 2023, nearly 4,000 people traveled to Florida to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to Guttmacher.\par \par \uc2 If the ban goes into effect, \u8220\'20\'1Cthose people who used to be able to get abortions in Florida are going to be forced to travel very far north, up the Eastern seaboard, past all the states with bans\u8221\'20\'1D, Burkhart said. \u8220\'20\'1CSome will probably travel to Illinois.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231207ejc7000me\par }\page {\par\fs20{