\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Texas woman files emergency lawsuit to terminate her non-viable pregnancy\b0\par\par\uc2 Susan Rinkunas \par \uc2 854 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 December 2023\par 23:23 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Kate Cox, a 31-year-old unable to get \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to multiple state bans, is first woman to ask court to allow procedure since Roe\par \par \uc2 A Texas woman filed an emergency lawsuit on Tuesday asking a judge to let her terminate her non-viable pregnancy. The woman recently learned of a lethal fetal diagnosis and her doctors said continuing the pregnancy threatens her life, health and future fertility \u8211\'20\'13 but she\u8217\'20\'19s been unable to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to Texas\u8217\'20\'19s multiple bans.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s the first time since the (now-overturned) Roe v Wade ruling in 1973 that an adult woman has asked a court to authorize an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 According to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://reproductiverights.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/12/Cox-v.-Texas-original-petition-FINAL.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 complaint}}}\uc2  filed in state court, Kate Cox is 20 weeks pregnant and has been to three different emergency rooms in the past month to be evaluated for severe cramping and fluid leaks. \uc2 Cox\uc2 , a 31-year-old mother of two, learned last week that her baby has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://medlineplus.gov/genetics/condition/trisomy-18/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 trisomy 18}}}\uc2 , a fatal chromosomal condition. Her doctors told her that, because she\u8217\'20\'19s had two previous cesarean sections, she faces a higher risk of uterine rupture and hysterectomy. Despite there being \u8220\'20\'1Cvirtually no chance that their baby would survive to birth or long afterwards\u8221\'20\'1D, she\u8217\'20\'19s been unable to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The complaint states that \u8220\'20\'1Cbecause of Texas\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, Ms Cox\u8217\'20\'19s physicians have informed her that their \u8216\'20\'18hands are tied\u8217\'20\'19 and she will have to wait until her baby dies inside her or carry the pregnancy to term, at which point she will be forced to have a third C-section, only to watch her baby suffer until death\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Her doctors said that if the baby\u8217\'20\'19s heartbeat stopped, they could induce labor \u8211\'20\'13 but they cannot perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedure known as dilation and evacuation (D&E). Due to her prior C-sections, inducing labor means she faces a higher risk of rupturing her uterus. A D&E would be the best medical option for her health, but doctors in the state who might provide it fear prosecution under Texas\u8217\'20\'19s criminal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. Cox and her husband, Justin, are asking a judge in Travis county, Texas, to let the couple get a D&E and allow Cox\u8217\'20\'19s OB-GYN, Dr Damla Karsan, to provide that care without the threat of prosecution by the Texas attorney general, \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , who is a defendant.\par \par \uc2 Cox is represented by lawyers from the \uc2 Center for Reproductive Rights\uc2 , and the suit seeks a temporary restraining order to block the state\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans from applying to her.\par \par \uc2 Separately, the legal organization is suing the state on behalf of 20 women and two OB-GYNs, including Karsan, in a case called Zurawski v Texas. The women in that case were denied abortions while experiencing dangerous pregnancy complications, and they asked the state court to block the bans for people facing medical emergencies. The Texas supreme court heard arguments in Zurawski last week and the plaintiffs are awaiting a ruling.\par \par \uc2 In Zurawski, the state argued that the 20 women didn\u8217\'20\'19t have standing to sue because they weren\u8217\'20\'19t actively seeking abortions. Assistant attorney general Beth Klusmann said last week that a woman seeking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2023/11/28/texas-supreme-court-abortion/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a health threat}}}\uc2  or {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2023/12/05/texas-abortion-lawsuit/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fetal anomaly}}}\uc2  would arguably have standing to ask the state to clarify the law.\par \par \uc2 The complaint says that while \uc2 Cox\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s life is not imminently at risk, the longer she stays pregnant, the higher the risks are to her health and fertility. She filed an emergency motion because she cannot wait for a decision in that case.\par \par \uc2 Cox said in a statement that, by filing the suit, she\u8217\'20\'19s trying to do what\u8217\'20\'19s best for both herself and her baby.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is not a matter of if I will have to say goodbye to my baby, but when,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CI do not want to continue the pain and suffering that has plagued this pregnancy. I do not want to put my body through the risks of continuing this pregnancy. I do not want my baby to arrive in this world only to watch her suffer. I need to end my pregnancy now so that I have the best chance for my health and a future pregnancy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Nancy Northup\uc2 , president and CEO of the \uc2 Center for Reproductive Rights\uc2 , said in a statement that Texas and other states with bans are putting people\u8217\'20\'19s lives at risk. She added that the court needed to move urgently: \u8220\'20\'1CWomen like Kate should not be forced to go to court to protect her health and preserve her future fertility.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The complaint noted the fortuitous timing that Cox heard of the existing challenge to Texas\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans on the very day she learned of the lethal fetal diagnosis.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBecause of the unusual confluence of events and the specific progression of her medical condition, Ms Cox both heard about the case and was able to reach out to plaintiffs\u8217\'20\'19 counsel for help,\u8221\'20\'1D it reads. \u8220\'20\'1CMost Texans will not be so lucky.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231205ejc500360\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
