\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Dozens of independent  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  clinics closed in 2023 post-Roe, study finds\b0\par\par\uc2 Ava Sasani \par \uc2 695 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 December 2023\par 16:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Twenty-three \u8216\'20\'18indie\u8217\'20\'19 clinics closed this year, leaving over a dozen states without a brick-and-mortar clinic that provides abortions\par \par \uc2 Dozens of independently owned reproductive health clinics shuttered in 2023, the year after the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade, according to a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abortioncarenetwork.org/communitiesneedclinics/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 new report}}}\uc2  from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Care Network.\par \par \uc2 The group found that 23 independently owned clinics closed this year, on top of the 42 that shuttered in 2022, leaving over a dozen states, mainly in the American south and midwest, without a single brick-and-mortar clinic that provides \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEven before Roe fell, we were the only \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic in a very rural, very underserved state with limited access to health care, and now that\u8217\'20\'19s all been exacerbated,\u8221\'20\'1D said Katie Quinonez-Alonzo, executive director of Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Center of West Virginia.\par \par \uc2 Like most independent clinics in the United States, the Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Center of West Virginia struggled to keep its doors open after the supreme court decision paved the way for the state to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year. The clinic still provides other reproductive and sexual health services, like gender-affirming care for transgender patients.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe want to stay here in our community and help the patients that are still counting on us, but it\u8217\'20\'19s been one uphill battle after another,\u8221\'20\'1D Quinonez-Alonzo told the Guardian.\par \par \uc2 The Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Center of West Virginia is an especially crucial lifeline for low-income, uninsured people in the state, who rely on the clinic for routine gynecological check-ups. Those services became harder to offer after West Virginia banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , slashing the clinic\u8217\'20\'19s revenue by roughly half a million dollars.\par \par \uc2 This year, Quinonez-Alonzo anticipates a roughly $350,000 budget deficit.\par \par \uc2 Independently owned clinics \u8211\'20\'13 in contrast with bigger players like \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 provide the majority of abortions in the United States. According to the ACN report, \u8220\'20\'1Cindie\u8221\'20\'1D clinics make up the majority of clinics operating in states that are most hostile to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and offer the broadest range of options for patients seeking the procedure. ACN researchers found that 73% of indie brick-and-mortar clinics offer both medical and surgical abortions, compared with just 42% of Planned Parenthood affiliates \u8211\'20\'13 so as they dwindle in number, so do options for women seeking care.\par \par \uc2 Before the supreme court overturned Roe, the West Alabama Women\u8217\'20\'19s Center provided over half of the abortions in the state.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn the deep south, it was always indie providers that were the ones providing abortions. Very few Planned Parenthoods existed in our region,\u8221\'20\'1D said Robin Marty, executive director of West Alabama Women\u8217\'20\'19s Center.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAlabama used to have three Planned Parenthoods, we have just one now, the others have closed,\u8221\'20\'1D Marty said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re still here, though.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 After Alabama enacted a sweeping ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Tuscaloosa clinic refocused on protecting newly pregnant people\u8217\'20\'19s access to affordable prenatal healthcare.\par \par \uc2 But Alabama is one of 10 states that has not expanded Medicaid, leaving roughly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.marchofdimes.org/peristats/data?reg=99&top=11&stop=158&lev=1&slev=4&obj=1&sreg=01"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one in seven women of childbearing age}}}\uc2  without any form of health insurance. The state allows newly pregnant women to apply for Medicaid, but that requires a doctor\u8217\'20\'19s letter confirming the pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBut of course, as these people do not have insurance, they can\u8217\'20\'19t get into a doctor in order to get this letter for Medicaid,\u8221\'20\'1D Marty said. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is why we\u8217\'20\'19re seeing so many people in Alabama who don\u8217\'20\'19t have prenatal care in the first trimester.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Even after a patient receives a doctor\u8217\'20\'19s letter confirming their pregnancy, it can take four to six weeks for the state to approve coverage. To help care for uninsured and pregnant people in Alabama, Marty said her clinic provides free prenatal care until a patient\u8217\'20\'19s Medicaid coverage is approved. If financial trouble forces the clinic to close, a bad maternal health landscape will get worse.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe people in our community need prenatal care and birth control and STI testing just as much as they need \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D Marty said. \u8220\'20\'1CFor these patients, there isn\u8217\'20\'19t another healthcare provider here for them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231205ejc5001s6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
