\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Moderate\u8217\'20\'19 or Roe v Wade killer: can Trump have it both ways on  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2 ?\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 1,076 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 December 2023\par 12:03 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The former president is proud of appointing the supreme court justices who overturned the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 now the issue is a vote loser for Republicans\par \par \uc2 A few months ago, the former president \uc2 Donald Trump{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2023/09/17/politics/donald-trump-abortion-2024/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 accused the Republican party}}}\uc2  of speaking \u8220\'20\'1Cvery inarticulately\u8221\'20\'1D on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And yet, for the GOP presidential frontrunner, inarticulateness seems to be a feature, not a bug, of his own approach to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Trump thinks he can run in 2024 as a \u8220\'20\'1Cmoderate\u8221\'20\'1D on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.rollingstone.com/politics/politics-features/donald-trump-abortion-moderate-run-2024-election-1234893936/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Rolling Stone reported}}}\uc2  this week \u8211\'20\'13 even though he\u8217\'20\'19s currently running ads in Iowa, a crucial state in the Republican primary, proclaiming himself \u8220\'20\'1Cthe most pro-life president ever\u8221\'20\'1D. It\u8217\'20\'19s a title to which Trump has a legitimate claim: his three nominees to the supreme court not only handed the nation\u8217\'20\'19s highest court a definitive conservative majority, but all three voted to overturn Roe v Wade in summer 2022.\par \par \uc2 That move handed the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement the victory of a lifetime, but Republicans have been paying for it ever since. They underperformed in both the 2022 midterms and the 2023 Virginia state elections, losses that have been widely credited to the party\u8217\'20\'19s inability to figure out a path forward on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates, meanwhile, won every \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related ballot measure of the last 18 months, even in red states. After Ohio, seemingly a conservative stronghold, voted to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in its state constitution earlier this month, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists rushed to remind Democrats that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/nov/08/abortion-winning-issue-rights-victories-2024-election"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  is a winning issue\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  in 2024.\par \par \uc2 While Republicans have flailed over how to message on an apparently toxic issue, Trump has \u8211\'20\'13 in typical Trump fashion \u8211\'20\'13 flip-flopped on it with apparent ease. Shortly after the 2022 midterms, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/3795054-trump-says-abortion-issue-responsible-for-gop-underperforming-expectations-in-midterms/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump blamed \u8220\'20\'1Cthe \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  issue\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  for Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 poor performance. He has refused to say whether he supports a federal ban and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/trump-desantis-abortion-ban-republican-primary-5bdbba55f9c2f328d49b5fbe9727677e"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 called the decision by Ron DeSantis, the Florida governor}}}\uc2 , to sign a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban a \u8220\'20\'1Cterrible thing\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 But all the while, Trump continues to take credit for overturning Roe.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI was able to kill Roe v Wade,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/donald-trump/trump-was-able-kill-roe-v-wade-rcna84897"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 he bragged on social media in May}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Howard Schweber, a professor of American politics and political theory at the \uc2 University of Wisconsin-Madison\uc2 , said that \u8220\'20\'1CTrump has what, in my experience of observing politics, seems like a nearly unique ability to maintain cognitive dissonance in ways that his supporters find untroubling.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHis supporters will say, \u8216\'20\'18Oh, well, he really means that when he says\u8217\'20\'19 \u8211\'20\'13 and then finish that sentence with whichever position they approve of. That\u8217\'20\'19s the gamble that he\u8217\'20\'19s taking,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Trump has not said what, if any, specific \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy he would support as president. DeSantis has said that he would support a 15-week national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, a position championed by the powerful anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group SBA Pro-Life America. Nikki Haley, the former governor of South Carolina, has said that she would {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/11/17/us/politics/nikki-haley-abortion-six-weeks.html#:~:text=While%20serving%20as%20governor%20in,any%20length%20passed%20by%20Congress."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sign an \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ban as president}}}\uc2 , but doubts that Republicans could muster the votes in Congress.\par \par \uc2 Iowa has a reputation for conservative evangelicalism, but {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/iowa-poll/2023/03/27/iowa-poll-most-iowans-back-legal-abortion-as-iowa-supreme-court-mulls-limits-roe-v-wade/69990037007/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 most Iowans believe}}}\uc2  that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or most cases. By not letting himself get nailed down on a specific \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, Trump might be approaching Iowa as though the presidential primary is already over, said Tim Hagle, a political science professor at the \uc2 University of Iowa\uc2 . (Which it very well be: Trump is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://projects.fivethirtyeight.com/polls/president-primary-r/2024/national/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 polling far higher}}}\uc2  than any of his competitors, who have largely cratered.) In a general election, where voters are more likely to be less dogmatic, it can pay to be vague \u8211\'20\'13 particularly on a charged issue like \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThings that you might say a little more forcefully during the nomination process during the primaries, you back off a little bit when it comes time to time for the general election,\u8221\'20\'1D Hagle said. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd that\u8217\'20\'19s been a strategy of candidates for decades.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Republicans in Iowa have launched an effort to amend the state constitution and clarify that it does not protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. In order for the amendment to show up on the ballot, the Republican-controlled state legislature would have to pass it before handing the measure to voters. That could backfire, increasing turnout among \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters who oppose Trump.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBut then we\u8217\'20\'19re also talking about turnout in the presidential year, which is high anyway,\u8221\'20\'1D Hagle said. \u8220\'20\'1CSo if you lose turnout in a midterm year, that\u8217\'20\'19s going to make more of a difference than in a presidential year.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/506759/broader-support-abortion-rights-continues-post-dobbs.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Most Americans oppose}}}\uc2  the overturning of Roe. But that doesn\u8217\'20\'19t mean voters are all that motivated by it: numerous polls since Roe\u8217\'20\'19s overturning have found that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/meet-the-press/meetthepressblog/poll-democracy-abortion-are-top-priorities-single-issue-voters-rcna126225"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Democrats are highly energized}}}\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while Republicans are less so \u8211\'20\'13 a reversal of the status quo while Roe was the law of the land.\par \par \uc2 As long as Trump wins the primary, he\u8217\'20\'19s in little danger of losing the conservative evangelicals who oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. While they may want him to be more forceful on the issue, it\u8217\'20\'19s improbable that they would turn to a Democrat in response to Trump\u8217\'20\'19s reticence.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSometimes the option is to not vote at all, but I can\u8217\'20\'19t imagine that they would want to do that either,\u8221\'20\'1D Hagle said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt does create a little heartburn on the part of the pro-life folks that supported him if all the sudden he\u8217\'20\'19s taking a more moderating position, but he may see that that\u8217\'20\'19s more appropriate given his electoral strategy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Even people who say that they would like to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cmostly legal\u8221\'20\'1D are not always that invested in doing so. A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/11/14/upshot/trump-abortion-republicans-2024-election.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 recent poll from the New York Times}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 which did not look at Iowa \u8211\'20\'13 found that, among voters who want \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be \u8220\'20\'1Cmostly legal\u8221\'20\'1D, Biden led by only one point. Those voters are also twice as likely to say they plan to vote based on economic issues, rather than social issues like \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Schweber, though, is convinced that there are would-be Trump voters who will defect solely based on their support of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWomen voters \u8211\'20\'13 particularly middle-class and upper-class, suburban women voters \u8211\'20\'13 do take \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights seriously,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. In 2016, Schweber said Republican women told him, \u8220\'20\'1CIt doesn\u8217\'20\'19t matter, they\u8217\'20\'19re never going to overrule Roe.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat sense of security is obviously gone,\u8221\'20\'1D he added.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231203ejc3000uq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
