\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 When does life end? A father didn\u8217\'20\'19t believe his daughter was dead. He says the hospital still harvested her organs\b0\par\par\uc2 Ann Neumann \par \uc2 7,596 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 December 2023\par 17:55 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 After Brittany O\u8217\'20\'19Connor was declared brain dead, her father clashed with medical authorities. The battle shines a light on America\u8217\'20\'19s legal definition of death \u8211\'20\'13 and the thousands waiting for organ donation\par \par \uc2 Mike O\u8217\'20\'19Connor had just been told that his daughter was brain dead.\par \par \uc2 Brittany had been in the hospital for six days. One end of a thick blue tube was taped to her mouth, the other connected to a respirator, which pushed air into her lungs with a mechanical force that shook her chest up and down.\par \par \uc2 Each day, he\u8217\'20\'19d come to her bedside to hope and pray she would open her eyes. And that\u8217\'20\'19s where he was, by her bed, when a policeman entered her room at the intensive care unit at Fresno community hospital.\par \par \uc2 Mike didn\u8217\'20\'19t believe that Brittany was dead. Forcefully, he told the care team that she was alive, that she was fighting, that they just wanted her heart, her lungs, her kidneys for donation. He feared that the hospital would remove her from life support and that Donor Network West would procure her organs.\par \par \uc2 He thought he was fighting for his daughter\u8217\'20\'19s life.\par \par \uc2 Mike believed the hospital and the organ procurement organization needed his consent. Yet the hospital had now called a police officer to remove him from Brittany\u8217\'20\'19s bedside, to remove him from the hospital premises.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CShe is without a doubt responding to my touch,\u8221\'20\'1D Mike told the officer.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey wanted me to pull the plug, but I said no.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Mike was given three minutes to say goodbye to his daughter.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 At the time of her death, Brittany was 26. She was the mother of two children, 10-year-old Lexi and four-year-old Lane. She was bright-eyed, funny, a little rebellious, and close to her sister, Kaylee. She did hair for friends at her home, giving them highlights and colors. But she also struggled with addiction for nearly a decade, getting clean at times and then falling back into use.\par \par \uc2 In the early morning hours of 17 November 2017, she was taken by ambulance to Fresno community hospital with hypoxia from an \u8220\'20\'1Cunknown duration\u8221\'20\'1D of asphyxiation.\par \par \uc2 Her live-in boyfriend reported that he had found her after an apparent suicide attempt. He called 911 as a neighbor gave Brittany CPR.\par \par \uc2 Mike wanted the officer \u8211\'20\'13 who, as he saw it, was charged to do what was right \u8211\'20\'13 to force the hospital\u8217\'20\'19s staff to hear his pleas.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBrain dead patients cannot respond to touch, and she clearly is \u8230\'20\'26 right?\u8221\'20\'1D Mike desperately said, touching Brittany\u8217\'20\'19s warm body. Brain dead patients cannot respond to external stimuli. \u8220\'20\'1CThat doctor came in and started talking to me about body parts and donating them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The police officer told Mike that he was a father too. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t want to take you to jail,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s the last thing I want to do.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m afraid they\u8217\'20\'19re going to kill her,\u8221\'20\'1D Mike told him. \u8220\'20\'1CThem pulling the plug on her now would be murder. And now I am worried about her safety.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 After three minutes, the officer escorted him out of the hospital.\par \par \uc2 Once home, Mike posted a message on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  asking his friends to call the hospital: \u8220\'20\'1CTELL THEM NOT TO PULL HER PLUG.\u8221\'20\'1D When he called the next day, he says, they refused to tell him where Brittany was.\par \par \uc2 Throughout the day, Mike posted a series of \uc2 Facebook\uc2  messages displaying escalating anxiety: the hospital was denying him information and access to his daughter, and then trying to kill her. Finally, he wrote: \u8220\'20\'1CTHEY WANT HER ORGANS!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 What Mike did not know is that, while he was begging friends to call the hospital and updating family about her health, doctors were already removing Brittany\u8217\'20\'19s heart, kidneys, liver, and lungs. The next day, her tissue was collected for donation.\par \par \uc2 No one from the hospital called Mike. He says he did not receive confirmation that Brittany was dead until three days later, when the coroner called to ask what Mike wanted to do with her remains.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 How we die is a deeply misunderstood process. Dying is not a momentary last breath after a serious illness or a tragic accident, but often the shutting down of a series of bodily systems, primarily the heart and lungs (circulation) and the brain (neurological function).\par \par \uc2 This has always been so, but in the 1960s medical advancements began to isolate and address circulation. These advancements include respirators that can mechanically force lungs to function, and defibrillators that can sometimes restart the heart. But there is still no way to resuscitate a human brain after its cells have ceased to function.\par \par \uc2 It was in 1967, when the first heart transplant was performed in South Africa, that the world of medicine realized the use of a dead person\u8217\'20\'19s organs to save the life of another. Patients without brain function, but whose heart and lungs were mechanically revived, were ideal organ donors; their organs were healthy because circulation (artificial or not) had maintained them.\par \par \uc2 The next year, a multidisciplinary team at \uc2 Harvard Medical School\uc2  convened to examine what it meant for a person to have heart and lung function, but not brain function. Their objective was twofold: to establish uniform criteria to determine when a brain had ceased to function; and to provide the burgeoning organ transplantation practice with a clear source of healthy organs.\par \par \uc2 Haider Warraich, a physician and professor at \uc2 Harvard Medical School\uc2 , wrote in his book Modern Death: \u8220\'20\'1CThe Harvard committee spoke as much to the legal community as it did to the medical, keenly aware of just how far behind the courts had been left by advances in medical resuscitative science.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Over the next decade, various US states had established their own legal and medical brain death precedents, often as the result of lawsuits. But from one state to another, the definition of death was not uniform, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.findlaw.com/healthcare/patient-rights/what-is-the-uniform-declaration-of-death-act-or-udda.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rendering it different}}}\uc2  in Wyoming, say, than it was in Kansas.\par \par \uc2 It wasn\u8217\'20\'19t until 1980, when President Jimmy Carter convened a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/presidents-commission-for-the-study-ethical-problems-medicine-and-biomedical-and-1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 taskforce}}}\uc2  that included theologians as well as doctors, historians, neurosurgeons and other experts, that a nationwide definition of death was established.\par \par \uc2 The Uniform Determination of Death Act (UDDA) offered {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.findlaw.com/healthcare/patient-rights/what-is-the-uniform-declaration-of-death-act-or-udda.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 two statutory definitions}}}\uc2  for when an individual is legally declared dead. There were now two ways to die: based on circulatory criteria (heart and lungs) and neurologic criteria (the brain).\par \par \uc2 In the latter, patients can not respond to external stimuli, like touch, but latent nervous system activity can be deceptive. Mike, for example, mistakenly thought that Brittany was responding to his touch. The body is warm, the patient breathing because circulation is often continued with medical assistance.\par \par \uc2 The UDDA was effectively adopted by all 50 states. Ultimately only one, New Jersey, offers a religious exemption, and three others \u8211\'20\'13 New York, California and Illinois \u8211\'20\'13 allow \u8220\'20\'1Creasonable accommodations\u8221\'20\'1D to families who require additional support in coping with the diagnosis.\par \par \uc2 It was apparent to the UDDA authors that clear guidelines were imperative to the public\u8217\'20\'19s comfort with organ donation; the act allowed the creation of today\u8217\'20\'19s organ procurement and transplantation system.\par \par \uc2 More than 40,000 lives a year are saved by organ donation. It is a revolutionary technology that has changed the medical system for the absolute good. It is also directly tied to the development of brain death criteria because, like Brittany, a vast majority of donors \u8211\'20\'13 more than 65% \u8211\'20\'13 are declared dead by neurological criteria, or brain death, before their organs are procured.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf anything, it is a relief not only for physicians but for families [of organ donors] when patients do meet the criteria for brain death,\u8221\'20\'1D Warraich wrote. Surveys show that these family members who choose donation find solace in their grief, knowing that their tragedy has extended the life of someone in need.\par \par \uc2 But for some who are mistrustful of or feel manipulated, bullied or disrespected by the medical institution they are at the mercy of, their loved one looks deceptively alive.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 After Brittany\u8217\'20\'19s death, things fell apart for Mike. Over the next two years, his life shut down: he gained 150lb and his alcoholism came rushing back. He was unemployed. Then his mother died. At one point he found himself homeless, living in his truck with his dog.\par \par \uc2 A family connection brought Mike to an experienced trial lawyer named Thornton Davidson. In 2018, they filed a lawsuit against Fresno community hospital and Donor Network West. It claimed that the hospital and organ procurement organization (OPO), \u8220\'20\'1Cwithout proper and effective legal authorization\u8221\'20\'1D, had \u8220\'20\'1Cperformed or assisted in harvesting organs and body parts from [Brittany]\u8217\'20\'19s dead body, and in doing so mutilated, desecrated, violated and outraged [Brittany]\u8217\'20\'19s human remains\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit claimed that the hospital and OPO had frozen Mike out of the decision-making process for Brittany\u8217\'20\'19s care, instead favoring Mike\u8217\'20\'19s ex-wife, Shawna, Brittany\u8217\'20\'19s mother. Mike said they did this as soon as they learned that he did not want her organs to be donated and that he believed she may still be alive.\par \par \uc2 (Counsel for both organizations declined to comment for this article.)\par \par \uc2 Brittany was an unmarried adult without adult children. The suit claimed that both organizations had colluded to violate the California Uniform Anatomical Gift Act by conspiring to obtain consent from only one parent instead of the legally required two, and that they made no \u8220\'20\'1Creasonable effort\u8221\'20\'1D to contact Mike for his consent.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit said the hospital and OPO were responsible for the intentional infliction of emotional distress on Mike.\par \par \uc2 In July 2019 the defendants, Fresno community hospital and Donor Network West, asked the court of Judge Rosemary McGuire to dismiss the intentional infliction of emotional distress clause from the suit. The court agreed.\par \par \uc2 To Mike and his lawyer, the court had made a mistake; Mike was \u8220\'20\'1Ccommitted to proving that the hospital and donor network had intentionally caused him harm \u8211\'20\'13 not just that they had done so by accident\u8221\'20\'1D, according to Davidson. They took their claim to the fifth district court of appeals.\par \par \uc2 On 31 January 2022, the court decided Mike\u8217\'20\'19s case could proceed. \u8220\'20\'1CWe conclude that O\u8217\'20\'19Connor\u8217\'20\'19s broader conception of extreme and outrageous conduct is correct,\u8221\'20\'1D the opinion said, \u8220\'20\'1Cand that his allegations of defendants\u8217\'20\'19 intentional conduct directed at him, and reckless conduct in his presence, are sufficient to state a cause of action for intentional infliction of emotional distress.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 For Mike and Davidson, the case doesn\u8217\'20\'19t rest on how Mike behaved while Brittany lay dying, nor is it about whether Brittany was dead or alive when Mike was escorted from her hospital room.\par \par \uc2 Instead, they say their suit exists to bring attention to how the altruism surrounding organ procurement too often allows hospitals and OPOs to flout the law.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 Organ procurement and transplant surgery are big business. In 2020, the cost of one heart transplant was more than $1.5m, a liver more than $800,000, a kidney nearly $500,000.\par \par \uc2 In March, the \uc2 US government{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/health/2023/03/22/transplant-system-overhaul-unos/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announced}}}\uc2  that it planned to break up the \uc2 United Network for Organ Sharing (UNOS)\uc2 , the congressionally created non-profit that has administered organ procurement in the US since 1984.\par \par \uc2 For years, patient advocates have railed against OPO behavior and operation, citing \uc2 UNOS\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s failure to meet the nation\u8217\'20\'19s needs.\par \par \uc2 The organization holds a $6.5bn annual federal contract \u8211\'20\'13 funds that largely come from fees patients pay to be added to the organ recipient waiting list. (About 20% of the total cost of transplant surgery is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://transplants.org/get-informed/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 required}}}\uc2  for waiting list registration.)\par \par \uc2 Hospitals are paid for the organ procurement organizations\u8217\'20\'19 use of their operating rooms and surgical resources. Donor Network West \u8211\'20\'13 which is overseen by \uc2 UNOS\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 received in excess of $81m in earnings the year after Brittany\u8217\'20\'19s death.\par \par \uc2 Some of the \uc2 UNOS\uc2  issues that have become too big for even the federal government to ignore include the woeful inadequacy of available organs to meet the needs of the country\u8217\'20\'19s ill. Each day, 22 people die while waiting for a donated organ. Nationally, more than 100,000 people are on a waiting list for hearts, lungs and other organs, a majority of them waiting for kidneys (a patient can wait for an average of 685 days to receive a kidney, 236 for a liver, and 213 for a heart).\par \par \uc2 In California alone, there are 23,000 patients on the list. The system is rife with inequality, with low-income and minority patients receiving fewer donations than their white counterparts. African American patients, for example, experience heart disease at a higher rate than white patients and are diagnosed with kidney disease four times as often, and yet they have a 10% lower likelihood of transplantation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHistorically, we\u8217\'20\'19ve excluded the poor,\u8221\'20\'1D Michele Goodwin, professor of constitutional law and global health policy at Georgetown Law School, told me about the US medical system. \u8220\'20\'1CHistorically, we have discriminated against people of color. So we\u8217\'20\'19ve not actually practiced inclusive medicine.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In 2018, Goodwin {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30584845/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote}}}\uc2  about a highly publicized case of a 13-year-old girl, Jahi McMath, who was declared brain dead by \uc2 Children\u8217\'20\'19s Hospital Oakland\uc2  in 2013 after a routine surgery for sleep apnea. Complications after surgery, including excessive blood loss, led to the hospital\u8217\'20\'19s determination. The family balked at the designation, staging a protest outside the hospital and a national media campaign.\par \par \uc2 McMath\u8217\'20\'19s family appealed to a non-profit organization that raised funds to move the young girl to New Jersey, where religious exemptions to the UDDA are legal. She was kept on organ support for nearly five years. All the while, her body grew and she began menstruating.\par \par \uc2 Goodwin wrote that McMath\u8217\'20\'19s family was protesting the treatment of their daughter and themselves as much as they were contesting the brain death diagnosis. After the surgery, the girl had begun to bleed excessively. When Nailah Winkfield, Jahi\u8217\'20\'19s mother, pointed it out to doctors and staff, she was ignored or told that it was normal.\par \par \uc2 Winkfield, who is Black, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2018/02/05/what-does-it-mean-to-die"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told}}}\uc2  the New Yorker in 2018: \u8220\'20\'1CNo one was listening to us, and I can\u8217\'20\'19t prove it, but I feel in my heart: if Jahi was a little white girl, I feel we would have gotten a little more help and attention.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Politics, too, plays a pronounced role in how medicine is delivered. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re in a very curious political moment,\u8221\'20\'1D Goodwin told me, \u8220\'20\'1Cthat\u8217\'20\'19s been influenced so deeply by religion in a way that would have been shocking and unheard of 50 years ago. As we think about how we understand birth, how we understand death, these are questions that now have a political-religious shroud over them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 Everyone agrees that a family meeting was held on 20 November, three days after Brittany was admitted to Fresno community hospital. Mike and Kaylee both say that everyone, including his ex-wife, Shawna, decided to give Brittany 30 days to fight. Mike was prepared to remove Brittany from life support after that date.\par \par \uc2 Both \u8220\'20\'1Cmother and father\u8221\'20\'1D are noted as \u8220\'20\'1Csurrogate decision-maker\u8221\'20\'1D in the medical records on the 20th. The day before, fearing the worst, Mike had set up a \uc2 GoFundMe\uc2  account to raise money for Brittany\u8217\'20\'19s funeral. (Shawna O\u8217\'20\'19Connor did not respond to repeated requests for an interview.)\par \par \uc2 But a note in the medical records, written two days after the meeting took place, says: \u8220\'20\'1CFamily meeting \u8230\'20\'26 showed parents with different opinions on management.\u8221\'20\'1D The note states that Shawna \u8220\'20\'1Cbelieves patient would not want to be kept in this condition because of continued suffering\u8221\'20\'1D, and says that Shawna and Brittany \u8220\'20\'1Cwatched the Terry Shivo [sic] case together and they stated none of them would want to [be] kept alive in that condition\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Terri Schiavo\u8217\'20\'19s case has become a touchstone for families who are faced with brain injuries that render a patient unconscious, unable to make their own medical decisions. It remains a landmark in social awareness of artificial organ support, consent, and guardianship. And it highlighted the role that religion plays in medical care provisions: the US Conference of Catholic Bishops, which governs the more than 600 Catholic hospitals in the country, changed its rules after the case. In Catholic hospitals, artificial nutrition and hydration is now \u8220\'20\'1Ccomfort care\u8221\'20\'1D, according to the organization\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.chausa.org/ethics/ethical-and-religious-directives"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ethical and religious directives}}}\uc2 . Discretion to remove it is left to the hospital director, not the family. Enforcement of this hierarchy is up to the local bishop.\par \par \uc2 At noon the day after Thanksgiving, six days after Brittany was admitted, Mike called to speak with the doctor (\u8220\'20\'1CDr. Harp NEVER called me,\u8221\'20\'1D Mike wrote on a copy of the medical records he received after Brittany\u8217\'20\'19s death) and was told to come to the hospital.\par \par \uc2 What followed, everyone agrees, was a series of interactions that involved a lot of yelling. Mike told hospital staff that he would \u8220\'20\'1Cshove the law so far down your throats that you\u8217\'20\'19re the ones who will need the tube\u8221\'20\'1D. The doctor wrote that Mike \u8220\'20\'1Crefused to believe that she was dead\u8221\'20\'1D and a staff member wrote that Mike was \u8220\'20\'1Cverbally threatening and aggressive\u8221\'20\'1D, stating that \u8220\'20\'1Cpeople in far worse conditions have come back\u8221\'20\'1D and that he was not going to allow Brittany to be removed from life support \u8220\'20\'1Cbecause my faith has told me she is going to make it through this. I\u8217\'20\'19m not letting her die.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 At 4.18pm, the medical records state, the Fresno police department came to the unit at the behest of the hospital.\par \par \uc2 Dr Lynn Burnett, who worked for Fresno community hospital and is now deceased, wrote an extensive explanation the same day, 24 November, for why Mike should have no say in Brittany\u8217\'20\'19s care: \u8220\'20\'1CIn this case, the Patient\u8217\'20\'19s Father has voiced Religious objections to discontinuation of physiologic support. His objection, however DOES NOT pertain to religious practices, but rather, to the medical determination of Death by Neurological Criteria.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Crucially, he then wrote: \u8220\'20\'1CGiven these events, it is Ethically Appropriate NOT to tell the Patient\u8217\'20\'19s Father about the plan for organ recovery until after such has been accomplished.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A note in the medical record states that \u8220\'20\'1Corgan donation was never brought up to the father by any hospital staff\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The day after Brittany\u8217\'20\'19s funeral, which Shawna did not attend, Mike visited his ex-wife with a camera. She appeared tall and lean in the video, wearing a black shirt and pants. Her hair was short, blond and tousled. She was looking at Mike, not the camera. \u8220\'20\'1CThere was a discrepancy\u8230\'20\'26 I did not want a donation and Shawna knew that,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWell, yeah, I knew that,\u8221\'20\'1D Shawna said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey just kept pushing me and they would push me and they got us separated. They took me into a room at one point and said that I was the logical one and that they were no longer going to deal with you at all.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 Fresno, in the center of the San Joaquin Valley and the state, is home to nearly 1 million people. The sprawling city\u8217\'20\'19s backdrop, when the air is clear, is the white-capped peaks of the Sierra Nevada mountains. Windswept fields surround the city. Clouds of white blossoms adorned acres of almond, apple and plum trees when I was there.\par \par \uc2 Mike, Brittany, and Kaylee have always lived in Fresno \u8211\'20\'13 and all four have struggled with the addiction, health issues, and underemployment that are endemic to the city.\par \par \uc2 Fresno is characterized foremost by agriculture, land development, and disparity \u8211\'20\'13 economic, health, educational, racial. The haves traditionally owned and developed the valley\u8217\'20\'19s land and the have-nots farmed and built it.\par \par \uc2 Perennially on the list of cities with the highest rates of extreme poverty, social inequality, drug use, suicides and overdoses, Fresno has been plagued with poor health outcomes for decades.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COld mayors, new mayors have called it \u8216\'20\'18a tale of two cities\u8217\'20\'19, but no mayor has been able to make the divide less stark,\u8221\'20\'1D Mark Arax, a Fresno native, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/06/01/magazine/fresno-politics-poverty.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote about the city}}}\uc2  in the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  last summer. \u8220\'20\'1CIn the span of 15 miles, from the wealthy subdivisions and megachurches of the Northside to the meth-fueled hustle of the Southside, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://unequalfrombirth.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 life expectancy drops 20 years}}}\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Even if it\u8217\'20\'19s one out of a hundred chances that your daughter is coming back \u8211\'20\'13 what father wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t take those odds?\par \par \uc2 Mike O\u8217\'20\'19Connor\par \par \uc2 On Valentine\u8217\'20\'19s Day last spring, I pulled into the dusty driveway of the sober ranch where Mike O\u8217\'20\'19Connor had been living for nearly 12 months, working as a house manager. He\u8217\'20\'19d been sober for several years but found the work rewarding.\par \par \uc2 The ranch was on the outskirts of Fresno, a vast canopy of orchards lined the surrounding fields. A dry wind pinwheeled the leaves on the palm trees behind the house.\par \par \uc2 Mike and his lawyer, Thornton Davidson, were scheduled to attend a mandatory settlement meeting in a week, and I wanted to see them before any accompanying non-disclosure agreement prevented them from talking about the case. (They chose not to settle.)\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMy main problem is,\u8221\'20\'1D Mike said, \u8220\'20\'1Cit\u8217\'20\'19s a hospital where you\u8217\'20\'19re supposed to try to save people. They weren\u8217\'20\'19t trying to save her. They were trying to preserve her.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBrain dead doesn\u8217\'20\'19t mean soul dead,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CSo, in my opinion, even if it\u8217\'20\'19s one out of a hundred chances, which is what they told me, that your daughter is coming back \u8230\'20\'26 what father wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t take those odds?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He said the hospital and organ procurement staff had befriended Shawna. \u8220\'20\'1CIf they wanted to comfort her and make her feel great, great. She needed that at that time. But they didn\u8217\'20\'19t do it out of love. They did it for money. They did it because they wanted body parts.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Even if Mike felt that his daughter had had enough time to fight, he would never have consented to organ donation because Brittany had told him she did not want to be a donor.\par \par \uc2 When she was eight or nine, Mike said, they saw a TV commercial for organ donation. Brittany asked what it was and after Mike told her, they agreed that \u8220\'20\'1Cwe, neither of us, wanted to be donors\u8221\'20\'1D, Mike said. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd one of the main reasons was, she always said: \u8216\'20\'18My heart is your heart, Dad.\u8217\'20\'19 And I said: \u8216\'20\'18My heart\u8217\'20\'19s yours too, don\u8217\'20\'19t ever let them take mine.\u8217\'20\'19 We wanted to be buried intact, we didn\u8217\'20\'19t want to be butchered.\u8221\'20\'1D The phrase became an expression of familial love and commitment between them: \u8220\'20\'1CMy heart is your heart.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Mike had a specific reason to oppose organ donation. For four months, after he was discharged from the navy, where he served as an EMT, he worked as a procurement technician for California Blood Bank, removing organs, bone grafts, and tissue from cadavers for donation and research. \u8220\'20\'1COne of the reasons I\u8217\'20\'19m so against it is the way it\u8217\'20\'19s done. It\u8217\'20\'19s brutal. I\u8217\'20\'19ve literally opened up people and taken out not just bone grafts but the entire joint.\u8221\'20\'1D We noted that we\u8217\'20\'19d both had ACL replacements in our left knees, where the torn ligament is replaced with one from a cadaver, mine a few months ago, Mike\u8217\'20\'19s 30 years ago, before the navy.\par \par \uc2 It was during his time at the blood bank that he changed his mind about donation. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s when I found out what donation was all about,\u8221\'20\'1D Mike said, referring to the procurement process. \u8220\'20\'1CYou just keep the heart alive. You keep your blood running through and they\u8217\'20\'19re on a cold slab in a cold room. Somebody comes in and takes the kidneys. And the heart specialist, not coming for eight hours. You just lay there.\u8221\'20\'1D Mike grew distant as he talked, as if imagining what Brittany\u8217\'20\'19s body was exposed to.\par \par \uc2 Mike doesn\u8217\'20\'19t oppose donation for others; he\u8217\'20\'19s just seen too much for him to want it for himself. And Brittany agreed.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhy didn\u8217\'20\'19t the hospital talk to you about Brittany\u8217\'20\'19s diagnosis, organ donation, her death?\u8221\'20\'1D I asked him the next day, when I picked him up. We planned to drive to locations around Fresno that were meaningful to him, including the house where he grew up.\par \par \uc2 We were sitting in the parking lot of a distinctly California strip mall. On our right were the beige stucco walls of a nondescript drug testing office where Mike had once worked.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSeems to me they just weren\u8217\'20\'19t willing to. They had some kind of preconceived notion about me,\u8221\'20\'1D Mike said. Instead of staff explaining what was happening, he said, he remembers them repeating \u8220\'20\'1Cbrain dead \u8221\'20\'1D, assuming he knew what that meant. \u8220\'20\'1CThey used the term to shut down any conversation,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s supposed to be a choice. If you\u8217\'20\'19re not there to make that choice, the person closest to you is supposed to make that decision for you,\u8221\'20\'1D he said of organ donation, \u8220\'20\'1Cand that\u8217\'20\'19s what was taken from me.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit was a way to teach Fresno community hospital and Donor Network West a lesson, sure. But what motivated Mike was preventing what happened to him and to Brittany from happening to anyone else, he said.\par \par \uc2 We sat quietly in my white rental car for a moment. \u8220\'20\'1CThey wanted her organs. They stopped [talking to me] because it was the easiest, fastest way of getting the organs,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey gave it all away. Everything. And I have thought about it every day for five years, trying to find another reason, so that I could be OK.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat is it you come back to again and again?\u8221\'20\'1D I asked.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CCould I have saved her? Was she really dead? Am I gonna pay for this in the long run? When I get to heaven, is God gonna say\u8230\'20\'26.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSo it\u8217\'20\'19s made you question your own salvation?\u8221\'20\'1D I asked.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIs it a mortal sin? Could it be considered murder?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou think you\u8217\'20\'19re responsible for your daughter\u8217\'20\'19s death?\u8221\'20\'1D I asked.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t know. I think I might be. A lot of times I wish I was anybody but myself.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 Every expert I spoke with about the Uniform Determination of Death Act expressed their concern for the protection and improvement of organ donation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe biggest thing at stake is public trust in medicine and in transplantation,\u8221\'20\'1D Brendan Parent, a professor of population health and surgery at New York University\u8217\'20\'19s Grossman School of Medicine, told me. \u8220\'20\'1CThe framework we have created for donation is that we all get to decide what happens to our bodies after we die,\u8221\'20\'1D Parent said. \u8220\'20\'1COrgans are gifts and we need permission.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It is because they are gifts that the system needs to be transparent, just, and clear to the public, he told me. Any actual or perceived injustice, either in how brain death is diagnosed or how organs are procured, can harm an organ donation system already under great pressure.\par \par \uc2 The idea that brain death was created to facilitate organ procurement means to some groups that \u8220\'20\'1Csomething nefarious is going on\u8221\'20\'1D, Parent said. Fear of being an organ donor persists often because many believe that healthcare professionals will not try to save a designated donor\u8217\'20\'19s life.\par \par \uc2 The fear can be rooted in medicine\u8217\'20\'19s very real unjust and paternalistic history. \u8220\'20\'1CWe can\u8217\'20\'19t just say that it\u8217\'20\'19s a myth,\u8221\'20\'1D Parent said, referring to the sterilizations, unauthorized testing, and neglect of disabled people and people of color that have plagued modern medicine. The Tuskegee syphilis experiments are one example. But the fear is also rooted, he said, in \u8220\'20\'1Cthe pressures of the organ donation system\u8221\'20\'1D, amid a dire shortage of viable organs.\par \par \uc2 Parent\u8217\'20\'19s concern is that any effort to discredit the experience of the loved ones, like Mike, who might be traumatized by how organs are procured could lead to further erosion of public trust. He said understanding and listening to questioning families can only improve the entire organ procurement system.\par \par \uc2 Goodwin reiterated a need to strengthen the organ donation process, for the sake of the many thousands of people on the waiting list. The best way to do so, in her estimation, is to make healthcare more equitable. \u8220\'20\'1CMore inclusive medicine actually would mean that we were more efficacious overall,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Thaddeus Pope, a lawyer and professor at Mitchell Hamline School of Law in Minnesota, has been advocating for the revision of the UDDA for several years, urging the Uniform Law Commission \u8211\'20\'13 the national non-profit that works to make state laws as uniform as possible for legal consistency \u8211\'20\'13 to take up revision of the criteria used to determine brain death. His objective, and that of his colleagues and co-authors, he said, has been to improve equality and the number of organs donated. (I first learned about Brittany\u8217\'20\'19s case from Pope\u8217\'20\'19s website; at the request of Mike\u8217\'20\'19s lawyer, Pope has since become a designated expert witness on the issue of ethics for the case.)\par \par \uc2 I met with Wesley Ely, co-director of the critical illness, brain dysfunction, survivorship (CIBS) center at \uc2 Vanderbilt University Medical Center\uc2 , in the vaulted, book-lined anteroom of the \uc2 National Academy of Sciences\uc2  in Washington DC in May. \u8220\'20\'1CI think it\u8217\'20\'19s important to optimize organs for the patients who are waiting, but we can\u8217\'20\'19t do that at the expense of our ethical and moral values,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. He told me plainly that he believes in brain death as currently laid out in the UDDA. But he acknowledged that it was difficult for some families to understand the diagnosis. He also sympathizes with patients who are hesitant donors.\par \par \uc2 Ely said that he, as the former medical director of a lung transplant program and a longtime practicing critical care doctor, has been faced with explaining donation to families after brain death. He emphasized that it was possible to explain the process to patients\u8217\'20\'19 hesitant loved ones: \u8220\'20\'1CIf you take time, hold their hand, walk them through the notion of why the body can\u8217\'20\'19t live a meaningful life, then they can begin to understand that if it was the patient\u8217\'20\'19s preference, this should be consented to.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It is a weird experience to walk in, see someone\u8217\'20\'19s cavity opened and organs taken out. And I don\u8217\'20\'19t think we should ever get over that\par \par \uc2 Dr Wesley Ely\par \par \uc2 Still, organ procurement is an emotionally complex process. \u8220\'20\'1CIt is a very weird experience to walk in, see someone\u8217\'20\'19s cavity opened, and all these organs taken out of it. And I don\u8217\'20\'19t think we should ever get over that,\u8221\'20\'1D Ely said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere is a beauty to giving organs to another person. But it\u8217\'20\'19s not for everybody.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 On the phone, Brittany\u8217\'20\'19s sister, Kaylee, has a bright, tinkling voice. She is the mother of two children, seven-year-old Tommy and six-month-old Karter.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t know why it was such a thing with her, but she always said that organ donation was a scam,\u8221\'20\'1D she told me about Brittany, who was seven years older. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd I was like, \u8216\'20\'18What do you mean? You\u8217\'20\'19re selfish.\u8217\'20\'19 It\u8217\'20\'19s crazy how she never wanted [organ donation], and that would end up killing her.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 When I asked why she described her father as the only \u8220\'20\'1Creasonably minded\u8221\'20\'1D parent, she said that her mother was very sick. \u8220\'20\'1CShe\u8217\'20\'19s been addicted my entire life. But then there\u8217\'20\'19s mental illness. It\u8217\'20\'19s been really hard to grow up with that. It was really hard on Brittany.\u8221\'20\'1D In many ways, she says, it was Brittany who raised her.\par \par \uc2 When I told Kaylee that Mike believed Shawna had been coerced by the hospital staff, she agreed. I asked her why. \u8220\'20\'1COrgans are valuable and Brittany was only 26, my age right now. There\u8217\'20\'19s definitely not enough organs for everybody in the world,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 She also thinks the EMTs, the hospital, the police and the organ donation staff looked down on Brittany and her father. The police knew the house Brittany was living in. There was addiction. Her father was overweight. \u8220\'20\'1CI felt like they thought we were some white trash, you know, some low-income family. I mean, who\u8217\'20\'19s gonna fight for her?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Kaylee is heartbroken. \u8220\'20\'1CI was 20 years old and just out of my mind about it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 To this day, she doesn\u8217\'20\'19t know who\u8217\'20\'19s to blame for her sister\u8217\'20\'19s death and donation. But she now knows that she\u8217\'20\'19ll never donate her organs.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 Current tests to evaluate brain death include putting ice water in the ear, removing the patient from a respirator, and shining light in the eye; the goal is to demonstrate that there\u8217\'20\'19s no response to external stimulation, no brainstem reflexes, and no ability to breathe without assistance. No patient who has been accurately diagnosed as brain dead has ever recovered.\par \par \uc2 The UDDA, which stipulates criteria for brain death but does not state specific tests that should be used, notes that doctors rely on \u8220\'20\'1Caccepted medical standards\u8221\'20\'1D. Doctors have been trusted to use the necessary criteria to make their determinations.\par \par \uc2 But after nearly 40 years of relative stability, the UDDA has come under scrutiny.\par \par \uc2 Several high-profile cases like Brittany\u8217\'20\'19s, in the US and abroad, have rallied families, allies, and religious groups to argue that brain dead patients are actually not dead. The case of Jahi McMath, the young girl who remained on organ support for five years despite a brain death designation, was watched by the medical community with great attention; it upended the understanding that the bodies of the brain dead would soon begin to decompose. (Current testing does not examine the functioning of the pituitary gland or the hypothalamus, parts of the brain that kept McMath\u8217\'20\'19s body from decomposing.)\par \par \uc2 Lawyers, doctors and bioethicists began to call for a revision of the brain death designation to bring bedside testing in line with the UDDA, to make it more uniform, to increase clinicians\u8217\'20\'19 confidence and the public\u8217\'20\'19s awareness of what brain death is. The hope was to save families and healthcare providers from the doubt and distrust that a lack of uniform criteria can garner \u8211\'20\'13 and to save everyone the financial and emotional destruction of lawsuits.\par \par \uc2 The Uniform Law Commission\u8217\'20\'19s early meetings proved contentious, revealing a wide array of ideas about what changes should be made to the definition of brain death, some suggesting more rigorous testing, others less. Still others proposed leaving the old standards in place. Earlier this year, a draft introduced at a meeting in Hawaii included a universal religious exemption and more clinician discretion.\par \par \uc2 The lack of consensus among committee members caused ULC to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8967425/#:~:text=The%20Uniform%20Law%20Commission%20recently,424%20%5B2017%5D%2C%20Okla."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 postpone}}}\uc2  their projected date for recommended legislation, originally set for 2023, an unprecedented additional year.\par \par \uc2 Cases like McMath\u8217\'20\'19s may be few but their attending publicity \u8211\'20\'13 whether in legacy media or across religious or rightwing channels \u8211\'20\'13 influences millions and can undermine established medical paradigms. And the impact that the UDDA has on transplantation is enormous.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 When life ends is particularly contentious in an environment where half the population\u8217\'20\'19s medical rights are held hostage by the politics of when life begins.\par \par \uc2 The conservative lawyer James Bopp Jr is most {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/07/07/podcasts/the-daily/anti-abortion-roe-v-wade.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 responsible}}}\uc2  for the legal assault on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access that has taken place since the passage of Roe v Wade in 1973, and for the decision being overturned in the summer of 2022. He was at the forefront of litigation that led to the institution of waiting periods, age limits, and notification laws that incrementally limited who could receive an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , where and when.\par \par \uc2 But it is his current work on the Uniform Law Commission\u8217\'20\'19s review of the UDDA that has brought him renewed media attention.\par \par \uc2 When I spoke to Bopp this summer, he was genial and candid. Publicly he\u8217\'20\'19s been clear that he sees \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the debate surrounding brain death to be on the same continuum because, from the moment of conception, the fetus \u8220\'20\'1Cdoes not yet exercise functions we identify as human\u8221\'20\'1D. Neither does the brain dead patient, and therefore, like the fetus, according to Bopp, they must be protected.\par \par \uc2 He told me that he agreed in the 1980s with the UDDA\u8217\'20\'19s criteria for brain death, and, as general counsel to the \uc2 National Right to Life Committee\uc2 , a non-profit anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group with chapters in all 50 states, he urged the organization to support it.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey asked me, \u8216\'20\'18Does this meet our criteria that every human life has inherent value from fertilization to natural death? Does whole brain [death] determine natural biological death?\u8217\'20\'19 I said yes.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But to Bopp and his allies, new information has emerged: testing for death of the \u8220\'20\'1Cwhole brain, including the brain stem\u8221\'20\'1D, as stated in the UDDA, is not (and never has been) consistently conducted throughout the country. And cases like that of McMath have shown that other brain functions not addressed by existing testing, such as those of the hypothalamus and pituitary, may continue after the death of the rest of the brain.\par \par \uc2 Bopp\u8217\'20\'19s concern is that biological death may not occur at the time that all brain function ceases. Others on the Uniform Law Commission, where he is a member of the drafting committee, have focused instead, he told me, on consciousness. Most families, when they understand that their loved one will never be conscious again, decide to remove organ support, or \u8220\'20\'1Cpull the plug\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 But Bopp thinks this is unjust. Unlike those who see consciousness as essential to life, patients like McMath, and perhaps Brittany O\u8217\'20\'19Connor, in the eyes of Bopp and other \u8220\'20\'1Cpro-life\u8221\'20\'1D advocates, are disabled. Severely disabled, yes, but too often \u8220\'20\'1Cdirectly targeted\u8221\'20\'1D by those with a \u8220\'20\'1Crelative value of human life\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 What Bopp calls integrated function, Joseph Eble, a physician and president of the Tulsa, Oklahoma, Catholic Medical Association, calls the soul.\par \par \uc2 A prolific writer for Catholic publications, Eble has appeared on a host of Catholic TV shows, podcasts, and radio shows to talk about his interpretation of brain death. \u8220\'20\'1CThe human person is the substantial union of the body and soul,\u8221\'20\'1D Eble said on the St Philip Institute Podcast last year. \u8220\'20\'1CThe soul is the principle of integration of the body. Its absence is evident, despite its invisibility. When the body is no longer integrated, [it is in] a state that is indicated by decomposition.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Hugh Brown, executive vice-president of the American Life League, a Catholic non-profit with a $3.5m annual budget, recently weighed in on brain death on an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Catholic {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://relevantradio.com/2023/08/why-redefine-brain-dead/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 podcast}}}\uc2  hosted on more than 200 channels of Relevant Radio, a Catholic network. Brown told the host that protecting those with a brain death diagnosis was as important as protecting what he calls the unborn because \u8220\'20\'1Cduring the first six weeks of pregnancy, our body lives without a brain\u8221\'20\'1D. Brown\u8217\'20\'19s belief is that the brain is not necessary for human life.\par \par \uc2 It might be easy for some of us to shrug at Brown\u8217\'20\'19s definition of life, or Eble\u8217\'20\'19s formulation of body-soul integration, but similar ideas about brain death exist within today\u8217\'20\'19s medical journals.\par \par \uc2 Alan Shewmon, a pediatrician and professor emeritus at the \uc2 University of California, Los Angeles\uc2 , has argued against the brain death designation for decades, becoming a hero with credibility to many on the religious right. In 1998, he published a study that claims a significant number of brain dead patients retained enough blood flow to the brain to prevent its deterioration. Shewmon advised McMath\u8217\'20\'19s family, finding that McMath had what he calls \u8220\'20\'1Cchronic brain death\u8221\'20\'1D, not a terminal ailment.\par \par \uc2 When I asked Bopp what would happen if every hospital in the US were forced to comply with his formulation of brain death, keeping brain dead patients on organ support indefinitely, he chided me for putting a price on human life. And yet, when quality healthcare is a luxury in the US, when only those who can afford organ transplants get them, it is evident that we already do.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 Thornton Davidson, Mike\u8217\'20\'19s lawyer, is surprisingly accessible thanks to a puckish, wry wit. We met in the conference room of his modest office on Shaw Street in Fresno the day after I visited with Mike.\par \par \uc2 We were discussing the \u8220\'20\'1Ctrolley problem\u8221\'20\'1D, a thought experiment that goes like this: there is a runaway trolley on a track. You are a bystander and see that a nearby switch would allow you to divert the trolley from its current course, which will undoubtedly kill five people. But if you divert the trolley, the one person on the alternate track will surely be killed. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat are you gonna do?\u8221\'20\'1D Davidson asked. \u8220\'20\'1CYou have a choice: kill one person to save five? They weren\u8217\'20\'19t going to kill [Brittany], but the argument would be: give up the organs and save five people or bury her with her organs, which will rot.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Davidson waited a beat. \u8220\'20\'1CBut that\u8217\'20\'19s not what the case is about.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat\u8217\'20\'19s the case about?\u8221\'20\'1D I dutifully asked.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLove,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe legislature,\u8221\'20\'1D he said, \u8220\'20\'1Cmade it so that people who were the closest, who had the closest bond, had the authority to make this decision in the absence of what the person dictated.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBut the law also anticipated that where you had more than one person, you could have a conflict. And so the law doubled down on the protection of that familial relationship. And it said, \u8216\'20\'18It\u8217\'20\'19s got to be a majority.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In Brittany\u8217\'20\'19s case, the majority should have been both Mike and Shawna, Brittany\u8217\'20\'19s parents.\par \par \uc2 In this way the legislature created a \u8220\'20\'1Cfailsafe\u8221\'20\'1D, so that if there was not unanimity among the parties, the organs would not be removed from the body.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s why the case is about love, because this is one of the rare instances where the law protects that familial relationship.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The hospital and Donor Network West might say that five lives are ethically more valuable than one. They might say Mike wasn\u8217\'20\'19t \u8220\'20\'1Creasonably available\u8221\'20\'1D, despite having him escorted off the premises by a policeman, that he was a threatening voice at his daughter\u8217\'20\'19s bedside. They might even say that it was up to Shawna to notify Mike of her decision. But what matters to Mike, and probably to the judge who will preside over the trial, is what the law says.\par \par \uc2 In the third week of September, a trial judge threw Donor Network West out of the lawsuit, but kept Fresno community hospital. According to Davidson, this showed how the courts \u8220\'20\'1Cbend over backwards to protect the organ procurement industry\u8221\'20\'1D and allow them to flout the law.\par \par \uc2 Davidson planned to continue with the trial against the hospital, and says he\u8217\'20\'19s not given up on holding Donor Network West accountable.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 Mike and I drove east to the cemetery in Clovis, California, where Brittany is buried. Cedar trees line the open field and colorful plastic flowers dot the stones, which are flush with the dry ground. Nearby the 168 bends east before heading north, out of town, and into the Sierra national forest.\par \par \uc2 Mike told me that he would like a clause added to the California Uniform Anatomical Gift Act that further codifies unanimous agreement for organ donation among decision-makers. Brittany\u8217\'20\'19s clause, he imagines. \u8220\'20\'1CThat would be kind of cool, to have my daughter\u8217\'20\'19s name on it for as long as there is a clause, for as long as there is donation. She\u8217\'20\'19ll be remembered for ever.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In the last week of September, the Uniform Law Commission paused its review of the UDDA. \u8220\'20\'1CThe result of this pause is that, although we will continue to hope mid-level principles will become apparent,\u8221\'20\'1D the email, sent to the commission\u8217\'20\'19s members and observers, stated, \u8220\'20\'1Cno further drafting committee meetings will be scheduled at this time.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 I followed Mike to Brittany\u8217\'20\'19s stone and watched him dust a bit of dirt and grass from it. He designed the marker: a teal butterfly is on either side, just below Brittany\u8217\'20\'19s name. Between them, there are two hearts, one with her picture and the other with the words \u8220\'20\'1CWe find the trees behind the trees\u8221\'20\'1D, the lyrics to a song he and Brittany made up when she was young.\par \par \uc2 Across the bottom the marker reads: \u8220\'20\'1CLoved By All.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 In the US, you can call or text the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://988lifeline.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 National Suicide Prevention Lifeline}}}\uc2  on 988, chat on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://988lifeline.org/chat/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 988lifeline.org}}}\uc2 , or {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.crisistextline.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 text HOME}}}\uc2  to 741741 to connect with a crisis counselor. In the UK and Ireland, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.samaritans.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Samaritans}}}\uc2  can be contacted on freephone 116 123, or email {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:jo@samaritans.org"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jo@samaritans.org}}}\uc2  or {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:jo@samaritans.ie"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jo@samaritans.ie}}}\uc2 . In Australia, the crisis support service {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.lifeline.org.au/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Lifeline}}}\uc2  is 13 11 14. Other international helplines can be found at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.befrienders.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 befrienders.org}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 This article was amended on 28 and 29 November 2023 to correct the spelling in a picture caption of Christiaan Barnard\u8217\'20\'19s name; to indicate in the headline that the matter is the subject of a lawsuit by the father (Mike O\u8217\'20\'19Connor) against the hospital; and to note that it was a trial judge, and not a fifth circuit judge, who threw Donor Network West out of the lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 This article was supported by The Economic Hardship Reporting Project\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231128ejbs001rz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
