\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Idaho asks supreme court to decide on law penalizing  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  providers\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 955 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 November 2023\par 11:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 At issue is a court ruling that the state\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban conflicts with government rules mandating the provision of emergency care\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  is on the verge of being dragged back into the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars.\par \par \uc2 Eighteen months after the court\u8217\'20\'19s conservative majority overturned Roe v Wade and abolished the national right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the state of Idaho, represented by the conservative legal powerhouse the \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2  (\uc2 ADF\uc2 ), has asked the nation\u8217\'20\'19s highest court to allow a law that penalizes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers. The state is requesting that the court halt a federal court decision finding that Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s ban conflicts with government rules governing the provision of emergency care.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Elena Kagan\uc2  has asked for additional briefing in the case by Thursday afternoon. The justices may rule anytime after that.\par \par \uc2 Idaho has implemented a near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban that only permits \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to save the life of a pregnant woman. Doctors have said that this kind of language is too vague and ignores the complex reality of pregnancy; in practice, they say, exceptions like Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s force them to wait and watch until patients become sick enough for doctors to legally intervene, imperiling their long-term health and even risking their lives.\par \par \uc2 In its first legal action to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access after Roe\u8217\'20\'19s demise, the justice department in August 2022 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/02/idaho-abortion-ban-justice-department-sues"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sued Idaho}}}\uc2  over its law, claiming that it violated the federal Emergency Medical Treatment and Labor Act, or Emtala. After Roe fell, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/emergency-medical-care-letter-to-health-care-providers.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Biden}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/emergency-medical-care-letter-to-health-care-providers.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 administration issued guidance}}}\uc2  clarifying that Emtala, which requires hospitals that receive federal funding to treat people in medical emergencies, also requires that doctors provide emergency abortions to people who need them.\par \par \uc2 Weeks after the justice department sued, a federal district judge sided with the Biden administration and ruled to block the provisions of Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s ban that the judge said clashed with Emtala.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen pregnant women come to a Medicare-funded hospital with an emergency medical condition, Emtala obligates the treating physician to provide stabilizing treatment, including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care. But regardless of the pregnant patient\u8217\'20\'19s condition, Idaho statutory law makes that treatment a crime,\u8221\'20\'1D the US district judge B Lynn Winmill wrote in his ruling.\par \par \uc2 This fall, a panel of judges on the US court of appeals for the ninth circuit put Winmill\u8217\'20\'19s ruling on hold while litigation plays out. However, days later, a larger panel of judges walked back that decision and agreed to rehear the matter.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents have rejected the interpretation that Emtala protects \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in medical emergencies.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEmtala ensures that patients are not denied treatments that are authorized under state law because of an inability to pay. It does not ensure that patients are offered unauthorized treatments,\u8221\'20\'1D lawyers for the \uc2 ADF{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/23/23A470/290151/20231120154722276_2023-11-20%20Motion%20with%20Appendix.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote in their petition}}}\uc2  to the supreme court. \u8220\'20\'1CThere is no \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -access purpose hiding in the 37-year-old Emtala statute.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, the Biden administration argued before a different federal appeals court, the notoriously conservative fifth US circuit of appeals in New Orleans, that emergency room physicians should be told they can perform abortions in emergencies \u8211\'20\'13 even if they believe that an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would violate Texas\u8217\'20\'19s near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. A federal court judge last year ruled to block the Biden administration from enforcing its Emtala guidance in Texas.\par \par \uc2 Generally, the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  is more likely to get involved in a case when appeals courts in different parts of the country issue conflicting rulings \u8211\'20\'13 as is now the case in Idaho and Texas.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re probably eventually going to have to rule on the Emtala issue,\u8221\'20\'1D Joanna Grossman, a law professor at the Southern Methodist University Dedman School of Law. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t think the supreme court is getting their way \u8211\'20\'13 they wanted Dobbs to be the last word on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D she continued, referring to the case that overturned Roe.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/nov/28/texas-supreme-court-hears-extreme-abortion-ban-lawsuit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Another lawsuit currently unfolding in Texas state court}}}\uc2  seeks to clarify when doctors can perform abortions in medical emergencies. Both cases, Grossman said, are about the same fundamental question: when do people need abortions?\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CCan you draw a line between abortions that are medically necessary and ones that aren\u8217\'20\'19t? Is that even possible? And if you could draw a line, who gets to draw the line?\u8221\'20\'1D Grossman said. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s really getting at pretty core issues on who regulates \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or who has control over the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Now that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2023/nov/10/state-abortion-laws-us"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 16 states}}}\uc2  ban almost all abortions, activists on both sides have repeatedly clashed over the feasibility of exceptions written into \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. Dozens of women from states across the country have said they were denied abortions in medical emergencies, despite exceptions that were supposed to guarantee their safety in those situations. Some anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists, meanwhile, claim that there are no medical emergencies that would justify the intentional termination of a fetus.\par \par \uc2 Hospitals in Missouri and Kansas violated Emtala when they denied a woman an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/federal-investigation-finds-hospitals-that-denied-emergency-abortion-broke-the-law"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Biden administration found in an investigation}}}\uc2  earlier this year. The Center for Reproductive Rights has also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/sep/12/women-denied-abortions-medical-emergencies-challenge-bans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filed a complaint against a hospital in Oklahoma}}}\uc2 , after a woman said she was denied treatment for a nonviable pregnancy there.\par \par \uc2 The supreme court has also been asked to weigh in on another case brought by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups seeking to suspend FDA authorization for a key \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill. The justices have not yet said whether they will take that case.\par \par \uc2 Lawyers for the \uc2 ADF\uc2  said in court papers that if the supreme court takes up the Idaho case, arguments could be scheduled for as soon as April 2024. If this happens, the justices would rule by summer 2024 \u8211\'20\'13 months before the presidential election.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231130ejbu001jp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
