\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 The women who made painful choices challenge Texas\u8217\'20\'19s severe  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ban\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 1,335 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 November 2023\par 12:34 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The state supreme court will consider arguments from 20 women who say they were denied medically necessary abortions\par \par \uc2 After Danielle Mathisen and her husband realized they would be having a baby girl, they started calling her \u8220\'20\'1CMini\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CWe figured she would be a mini-me,\u8221\'20\'1D Mathisen said.\par \par \uc2 For months, Mathisen\u8217\'20\'19s pregnancy appeared normal. Genetic testing went well. Her parents were thrilled \u8211\'20\'13 this would be the first grandchild in the family.\par \par \uc2 But in September 2021, two weeks after Texas banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  past six weeks of pregnancy, Mathisen went in for her 18-week anatomy scan. And Mathisen, then a 25-year-old fourth-year medical student, quickly realized that something was terribly wrong.\par \par \uc2 Doctors soon confirmed Mathisen\u8217\'20\'19s fears: the fetus had only one kidney, severe scoliosis, a partially formed umbilical cord and improperly positioned hands and feet. The fetus also had an underdeveloped brain.\par \par \uc2 Mathisen\u8217\'20\'19s pregnancy was at risk of ending before she ever gave birth. If Mathisen did give birth, her baby would probably die from respiratory failure shortly afterward. \u8220\'20\'1CShe doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have enough brain to tell her lungs how to breathe,\u8221\'20\'1D Mathisen said.\par \par \uc2 If she continued the pregnancy, Mathisen\u8217\'20\'19s health could be at risk, too.\par \par \uc2 Soon after her diagnosis, Mathisen started calling \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics in Colorado. They were booked solid. Her mother ultimately found her an appointment at a clinic in New Mexico, which Mathisen said was holding appointments open for Texas women. The appointment was for the very next day.\par \par \uc2 Afterward, when she returned to Texas, Mathisen and her husband told everybody that she had had a miscarriage. \u8220\'20\'1CContinuing a pregnancy that was not going to end up with a baby in the crib, but a baby in the casket \u8211\'20\'13 it did not make sense for me, personally,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Now, though, Mathisen has gone public with her story. She is one of 20 Texas women who have sued their home state, arguing that they were denied medically necessary abortions. While Texas bans almost all abortions, the procedure, according to state law, should be allowed in medical emergencies. But \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates and doctors in the state say that the exceptions in the law are so vague that doctors can\u8217\'20\'19t decipher them. Instead, they are forced to watch until patients get sick enough to intervene.\par \par \uc2 The Texas supreme court will hear arguments in the case on Tuesday. Rather than demolishing Texas\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban outright, the women \u8211\'20\'13 as well as two doctors who have also joined the case \u8211\'20\'13 are hoping that the court will agree to clarify the state\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  exceptions.\par \par \uc2 A judge in Austin, Texas, heard arguments in the case in July, in a hearing believed to mark the first time that women testified in court about their experiences with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade last year.\par \par \uc2 Amanda Zurawski, the lead plaintiff in the case, talked about learning that her cervix had dilated too early in her pregnancy. She had no chance of giving birth to a healthy baby \u8211\'20\'13 but she was also unable to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Texas because doctors could still detect a fetal heartbeat, she said. Zurawski ultimately developed sepsis and spent three days in the ICU.\par \par \uc2 Samantha Casiano testified about discovering that her fetus had been diagnosed with anencephaly, a condition where parts of a fetus\u8217\'20\'19s skull and brain do not develop properly. But she did not have the money to travel out of state for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so she gave birth to a baby daughter who struggled for air and died just four hours after birth. Casiano was so overcome on the stand that, in the middle of testifying, she threw up.\par \par \uc2 A few weeks after the women\u8217\'20\'19s testimony, in August, the judge issued a preliminary injunction, ordering that doctors be allowed to perform abortions in cases where patients have an \u8220\'20\'1Cemergent medical condition\u8221\'20\'1D that threatens their life or health.\par \par \uc2 But Texas immediately appealed that ruling, which froze the preliminary injunction. In court filings, attorneys for Texas argued that doctors, not the state, are responsible for what happened to the women in the lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese independent decisions by third-party medical providers, right or wrong, break any purported chain of causation\u8221\'20\'1D between the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban and any medical emergency, they alleged.\par \par \uc2 In any case, the attorneys argued, these women don\u8217\'20\'19t have the legal right to sue, because the pregnancies at issue in the lawsuit are in the past and they are no longer at risk of imminent damage from Texas\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws. Multiple women involved in the lawsuit, including Mathisen, became pregnant again.\par \par \uc2 Nick Kabat, a staff attorney with the Center for Reproductive Rights, which is representing the women in the Texas case, said that the Texas state supreme court \u8211\'20\'13 made up exclusively of Republicans \u8211\'20\'13 could buy that argument. Kabat is preparing for the possibility that the justices rule that, in order to sue, advocates need a plaintiff who is actively in the midst of a medical emergency.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey could say that what you need is a woman who is in an emergency in desperate need of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D Kabat said. \u8220\'20\'1CSo basically, someone who has amniotic fluid dripping down their leg or they\u8217\'20\'19re actively bleeding, where they have an IV connected to them that\u8217\'20\'19s really keeping them alive. And you know what, if they say that, then we\u8217\'20\'19ll find that patient, because the patient exists in Texas, unfortunately.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 At the end of August, the Texas government {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/aug/28/texas-limited-access-abortion-law"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 enacted new legislation}}}\uc2  to, in theory, allow doctors to perform abortions in cases of ectopic pregnancies \u8211\'20\'13 a life-threatening condition \u8211\'20\'13 or when a pregnant person\u8217\'20\'19s water breaks too early for the pregnancy to be viable.\par \par \uc2 But critics say that those exceptions don\u8217\'20\'19t cover the vast array of nuanced issues that can arise in pregnancy. In addition, rather than completely shielding a doctor from prosecution, the new exceptions offer doctors a defense in court only after they have been charged \u8211\'20\'13 a legal mechanism known as an \u8220\'20\'1Caffirmative defense\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhatever fix the Texas government thinks it has adopted, it hasn\u8217\'20\'19t solved this issue,\u8221\'20\'1D Kabat said. \u8220\'20\'1CThat law has not changed the way doctors are practicing.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Mathisen ended up moving to Hawaii for her OB-GYN residency, rather than remain in Texas, where she had once hoped to work.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m currently pregnant with another baby girl, and I would love for her to live across the street from her grandma,\u8221\'20\'1D Mathisen said. \u8220\'20\'1CRight now, I can\u8217\'20\'19t do that, because I don\u8217\'20\'19t think I could go back to Texas and practice medicine if I cannot do abortions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Since the Texas lawsuit was first filed in March, the \uc2 Center for Reproductive Rights\uc2  has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/sep/12/women-denied-abortions-medical-emergencies-challenge-bans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filed similar lawsuits in Idaho and Tennessee}}}\uc2 . The Center is also representing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/sections/health-shots/2023/04/25/1171851775/oklahoma-woman-abortion-ban-study-shows-confusion-at-hospitals"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a woman}}}\uc2  who has filed a federal complaint against a hospital in Oklahoma that, she says, refused to give her an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a dangerous and nonviable pregnancy. Instead, staffers at the hospital allegedly told the woman to wait in the hospital parking lot until she was \u8220\'20\'1Ccrashing\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 If the Texas state supreme court rules in favor of women like Mathisen, it will not set a legal precedent for the lawsuits in other states. But Kabat believes it would help.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medical exemptions are worded in very similar ways,\u8221\'20\'1D Kabat said. \u8220\'20\'1CA ruling in Texas in our favor would be powerfully persuasive to justices deciding similar cases in other states.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Throughout her ordeal, Mathisen clung to one small source of comfort: in the ultrasound, Mathisen could see that Mini\u8217\'20\'19s hands were curved into the shape of a heart.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLike the heart that Taylor Swift makes,\u8221\'20\'1D Mathisen said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe heart that she made with her hands, through all of my tears and sobbing \u8211\'20\'13 I was like, \u8216\'20\'18She\u8217\'20\'19s OK.\u8217\'20\'19 It was just a sign that everything was going to be OK, even if we ended the pregnancy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231128ejbs001e4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
