\par\par \uc2 Books\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18A story of revolutionary deep care\u8217\'20\'19: revisiting the history of radical  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  defense\b0\par\par\uc2 Adrian Horton \par \uc2 1,365 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 November 2023\par 01:29 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 In the book Deep Care, historian Angela Hume offers lessons from generations of underground activists and clinicians who worked to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access\par \par \uc2 In the run-up and year following the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s reversal of Roe v Wade in June 2022, there emerged a narrative of return: that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in states where it was suddenly banned would revert to the underground. It would be a return to 1972, when diffuse, partially anonymous groups such as the Jane Collective, a secret network of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in Chicago immortalized in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2022/jun/07/theres-always-something-to-be-done-how-the-janes-provided-abortions-before-roe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 documentary The Janes}}}\uc2  and in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2022/jan/24/call-jane-review-abortion-drama-is-sensitive-if-not-revelatory"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 feature film starring Elizabeth Banks}}}\uc2 , stood in for legal reproductive healthcare.\par \par \uc2 In reality, the end of Roe didn\u8217\'20\'19t so much send the US back to a pre-1973 landscape of unsafe abortions, but {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2022/07/04/we-are-not-going-back-to-the-time-before-roe-we-are-going-somewhere-worse"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 toward a bleak and unprecedented}}}\uc2  future of criminalized pregnancy. And the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  underground never disappeared under Roe, anyway. Far from it \u8211\'20\'13 in a new book, the feminist historian, critic and poet Angela Hume draws on dozens of interviews with former unlicensed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, community clinic workers and volunteer clinic defenders who together formed the vibrant, multi-pronged and under-sung radical edge of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  defense movement.\par \par \uc2 Deep Care: the Radical Activists Who Provided Abortions, Defied the Law and Fought to Keep Clinics Open is, in part, an oral history of the loose collective of activists and clinicians orbiting a single feminist clinic in Oakland, California, over three decades. It is part collage of organization tactics, art and poetry, the creative side of political consciousness. And it is part roadmap for how to practice small-group, community-based, intersectional care, within and outside of the law and often against extreme hostility, even while Roe was technically on the books.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a story about revolutionary deep care,\u8221\'20\'1D Hume writes in the preface. \u8220\'20\'1CCommunity care that transforms and empowers us from the inside out, through practice and over time.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Hume, who is based in the Bay Area, initially set out to write a book about feminist poets and health advocates, and particularly the work of Pat Parker, a radical Black lesbian feminist and poet who worked at the Oakland Feminist Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Center (OFWHC) from 1978 until 1988. But speaking to Parker\u8217\'20\'19s former co-worker Linci Comy, the clinic\u8217\'20\'19s director for more than 30 years, Hume began to see OFWHC as a critical community hub, a bastion of reproductive rights activism and linchpin of several overlapping, related movements.\par \par \uc2 First, the oft-forgotten \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  self-help movement, a west coast-led political movement starting in the 1970s which encouraged lay people \u8211\'20\'13 as in, people outside the medical establishment \u8211\'20\'13 to learn about and practice gynecology on each other. This encompassed cervical self-exam and \u8220\'20\'1Cmenstrual extraction\u8221\'20\'1D (suction \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ), with the goal of opening up the practice within their own communities. Self-help was a group educational practice defined against the clinic\u8217\'20\'19s service for a fee; the idea was that \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is normal, and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is natural. It\u8217\'20\'19s a normal process and it\u8217\'20\'19s a natural process,\u8221\'20\'1D said Hume. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd that small groups of lay people, trained in sterile technique and menstrual extraction, could easily do this.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 After Roe protected the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , at least in name, some underground self-helpers shifted to the clinical setting; OFWHC, renamed West Coast Feminist Health Project/Women\u8217\'20\'19s Choice in 1988, was founded in October 1972 by 19-year-old Laura Brown as a self-help clinic that offered pregnancy screenings and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  referrals as well as workshops on the female body. And many continued to work outside a clinical setting, guided by the certainty that medical tools and knowledge should proliferate outside the long discriminatory system of institutionalized medicine and the practicality that just because \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was legal didn\u8217\'20\'19t mean it was affordable or accessible. \u8220\'20\'1CSelf-helpers understood that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was legal for many people in name only,\u8221\'20\'1D said Hume, particularly after the passage of the Hyde amendment in 1977, which barred federal Medicaid payments for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and effectively limited access for poor people.\par \par \uc2 The Hyde amendment was evidence for what many of Hume\u8217\'20\'19s interviewees explain in the book: \u8220\'20\'1CWe can\u8217\'20\'19t rely on the state to provide care,\u8221\'20\'1D Hume summarized. \u8220\'20\'1CWe couldn\u8217\'20\'19t then, and we can\u8217\'20\'19t now.\u8221\'20\'1D The self-help movement \u8220\'20\'1Cteaches us about how to empower, defend, and care for each other\u8221\'20\'1D, she said. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd when we can do this, we can strengthen our community from the inside, and we don\u8217\'20\'19t have to rely on the state.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Deep Care proceeds largely chronologically, as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  defense expanded to include a street movement to defend clinics against escalating attacks by the Christian right, which grew more organized and violent throughout the 1980s and 90s. Hume spoke with several former volunteers and organizers of Bay Area Coalition Against Operation Rescue (later Bay Area Coalition for Our Reproductive Rights), a group organized in the late 80s against a radical anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group which blocked clinic entrances. She gets into the weeds: how they recruited, how they build and deployed an emergency response network, how and where they stood outside clinics to create literal pathways for access; meeting notes, posters, a street theater wedding mocking the right\u8217\'20\'19s faux piety, which drew on the creative tactics employed by the sister grassroots movement Act Up, to end the Aids crisis.\par \par \uc2 The activists are astute on what drove the right\u8217\'20\'19s dogged focus on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which by the late 80s had become a coalition-building issue: it was always an expression of social and political control. A decade into Roe-era America, Hume writes, citing the insight of a clinic defender named Laura Weide, \u8220\'20\'1Cthe right had figured out how to dog-whistle their racism and build their forces around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a way that enabled their followers to feel moral,\u8221\'20\'1D a strategy which has evolved into present-day attacks on healthcare. \u8220\'20\'1CToday, the right is recycling the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  playbook to destroy access to healthcare or even its possibility for trans people. We\u8217\'20\'19re seeing the same information distortion, the harassment, the legislative attacks,\u8221\'20\'1D said Hume. \u8220\'20\'1CThe history teaches us that there will never not be a need for community-based care solutions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Whether in self-help circles, independent clinics or underground networks of clinic defense, small group and community capability is \u8220\'20\'1Cthe heart\u8221\'20\'1D of the book, said Hume. \u8220\'20\'1CDeep care takes time. It involves learning how to do intimate relationships differently. And it involves acknowledging that we share power by both taking care of each other and taking care of ourselves.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Hume was finishing the book when the Dobbs decision came down, and it looms; she ends this work of astonishing historical record with notes for the future. Among them: accompany each other, fight to keep clinics open, band against the body cops. And critically, make the referral. \u8220\'20\'1CHistory shows us that a key to safe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the referral to knowledgable, trustworthy care, and the referral has for centuries been a tool of underground \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  workers,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. With the spate of recent bans and widespread access to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2023/nov/09/plan-c-documentary-mail-abortion-pill-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  pills by mail}}}\uc2 , above and underground, via such organizations as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://aidaccess.org/en/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 AidAccess}}}\uc2  or {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.plancpills.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Plan C}}}\uc2 , the referral is \u8220\'20\'1Cmore important than ever before\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 And, crucially, the power of long-term, close-knit, compassion-based work at the local level \u8211\'20\'13 assisting with access, for example, or tracing funding for local crisis pregnancy centers that covertly discourage \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or combatting misinformation within one\u8217\'20\'19s community. \u8220\'20\'1CWhen small groups of people work closely and securely and dynamically together, they can make revolutionary change,\u8221\'20\'1D said Hume. \u8220\'20\'1CIt can be challenging and it takes a lot of patience and practice, but when you do it successfully, you can really build community power. It\u8217\'20\'19s one of the most important political lessons that there is.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Deep Care: The Radical Activists Who Provided Abortions, Defied the Law and Fought to Keep Clinics Open is out now\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 This article was amended on 27 November 2023. A previous version incorrectly stated that the Hyde Amendment barred the use of federal Medicare, not Medicaid, funds for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231127ejbr001bb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
