\par\par \uc2 Film\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18I feel called to do this\u8217\'20\'19: US providers sending  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  medication by mail\b0\par\par\uc2 Adrian Horton \par \uc2 1,397 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 November 2023\par 20:25 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The documentary Plan C embeds with the organization connecting \uc2 FDA\uc2 -approved pills to recipients in all 50 states\par \par \uc2 The question of why hangs over Plan C, a new documentary on efforts to expand access to medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the United States. Why seek a medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? Because it\u8217\'20\'19s safe, says one woman. (The two-pill combination of mifepristone and misoprostol, both certified by the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , are approved for the termination of pregnancy in the first trimester in 90 countries, although its use is severely restricted in the US.) Because of the comfort and safety of being in one\u8217\'20\'19s own home, says another in a montage of phone calls seeking medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  by mail. Because the fear of facing screaming protesters at clinics, because her family\u8217\'20\'19s military doctor refused to tie her tubes at 24, because \u8220\'20\'1CI felt more comfortable doing this at my own pace, at my own time\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Pregnant people seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  face a gauntlet of hurdles in the US, from cost to appointment scarcity to, depending on where one lives, the threat of prosecution. So, too, do providers, who because of the chaotic, cruel and patchwork reproductive healthcare landscape in the US, are forced to take on extra risk or extra work (or both) to provide basic reproductive healthcare. Still, \u8220\'20\'1CI feel called to do this. There\u8217\'20\'19s not really a why,\u8221\'20\'1D says Dr K (her full name, like many others, is withheld for protection), one of the many providers featured in Plan C who mail \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication out of state. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s like, why do you pick up your kid when they\u8217\'20\'19re crying? Is there a why? You just do it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Plan C, directed by Tracy Droz Tragos and filmed in the run-up to supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s overruling of Roe v Wade in June 2022, takes its name from the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.plancpills.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 organization}}}\uc2  founded by Elisa Wells, Amy Merrill and Francine Coeytaux in 2015 to advocate for medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and connect women with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill providers across the US. Even before the supreme court overturned nearly a half century of precedent in Dobbs v Jackson\u8217\'20\'19s Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization, the promise secured by the 1973 ruling \u8211\'20\'13 that people had the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the US under the constitution\u8217\'20\'19s guarantee of privacy \u8211\'20\'13 was already a fiction. Roe was gutted to the point of irrelevance in some states, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  made too prohibitively onerous or expensive in others.\par \par \uc2 Plan C was one of several groups who stepped up to fill the gaps in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2022/jun/28/tracking-where-abortion-laws-stand-in-every-state"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 constantly shifting, increasingly ominous legal landscape}}}\uc2 . Droz Tragos embedded with Coeytaux and the group\u8217\'20\'19s loose network of providers and volunteers beginning in 2018, spreading the word that mifepristone and misoprostol \u8220\'20\'1Care safer than Tylenol but more restricted than an opioid\u8221\'20\'1D, she said. And available by mail in all 50 states, though with different levels of legal risk. Fifteen states have so-called \u8220\'20\'1Cshield laws\u8221\'20\'1D protecting clinicians who treat patients from states where providing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is illegal, and six have specific telemedicine protections. One state, Nevada, has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/oct/30/self-managed-abortions-arrest-investiagtion-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 criminalized self-managed \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 , while Texas {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/aug/28/texas-limited-access-abortion-law#:~:text=In%20June%2C%20the%20Republican%20governor,suffering%20from%20an%20ectopic%20pregnancy."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 deputized citizens}}}\uc2  to privately sue \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers or anyone who \u8220\'20\'1Caids or abets\u8221\'20\'1D \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  by mail, then, represents a crucial window for access, particularly after a federal judge {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/health/federal-judge-rules-women-can-get-abortion-pill-without-doctor-visits"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 suspended}}}\uc2  the \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s in-person dispensing requirement for mifepristone during the Covid-19 pandemic, allowing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to be legally mailed without an in-person appointment. \u8220\'20\'1CTo be able order this online and get it to your door and do the protocol the way it\u8217\'20\'19s been done for decades already, but you get to avoid having to go to a clinic, or face protesters, or get gas money, and you can do it on your own time \u8230\'20\'26 it makes so much sense,\u8221\'20\'1D said Droz Tragos. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s safe, it\u8217\'20\'19s effective, and now it can be private, as it should be.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The 90-minute film is a collage of activism, personal testimony and tenacious adaptation as ever-confusing legal landscape and risk calculations mutate again and again. One provider in Minnesota meets clients in a traveling van in parking lots, even after a state senator puts out a public call to run her out of town. Another takes call after call at her home for 12 hours; another demonstrates how stuffing the pill bottles with cotton keeps them from jangling in the mail. The specter of risk is ever present \u8211\'20\'13 as one partner of a provider in \u8220\'20\'1CTrumpsylvania\u8221\'20\'1D puts it \u8211\'20\'13 \u8220\'20\'1Cshe\u8217\'20\'19s a force of nature, but not against a truck or a gun\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTo navigate this patchwork of laws takes some delicacy,\u8221\'20\'1D said Droz Tragos. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd depending on where you live, depending on your identity, depending on the community that you\u8217\'20\'19re in, people have different layers of safety, concern and risk. So everyone has to navigate this knowing their own circumstances.\u8221\'20\'1D Still, she and Plan C organizers remain frustrated by the lack of awareness of medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. \u8220\'20\'1CIt gets very confusing with misinformation,\u8221\'20\'1D said Droz Tragos. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s so, so hard even to let people know that this medication exists, that telemedicine is an option.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The film is, in part, a call to spread the word. \u8220\'20\'1CThe thing that will always surprise me is the generosity of spirit of people being willing to share their stories for the benefit of other people,\u8221\'20\'1D said Droz Tragos of the many medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  recipients in the film who shared their experiences \u8211\'20\'13 how they needed help meeting the cost, usually under $150, because all their monthly income went to rent; how one Texas woman, who just received just one of the two \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills and was in excruciating pain for five hours, resisted going to the hospital for fear that her boyfriend would be prosecuted for aiding or abetting. \u8220\'20\'1CThey did want to share their story, in part because medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and receiving medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the way they did, was such a game-changer,\u8221\'20\'1D said Droz Tragos. \u8220\'20\'1CThere was a certain amount of outrage of, \u8216\'20\'18Why didn\u8217\'20\'19t I know about this, and I don\u8217\'20\'19t want somebody else to have to go through the kind of pain and suffering and investigating to find this.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Plan C presents itself as one front of the reproductive rights movement now under intense, fracturing duress. In one standout, thorny scene, members of the organization grapple with how to handle the landscape in Texas, where volunteers face prosecution, as well as online criticism that the group\u8217\'20\'19s hard-charging methods were pushing people too far or undermining clinics. Droz Tragos described the in-movement friction as \u8220\'20\'1Csoul-crushing\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 \u8220\'20\'1CEveryone is in survival mode. Things get worse and then they get worse and then they get worse, and everyone is tired, and everybody is working really, really hard to help people and do things with a moral compass,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s devastating to see the kind of infighting and has been and continues to be among a majority of people who believe in bodily autonomy and making sure that people have access to the care that they need.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As bleak as the current state of reproductive healthcare in the US may be, arguably worse than when Plan C wrapped production in 2022, Plan C evinces the nationwide movement to get people the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care they want. In the six states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights were up to vote since the Dobbs ruling, all six have elected to keep them, even in \u8220\'20\'1Cred\u8221\'20\'1D states such as Kentucky and Kansas. Just days after I spoke to Droz Tragos, Ohio voters {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/nov/07/ohio-issue-1-abortion-rights-vote-result"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 enshrined \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  in the state\u8217\'20\'19s constitution}}}\uc2  by a decisive margin. The battle for reproductive rights, and the larger fight for reproductive justice in the US, is steeply uphill. But \u8220\'20\'1CI really hope audiences take away a spirit of, \u8216\'20\'18We\u8217\'20\'19re not stuck,\u8217\'20\'19 and what is possible when people come together,\u8221\'20\'1D said Droz Tragos. \u8220\'20\'1CThere are actually options and we don\u8217\'20\'19t have to sit back and take it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The subheading of this article was amended on 21 November 2023. Plan C provides information on where to access at-home pill options; it does not send pills to recipients as previously stated.\par \par \uc2 Plan C is available to watch digitally in the US on 14 November with a UK date to be announced\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231122ejbl0038p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
