\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Ohio voted to protect  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rights. Republicans are scheming to undo it\b0\par\par\uc2 Alice Herman \par \uc2 1,063 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 November 2023\par 12:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Twenty-seven GOP members emphasized in a letter after the 7 November vote they would work to prevent its implementation\par \par \uc2 Ohio voters overwhelmingly approved a constitutional amendment on 7 November, guaranteeing its citizens the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, but a group of rightwing Republican lawmakers is already trying to reverse that result.\par \par \uc2 The referendum, which Ohioans passed by 57% of votes, established a constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , overriding laws passed by the Republican-dominated legislature to dramatically restrict access. In response, 27 GOP members of the Ohio general assembly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ohiocapitaljournal.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/11/135GA_Member_Issue-1_Release_Final_to_Send.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 signed a statement}}}\uc2  the next day arguing the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights proposal \u8220\'20\'1Cfailed to mention a single, specific law\u8221\'20\'1D, and vowing to \u8220\'20\'1Cdo everything in [their] power\u8221\'20\'1D to prevent the restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws on the books in Ohio from being challenged.\par \par \uc2 Four of them went even further, circulating draft legislation to give the legislature \u8220\'20\'1Cexclusive authority over implementing\u8221\'20\'1D the constitutional amendment at the exclusion of the courts \u8211\'20\'13 a move legal experts have decried as unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTo prevent mischief by pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  courts,\u8221\'20\'1D wrote the four Republican lawmakers in a statement, \u8220\'20\'1COhio legislators will consider removing jurisdiction from the judiciary over this ambiguous ballot initiative.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The move is the latest attempt by some Republicans in the state to use their iron grip on the state legislature to push anti-democratic efforts. The legislature has for years declined to heed a 2015 ballot measure prohibiting partisan gerrymandering and attempted in August to make it harder for voters to directly affect policy through referendums. The latest move goes much further, essentially challenging the authority of the courts and the constitution.\par \par \uc2 This is Schoolhouse Rock-type stuff. We need to make sure that we have the three branches of the government\par \par \uc2 Ohio house speaker Jason Stephens\par \par \uc2 The draft legislation, however, has been rejected even by high-ranking Republicans: the house speaker, Jason Stephens, called it a non-starter and a clear violation of the basic democratic principle of separation of powers. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is Schoolhouse Rock-type stuff. We need to make sure that we have the three branches of the government,\u8221\'20\'1D he {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cincinnati.com/story/news/politics/2023/11/14/speaker-pans-bill-blocking-judicial-review-of-ohio-abortion-amendment/71580075007/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told reporters}}}\uc2  on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2 , the Republican Ohio governor, also expressed doubts about the bill\u8217\'20\'19s chances. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t think the public should start thinking that it is going to become law,\u8221\'20\'1D DeWine {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.statenews.org/government-politics/2023-11-14/ohio-house-leader-nixes-idea-from-some-republicans-to-seize-courts-power-over-abortion-laws?_amp=true"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Matt Huffman, the state senate majority leader, who vocally {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/may/23/ohio-abortion-rights-republican-ballot-super-majority"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 opposed}}}\uc2  the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights amendment, has yet to weigh in. But he has already floated another alternative: holding another referendum on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access to undo the recent decision. Huffman is term-limited and it\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cleveland.com/news/2023/09/potential-future-house-leadership-fight-looms-over-state-legislative-redistricting-analysis.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 widely expected}}}\uc2  he will run for a seat in the state house of representatives. There\u8217\'20\'19s been buzz locally that if he wins, Huffman could challenge Stephens for the speakership. Huffman did not respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 But just because the Republican proposal isn\u8217\'20\'19t likely to be adopted doesn\u8217\'20\'19t make it irrelevant. Voting rights advocates and political observers see it as just the latest effort to undermine democracy and cement rightwing policies, even if they\u8217\'20\'19re at odds with the majority of Ohio\u8217\'20\'19s voters.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe threat of election subversion is just as powerful as subversion itself, because it erodes trust and makes people question whether or not they want to participate \u8211\'20\'13 whether or not their vote will be counted, [and] whether after it\u8217\'20\'19s counted, if it will actually be respected,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kayla Griffin, the Ohio state director of the voting rights group All Voting Is Local.\par \par \uc2 State Republicans ignored a 2015 referendum prohibiting partisan gerrymandering and drew maps that gave Republicans an advantage in both branches of the legislature \u8211\'20\'13 they have a supermajority in each \u8211\'20\'13 and a lopsided number of congressional seats.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere is a disconnect between what voters want and what the legislature wants\par \par \uc2 Catherine Turcer of Common Cause Ohio\par \par \uc2 The conservative-leaning state supreme court ruled seven times that the maps violated the constitution, and after every ruling the legislature returned maps to the court without meaningfully changing them. The swing vote on the court retired at the end of 2022, leaving behind a court that will likely side with the GOP and let them preserve their supermajorities in the legislature.\par \par \uc2 Those maps have given them immense power to do whatever they want with state laws, even if they\u8217\'20\'19re unpopular with majorities of Ohioans.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere is a disconnect between what voters want and what the legislature wants,\u8221\'20\'1D said Catherine Turcer, executive director of Common Cause Ohio. \u8220\'20\'1CIt just highlights how gerrymandered the state legislature is.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 This summer, Republicans also tried to weaken the one check voters have on their power by making it dramatically harder to pass a statewide constitutional amendment \u8211\'20\'13 specifically targeting the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  initiative in November. The proposal would have raised the threshold to pass citizen-led ballot measures from a simple majority to 60%. Voters rejected it by a wide margin in August.\par \par \uc2 Frank LaRose\uc2 , the Republican secretary of state, who opposed the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  amendment, then rewrote the ballot language for the November election to include the term \u8220\'20\'1Cunborn child\u8221\'20\'1D, rather than \u8220\'20\'1Cfetus\u8221\'20\'1D, a move that opponents criticized as trying to unduly sway voters.\par \par \uc2 The latest threats by the right wing of the state GOP to suppress the implementation of the constitutional amendment are a marked escalation in that effort.\par \par \uc2 If Republicans in the state legislature were to pass their proposed legislation challenging the authority of the courts to interpret the constitution, the issue would almost certainly end up disputed in court.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt really represents an attempt to interfere with the judicial branch,\u8221\'20\'1D said Steven Steinglass, dean emeritus of the Cleveland State University College of Law and the author of The Ohio State Constitution: A Reference Guide. \u8220\'20\'1CIt violates the Ohio principles of separation of powers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The latest proposal appears too much even for the state\u8217\'20\'19s leading Republicans. But it shows how far some elected officials are willing to go to try to force their views on their state \u8211\'20\'13 even when it\u8217\'20\'19s clear that large majorities of Ohioans disagree.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is certainly coming from a far-right faction of the Republican caucus. That said, this faction has gained more and more power,\u8221\'20\'1D said the Ohio Democratic house minority leader, Allison Russo. \u8220\'20\'1CIt just speaks to how this body has become more and more extreme.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231117ejbh001s0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
