\par\par \uc2 Books\par \b \uc2 Party of the People review: Republican strength \u8211\'20\'13 and weakness \u8211\'20\'13 examined\b0\par\par\uc2 Lloyd Green \par \uc2 1,015 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 November 2023\par 07:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Patrick Ruffini\uc2 , a GOP pollster, soft-pedals \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and insurrection but is sharp on his party\u8217\'20\'19s working-class turn\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/nov/08/us-elections-2023-abortion-rights-virginia-kentucky"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voters in Ohio, Kentucky and Virginia}}}\uc2  stood up for individual autonomy, saying no to rolling back \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. Ohio, a conservative state, enshrined such rights in its constitution. In Virginia, a closely contested battleground, both houses went Democratic, a rebuff to the Republican governor, Glenn Youngkin. In Kentucky, \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2 , a Democratic, pro-choice governor, handily won re-election.\par \par \uc2 The personal is the political. The supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s rejection of Roe v Wade and attendant abandonment of privacy as a constitutional mandate stand to haunt the Republican party. Next year\u8217\'20\'19s presidential election is no longer just about the possible return of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , with his two impeachments and smorgasbord of civil and criminal charges. A national referendum on values looms.\par \par \uc2 Into this morass jumps \uc2 Patrick Ruffini\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/PatrickRuffini?ref_src=twsrc%5Egoogle%7Ctwcamp%5Eserp%7Ctwgr%5Eauthor"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a founder of Echelon Insights}}}\uc2 , a Republican polling firm. Party of the People is his look at the US\u8217\'20\'19s shifting demographics. Turns out, it\u8217\'20\'19s not all bad for the Republican cause. With good reason, Ruffini\u8217\'20\'19s subtitle is \u8220\'20\'1CInside the Multiracial Populist Coalition Remaking the GOP\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CA historic realignment of working-class voters helped Trump defy the odds and win in 2016, and brought him to within a hair of re-election in 2020,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/magazine/2023/11/04/new-republican-party-working-class-coalition-00122822"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ruffini writes}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is faltering among the core Democratic groups that were once the mainstay of \u8216\'20\'18the party of the people\u8217\'20\'19 \u8211\'20\'13 working-class voters of color.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Cultural re-sorting continues. Since the 2000 election, educational polarization has come to prominence. Before then, Ruffini {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/magazine/2023/11/04/new-republican-party-working-class-coalition-00122822"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 observes}}}\uc2 , \u8220\'20\'1Cclass \u8211\'20\'13 defined in terms of income \u8211\'20\'13 was widely understood to be the main dividing line in our politics\u8221\'20\'1D. Now it is educational attainment: where you and your spouse went to school.\par \par \uc2 Once the home of Franklin Roosevelt and the New Deal coalition, the Democratic party has emerged primarily as a haven for college graduates, identity politics and multiculturalism. In one extreme outcome, in 2020, it helped birth an idiotic and self-defeating slogan: \u8220\'20\'1CDefund the police.\u8221\'20\'1D On race, white liberals are generally more fervent than communities of color.\par \par \uc2 The Republicans are their mirror image. Over six decades, the GOP has morphed into a magnet for evangelicals, church-goers, southern white voters and white Americans without a four-year degree. It incubated the forces unleashed on January 6 and on display in Charlottesville, Virginia, where neo-Nazis marched in 2017. Significantly, however, the GOP also shows the potential to attract working-class voters across lines of race and ethnicity \u8211\'20\'13 a point Ruffini repeatedly and rightly stresses.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNumerous polls have shown Trump reaching nearly 20% of the Black vote and drawing to within 10 points of Biden among Hispanic voters,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/magazine/2023/11/04/new-republican-party-working-class-coalition-00122822"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 he states}}}\uc2 . If those numbers hold next November, Trump may well be measuring the Oval Office curtains again.\par \par \uc2 Despite what \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2  and the rest of the progressive \u8220\'20\'1Csquad\u8221\'20\'1D in Congress may say, crime and immigration resonate with voters of color. Open borders and wokeness? Less so. The expression \u8220\'20\'1CLatinx\u8221\'20\'1D is best kept in faculty lounges.\par \par \uc2 One need look no further than New York. Immigration is no longer simply a Republican talking point. It is bringing the city to a boiling point. The mayor, Eric Adams, and the Biden administration are at loggerheads on the issue. Last Tuesday, residents of the Bronx, a borough made up mostly of people of color, put a Republican on the city council. On eastern Long Island, the GOP gained control of Suffolk county.\par \par \uc2 Ruffini examines New York political history. He reminds us that in 1965, the conservative columnist William F Buckley ran for mayor. He finished at the back of the pack but gained marked support in white working- and middle-class enclaves. His embrace of the police and skepticism of welfare counted.\par \par \uc2 Five years later, in spring 1970, lower Manhattan witnessed the \u8220\'20\'1Chard-hat riot\u8221\'20\'1D, aimed at anti-war protesters. Later that year, Buckley\u8217\'20\'19s brother, James, won a \uc2 US Senate\uc2  seat with a plurality in a three-way race. In the presidential elections of 1972, 1980 and 1984, New York went Republican. Now, though it seems a Democratic sure thing, the state\u8217\'20\'19s population is stagnating, its share of the electoral vote receding.\par \par \uc2 Ruffini is not infallible. Wrongly, he downplays the salience of the Dobbs v Jackson supreme court decision, which gutted the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the subsequent emergence of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a key election issue. He acknowledges that Dobbs provided a boost to Democrats in 2022 but does not spell out how it thwarted an anticipated red wave and hastened Kevin McCarthy\u8217\'20\'19s downfall as Republican speaker.\par \par \uc2 Party of the People contains multiple references to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  but mentions Dobbs three times only. As for \u8220\'20\'1Cprivacy\u8221\'20\'1D, Ruffini never uses the word. \u8220\'20\'1CJanuary 6\u8221\'20\'1D makes a single appearance \u8211\'20\'13 and only in passing. \u8220\'20\'1CInsurrection\u8221\'20\'1D is not seen. It is almost as if Ruffini is seeking to avoid offending the powers that be.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTrump redefined conservative populism in a secular direction, replacing issues like \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  with immigration and anti-PC rhetoric,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/PatrickRuffini/status/1722074316316016648"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ruffini tweeted on election night}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CMany of his voters voted yes in Ohio.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Yes. But not that many.\par \par \uc2 A little more than one in six Ohio Republicans backed the measure, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2023/11/07/ohio-issue-1-exit-poll-results/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to exit polls}}}\uc2 . On the other hand, 83% of Black voters, 73% of Latinos, more than three-quarters of young voters and five out of eight college graduates identified as pro-choice.\par \par \uc2 Though more conservative than white liberals, voters of color are generally pro-choice. Indeed, in Ohio, their support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access outpaced that found in the general electorate. White voters backed the measure 53%-47%. It passed by 57%-43%.\par \par \uc2 But Democrats should not gloat. The FDR coalition is dead. The party last won by a landslide in 1964. Inflation\u8217\'20\'19s scars remain visible. Kitchen-table issues still count. Trump leads in the polls. Ruffini has a real and meaningful message.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Party of the People is\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bookshop.org/p/books/party-of-the-people-inside-the-multiracial-populist-coalition-that-is-saving-the-gop/18899632?ean=9781982198626"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 published in the US}}}\uc2  by \uc2 Simon & Schuster\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231111ejbb000mf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
