\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Democrats hope to emulate Ohio ballot-measure plan to protect  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rights\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 715 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 November 2023\par 17:26 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Democrats and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters show enthusiasm for ballot initiatives \u8211\'20\'13 but organizers are already meeting interference\par \par \uc2 In the days since Ohio voters overwhelmingly backed a ballot measure to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the state constitution, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters and Democrats across the country have become enthralled with the idea of using such measures to protect access to the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Not only are ballot measures often the best chance states have of restoring or protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, but they have proved to be a shot in the arm for Democratic and Republican voters alike. Ballot measures in states such as Kansas and Ohio, once considered conservative strongholds, have won by overwhelming margins \u8211\'20\'13 and won in areas that Joe Biden lost in 2020.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related referendums are already under way in swing states such as Arizona, Nevada and Florida. However, despite the post election-day enthusiasm, these measures are far from a failsafe.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey are an important tool. But they require a lot of preparation and work,\u8221\'20\'1D said Deirdre Schifeling, chief political and advocacy officer at \uc2 the American Civil Liberties Union\uc2 . Although the national \uc2 ACLU\uc2  is still deciding where to direct its firepower, the organization\u8217\'20\'19s state affiliates are involved with several ballot-measure efforts.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIs there a very strong and united in-state coalition? Is there a commitment from funders that they\u8217\'20\'19re really ready to do this?\u8221\'20\'1D Schifeling said.\par \par \uc2 In many states, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters still have several months to gather enough signatures to secure a spot on the ballot. In purple Arizona, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights activists are eager to garner the votes of independents and Republicans. They are pitching their measure as a rebuke to government overreach, just as activists did in Kansas and Ohio.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt may be, in the narrowest sense, possible to win with only Democrats. But that\u8217\'20\'19s not even on our radar,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jodi Liggett, senior adviser for \uc2 Reproductive Freedom for All\uc2  Arizona, which is championing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ballot measure. \u8220\'20\'1CWe can trust Arizonans to make good decisions, and they want to restore their freedom to access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  without government interference.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 However, in some states, ballot-measure organizers are already running into interference from state officials.\par \par \uc2 In Missouri, which bans almost all abortions, two groups have proposed separate sets of ballot amendments. One set of amendments would overturn Missouri\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban by enshrining reproductive rights writ large into the state constitution. The other would add a number of exceptions into the state\u8217\'20\'19s existing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, including for rape and incest, as well as potentially legalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first 12 weeks of pregnancy. (As each group submitted a number of proposals, the language varies from proposal to proposal.)\par \par \uc2 But both groups have ended up in court over the amendments, arguing that \uc2 Missouri\u8217\'20\'19s secretary of state\uc2 , John Ashcroft \u8211\'20\'13 who is in charge of writing summaries of the ballot initiatives that will appear in front of voters \u8211\'20\'13 wants to rewrite the initiatives with misleading language. For example, Ashcroft {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sos.mo.gov/petitions/2024ipcirculation#2024129"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 suggested telling voters}}}\uc2  that one amendment would \u8220\'20\'1Cnullify Missouri laws protecting the right to life\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Florida\u8217\'20\'19s attorney general, \uc2 Ashley Moody\uc2 , has asked that state\u8217\'20\'19s court to keep a proposed initiative to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights off the ballot. The initiative is \u8220\'20\'1Cpart of a similar overall design to lay ticking timebombs that will enable \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  proponents later to argue that the amendment has a much broader meaning than voters would ever have thought\u8221\'20\'1D, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://acis-api.flcourts.gov/courts/68f021c4-6a44-4735-9a76-5360b2e8af13/cms/case/8ea48e7f-fe8b-4112-aac6-4214b4f8573a/docketentrydocuments/458dd4bd-af51-464d-84e8-bb95cd8defb1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Moody argued in a late October filing}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The ballot initiative, should it end up in front of voters, would protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until fetal viability \u8211\'20\'13 a benchmark that generally occurs around 24 weeks of pregnancy. Florida currently bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  past 15 weeks of pregnancy, but the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/sep/08/florida-abortion-ban-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 state supreme court is now weighing a lawsuit}}}\uc2  over that ban. If the state supreme court upholds it, then a six-week ban would be cleared to take effect.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou can have situations where a state court or state legislature will find reasons to throw out your ballot language because you put a comma in the wrong place, or they decide the definition of a word needs to be something else,\u8221\'20\'1D Schifeling said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19ll use whatever they can use to, to disqualify a ballot initiative.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231110ejba001pa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
