\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  rights and historic wins: key takeaways from the US\u8217\'20\'19s off-year elections\b0\par\par\uc2 Aben\u233\'00\'E9 Clayton and agencies \par \uc2 1,104 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 November 2023\par 13:53 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Virginia Democrats frustrated the Republican agenda as Philadelphia got its first female mayor\par \par \uc2 Voters across the US went to the polls on Tuesday for an array of key races that may set the tone for the general election next year.\par \par \uc2 The night delivered some historic wins and some surprise outcomes. Here are the key takeaways from the night.\par \par \uc2 Virginia voters stave off Republican agenda\par \par \uc2 In Virginia, where all 140 state legislative seats were up for election, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/nov/07/virginia-election-voting-results-house-delegates-senate"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Democrats retained their majority in the state senate}}}\uc2 , crushing Republican lawmakers\u8217\'20\'19 hopes of gaining control of the legislature.\par \par \uc2 In a surprising victory, Democrats also flipped the house of delegates, the lower chamber of the state house. That means they will be able to effectively block the Republican governor Glenn Youngkin\u8217\'20\'19s agenda, which includes a ban on abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy with exceptions for rape, incest and medical emergencies.\par \par \uc2 Gaining control of both chambers would have allowed Republicans to swiftly move ahead with conservative policy priorities. Though not all of the races have been called, Democrats are already celebrating in what was seen as a bellwether for 2024.\par \par \uc2 A pro-choice victory in Ohio\par \par \uc2 In the nation\u8217\'20\'19s only race where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was on the ballot, Ohio voters overwhelming decided to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections in their state constitution.\par \par \uc2 The vote to approve \u8220\'20\'1CIssue 1\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/nov/07/ohio-issue-1-abortion-rights-vote-result"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 passed with nearly 60%}}}\uc2  of the vote on Tuesday after months of campaigning that saw millions of dollars pour in from both sides.\par \par \uc2 As Carter Sherman, reporting for the Guardian in Akron, Ohio, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/nov/07/ohio-vote-abortion-rights-ban-what-is-issue-1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 writes}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access has been embattled in Ohio since the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade last year, sending the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  back to the states and leading 16 states to ban nearly all abortions. Ohio has a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban on the books, which briefly took effect until a court paused it. Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s results should prevent it from being reinstated.\par \par \uc2 Democrat holds on to Kentucky governorship\par \par \uc2 Following a high-profile race that pitted two former law firm colleagues against each other, the Democratic incumbent, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/nov/07/kentucky-governor-election-results-andy-beshear-democrat-wins"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Andy Beshear, will retain}}}\uc2  his gubernatorial seat in the largely conservative state after Kentucky voters chose him over the Republican \uc2 Daniel Cameron\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Cameron, the state\u8217\'20\'19s first Black attorney general, would have been the nation\u8217\'20\'19s first Black republican governor had he been elected. During his campaign for the governor\u8217\'20\'19s seat, Cameron faced criticism from the family of Breonna Taylor, who were angered at his handling of the investigation into her killing.\par \par \uc2 In Mississippi, a Republican governor keeps his job\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/oct/29/brandon-presley-elvis-presley-mississippi-governor-tate-reeves"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Brandon Presley}}}\uc2 , a Democrat and relation of Elvis Presley, lost his race for governor, conceding to the incumbent \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2  on Tuesday night. Reeves avoided what would have been a remarkable upset, calling his victory \u8220\'20\'1Csweet\u8221\'20\'1D, and congratulated Presley for \u8220\'20\'1Crunning hard all the way through\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 It was a hard-fought contest that saw the candidates exchange verbal blows, writes the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/nov/07/mississippi-governor-election-results-tate-reeves-republican-wins"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Adria Walker in Mississippi}}}\uc2 , \u8220\'20\'1Cwith Reeves alleging that Presley had been bought by out-of-state, liberal political interest groups. Presley hammered Reeves on his and his family\u8217\'20\'19s alleged involvement in the state\u8217\'20\'19s ongoing corruption scandal.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Election day was disrupted when polling places in the state\u8217\'20\'19s largest county ran out of ballots and voters endured long lines in a key Democratic stronghold.\par \par \uc2 Philly gets its first female mayor\par \par \uc2 In Philadelphia, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/nov/07/cherelle-parker-first-woman-elected-mayor-philadelphia"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voters elected Cherelle Parker}}}\uc2  as the 100th mayor, and first woman to lead the city. Parker beat out her Democratic opponents and was heavily favored over the Republican candidate David Oh. Parker ran as a moderate in Democratic stronghold where issues like crime, gun violence and blight are consistently top of mind. Parker has been involved in politics since she was a teenager and was a Pennsylvania state representative from 2005 to 2016 and a Philadelphia city council member from 2016 to 2022.\par \par \uc2 Rhode Islanders are sending their first Black representative to Congress\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/nov/07/rhode-island-election-result-gabe-amo-win"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Democrat Gabe Amo, 35, defeated}}}\uc2  the Republican Gerry Leonard to win Rhode Island\u8217\'20\'19s first congressional district. Amo, who grew up in Pawtucket as the son of Ghanaian and Liberian immigrants, claimed more than 32% of the vote and will fill the seat of fellow Democrat \uc2 David Cicilline\uc2 , who stepped down this summer to become president and CEO of the \uc2 Rhode Island Foundation\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 From the \u8216\'20\'18Central Park Five\u8217\'20\'19 to New York City council\par \par \uc2 Yusef Salaam won a seat on the New York City council, completing a stunning reversal of fortune decades after he was wrongly imprisoned in an infamous rape case. Salaam, a Democrat, will represent a central Harlem district on the city council, having run unopposed for the seat in one of many local elections held across New York state.\par \par \uc2 The victory comes more than two decades after DNA evidence was used to overturn the convictions of Salaam and four other Black and Latino men in the 1989 rape and beating of a white jogger in Central Park. Salaam was arrested at age 15 and imprisoned for almost seven years. The group became known as the \u8220\'20\'1CCentral Park Five\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFor me, this means that we can really become our ancestors\u8217\'20\'19 wildest dreams,\u8221\'20\'1D Salaam said in an interview with the AP before the election.\par \par \uc2 A loss for a Uvalde mother turned campaigner\par \par \uc2 Kim Mata-Rubio, the mother of one the 19 children killed at Robb elementary school in Uvalde, Texas, has lost her mayoral bid. After her 10-year-old daughter was killed in May 2022, Mata-Rubio became an outspoken gun violence prevention advocate.\par \par \uc2 She announced her bid for mayor of Uvalde in June and ran on the promise of making the city \u8220\'20\'1Ca place where every citizen feels heard, where we honor our past while building a brighter future, and where tragedies like the one my family experienced catalyze positive change for all\u8221\'20\'1D, according to her campaign website.\par \par \uc2 Ohioans vote to legalize recreational marijuana\par \par \uc2 In another rebuke to the Republican leadership, Ohio voters have made the state the 24th in the nation to legalize marijuana for recreational use. The legislation\u8217\'20\'19s approval comes after Republican officials, including Governor \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2 , publicly denounced the legalization over fears it would negatively affect workplace and road safety.\par \par \uc2 Under the new law, people who are at least 21 years old are allowed to buy marijuana and possess up to two and a half ounces of it. \u8220\'20\'1CMarijuana is no longer a controversial issue,\u8221\'20\'1D said Tom Haren, a spokesman for the Coalition to Regulate Marijuana Like Alcohol, which backed the Ohio proposal.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231108ejb8001mj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
