\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Mike Johnson, the new speaker of the House, is a gender extremist\b0\par\par\uc2 Moira Donegan \par \uc2 1,177 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 November 2023\par 11:14 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Gender conservatism does not tend to attract as much notice as the other pillars of the far-right ideology, but it is central to the Republican ideology\par \par \uc2 Late last month, when House Republicans ended their chaotic, weeks-long search for a new speaker by elevating Louisiana\u8217\'20\'19s Mike Johnson, a curious trend of stories began appearing in national media. Democratic operatives (and perhaps a few of Johnson\u8217\'20\'19s Republican adversaries, too) had begun leaking what\u8217\'20\'19s known in Washington as \u8220\'20\'1Coppo\u8221\'20\'1D, or opposition research \u8211\'20\'13 unflattering truths about political rivals \u8211\'20\'13 about the new speaker.\par \par \uc2 For many politicians, the embarrassing secrets revealed in an oppo dump are somewhat oblique; usually, they\u8217\'20\'19re about money. Maybe the candidate, as a young lawyer, represented a bank in a case where he aimed to repossess the home of a poor widow; maybe the candidate\u8217\'20\'19s husband or daughter was appointed to a job they did not seem quite qualified for, raising questions about nepotism or access trading. The aim of such stories is to make a politician appear corrupt, or unscrupulous \u8211\'20\'13 like someone beholden to greed and not to principle.\par \par \uc2 But the picture that has emerged instead of the once-obscure Louisiana congressmen has not been that of the typically cynical climber, maneuvering corporate heights in pursuit of their own ambition without regard to ethics. Instead, the revelations that have emerged about Mike Johnson since his ascent to the speakership paint a picture of a fevered zealot: in thrall of baroque and morbid religious fantasies; beholden to a regressive, bigoted and morbid worldview; and above all, obsessed \u8211\'20\'13 with a lurid and creepy enthusiasm \u8211\'20\'13 with sex, and how he thinks it should be done.\par \par \uc2 The enforcement of a Christian sexual morality and a strict gender hierarchy of men over women have not been incidental or minor themes of Johnson\u8217\'20\'19s career: they have been its primary goal, one he pursued doggedly through his pre-congressional life. As a lawyer, he worked against gay marriage, and to uphold Louisiana\u8217\'20\'19s criminal ban on gay sex, writing briefs that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2023/10/25/politics/mike-johnson-gay-sex-criminalization-kfile/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 described homosexuality}}}\uc2  as \u8220\'20\'1Cinherently unnatural\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Ca dangerous lifestyle\u8221\'20\'1D which he compared to pedophilia and bestiality. He still opposes marriage equality, and led efforts to squash the speakership candidacy of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2023/10/25/politics/mike-johnson-gay-sex-criminalization-kfile/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tom Emmer}}}\uc2  last month in part because of Emmer\u8217\'20\'19s support for gay marriage rights. Along the way, Johnson has authored {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2022/10/21/1130297123/national-dont-say-gay-stop-children-sexualization-bill"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a national version}}}\uc2  of Florida\u8217\'20\'19s so-called \u8220\'20\'1Cdon\u8217\'20\'19t say gay\u8221\'20\'1D bill, which would outlaw mentions of homosexuality at schools, hospitals and other federally funded facilities. He opposes access to transition-related healthcare for adolescents and adults alike, and both he and his wife have worked to advance so-called \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.msnbc.com/the-reidout/reidout-blog/house-speaker-mike-johnson-conversion-therapy-rcna123350"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 conversion therapy}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D, an abusive, homophobic practice that has been outlawed in several states.\par \par \uc2 It probably goes without saying that Johnson, like many Republicans and nearly all of the party\u8217\'20\'19s luminaries, favors a national ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which he calls a \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thehill.com/homenews/senate/4292782-gop-senators-warn-speaker-abortion/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 holocaust}}}\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D While more savvy Republicans like Glenn Youngkin have attempted to frame themselves as \u8220\'20\'1Cmoderates\u8221\'20\'1D by placing their preferred \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans at supposedly more amenable points in pregnancy, like 15 weeks, Johnson has made no such effort: he has sponsored legislation that would ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide at all stages of pregnancy, establishing a \u8220\'20\'1Cright to life\u8221\'20\'1D for fertilized eggs that supersedes women\u8217\'20\'19s rights to dignity and self-determination.\par \par \uc2 His sweeping antagonism to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights has extended to several kinds of birth control, such as IUDs, implants and many birth control pills. In his career as a lawyer for the \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 a rightwing legal shop spearheading efforts to advance Christian gender conservatism through litigation \u8211\'20\'13 he argued that the most popular kinds of hormonal birth control, and those that are controlled by women, are equivalent to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and should therefore be banned. When the House advanced a bill to codify the right to contraception after the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Dobbs ruling in 2022, Johnson {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.yahoo.com/house-speaker-mike-johnson-plays-175500711.html?guccounter=1&guce_referrer=aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuZ29vZ2xlLmNvbS8&guce_referrer_sig=AQAAAFWmlc-oSzSUDSZ729rsfXr-pwhgh8hN3ZR8DLPA2TtET7nHwp_XMB54d6ZYLsYmDvfT94U6tkoW90Lfrto9EbQxu9Q5Q7cFm-MUzPHnC1RKim41GtGxx96PjqmO7NXnkBfMxLuoHRMNMU67YhiWNZk7RgfvAvoTFhtHIx5sel5P#:~:text=Johnson%20voted%20against%20the%20Right,Administration%2Dapproved%20forms%20of%20contraception."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voted against it}}}\uc2 . He has since played dumb on the issue, claiming he does not remember his opposition to birth control in an interview with Shannon Bream of Fox News.\par \par \uc2 Johnson says that women\u8217\'20\'19s freedom to leave marriages, along with their freedom to elect out of motherhood when they choose, is responsible for mass shootings\par \par \uc2 In light of his aggressively misogynist and anti-gay views on public policy, it is likely not surprising that Johnson also advances a disturbing and sexist view of the private sphere. He has condemned no-fault divorce, the liberalized regime of divorce law that was won by feminists in the 20th century, and which allowed women to initiate divorce and to exit marriages without having to prove either infidelity or abuse to a court. Johnson says that women\u8217\'20\'19s freedom to leave marriages, along with their freedom to elect out of motherhood when they choose, is responsible for mass shootings.\par \par \uc2 He and his own wife have a so-called \u8220\'20\'1Ccovenant marriage\u8221\'20\'1D, a religious arrangement that formalizes men\u8217\'20\'19s superiority and constricts women\u8217\'20\'19s freedom to leave, designed for conservative straight couples who feel that no-fault divorce and gay marriage rights somehow degrade their own unions. He has also spoken of being in a bizarre arrangement of mutual masturbation monitoring with his son, with whom Johnson installed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.rollingstone.com/politics/politics-news/mike-johnson-son-monitor-porn-intake-covenant-eyes-1234870634/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 family surveillance technology}}}\uc2  that reports users\u8217\'20\'19 pornography consumption habits to one another.\par \par \uc2 It would be easy to see Johnson\u8217\'20\'19s wildly regressive gender politics as a personal quirk \u8211\'20\'13 his beliefs that gay people are sinful and inferior; that women should not be able to live freely from men or use their bodies in ways that are counter to wishes of the men close to them; that marriage should act, for men, as an entitlement to absolute control, and for women, as a prison. But these ideas are not quirks; they are part of a powerful constituency in the Republican party, one that has now found its way into the speakership, second in line for the presidency.\par \par \uc2 Gender conservatism does not tend to attract as much notice as the other pillars of the far-right ideology: it is less distinct than the far right\u8217\'20\'19s avowed white supremacy, less flashy than its hostility to democracy. But the convictions shared by Johnson \u8211\'20\'13 about women\u8217\'20\'19s inferiority and men\u8217\'20\'19s right to control them, about gay people\u8217\'20\'19s moral transgression, and about the ways that the sexed body at birth can, and must, be used to determine the outcomes of a person\u8217\'20\'19s life \u8211\'20\'13 have become the foundation upon which the Republican party\u8217\'20\'19s warring factions are set to unite.\par \par \uc2 The notion that the Christian right tradition that Johnson represents would be uncomfortable with Trumpism was always overstated; in America, Christian conservatives have always had more moral vanity than moral conviction. But now, Johnson\u8217\'20\'19s ascent to the head of the thoroughly Trumpist House Republican caucus marks the groundbreaking for a new party order. The Republican party is rebuilding itself: it\u8217\'20\'19s building on misogyny.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Moira Donegan is a Guardian US columnist\par \par \uc2 Document 