\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Election day 2023: polls close in Ohio and Virginia where  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rights are at stake; Mississippi partially extends voting \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Stein (now) and Joanna Walters (earlier) \par \uc2 10,705 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 November 2023\par 02:20 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 This live blog is now closed. For the latest on the 2023 election, read our latest reports:Ohio voters add \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights to state constitutionKentucky\u8217\'20\'19s Democratic incumbent Andy Beshear retains governorship\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.15am GMT\par \par \uc2 Closing summary\par \par \uc2 It was election day for off-year races nationwide, and Democrats triumphed in major contests. In Kentucky, Democratic governor Andy Beshear {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2023/nov/07/election-day-latest-voting-abortion-virginia-kentucky-ohio-live-updates?page=with:block-654aebb38f081cf924385ca8#block-654aebb38f081cf924385ca8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 won re-election}}}\uc2  in what is otherwise a red state, while in neighboring Ohio, voters {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2023/nov/07/election-day-latest-voting-abortion-virginia-kentucky-ohio-live-updates?page=with:block-654aed168f08c85ed0cfb24c#block-654aed168f08c85ed0cfb24c"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 approved}}}\uc2  a constitutional amendment protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. We are still awaiting the results of races for all 140 seats in the Virginia general assembly, but Democrats {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2023/nov/07/election-day-latest-voting-abortion-virginia-kentucky-ohio-live-updates?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-654ae7b38f08a667a2c270fd#block-654ae7b38f08a667a2c270fd"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 appear on course}}}\uc2  to maintain their majority in the senate, denying Republican governor Glenn Youngkin the majority he needs to pass his agenda \u8211\'20\'13 including a ban on abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy. Another race yet to be called is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2023/nov/07/election-day-latest-voting-abortion-virginia-kentucky-ohio-live-updates?page=with:block-654ade688f08c85ed0cfb1d6#block-654ade688f08c85ed0cfb1d6"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in Mississippi}}}\uc2 , where Republican governor Tate Reeves is standing for a second term against Democrat Brandon Presley.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s what else happened today:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The US House is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2023/nov/07/election-day-latest-voting-abortion-virginia-kentucky-ohio-live-updates?page=with:block-654a932e8f08c85ed0cfaf85#block-654a932e8f08c85ed0cfaf85"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 moving forward}}}\uc2  with a resolution to censure the progressive Democrat Rashida Tlaib over comments criticizing Israel and supporting the Palestinian cause. In {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2023/nov/07/election-day-latest-voting-abortion-virginia-kentucky-ohio-live-updates?page=with:block-654a9b928f08a667a2c26e9c#block-654a9b928f08a667a2c26e9c"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a speech}}}\uc2 , Tlaib said she would continue calling for a ceasefire in the ongoing invasion of Gaza.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 David Weiss, the special counsel investigating Hunter Biden, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2023/nov/07/election-day-latest-voting-abortion-virginia-kentucky-ohio-live-updates?page=with:block-654a89bc8f081cf924385969#block-654a89bc8f081cf924385969"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 defended his independence}}}\uc2  in an unusual behind-closed-doors appearance before the Republican-controlled House judiciary committee.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Iowa \u8217\'20\'19s Republican governor, Kim Reynolds,{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2023/nov/07/election-day-latest-voting-abortion-virginia-kentucky-ohio-live-updates?page=with:block-654a7b178f081cf9243858d5#block-654a7b178f081cf9243858d5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 endorsed}}}\uc2  her Florida counterpart, Ron DeSantis, for president, saying she does not think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  can win next year.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 , the top Democrat in the House, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2023/nov/07/election-day-latest-voting-abortion-virginia-kentucky-ohio-live-updates?page=with:block-654aafdc8f08a667a2c26f4b#block-654aafdc8f08a667a2c26f4b"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 decried}}}\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cad hominem attacks against colleagues\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 a veiled reference to the controversy over Tlaib\u8217\'20\'19s remarks.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 In Ohio, the story of a 10-year-old rape victim who had to travel out of the state to seek an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2023/nov/07/election-day-latest-voting-abortion-virginia-kentucky-ohio-live-updates?page=with:block-654abe998f08c85ed0cfb0df#block-654abe998f08c85ed0cfb0df"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 continued}}}\uc2  to resonate with voters.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.17am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.10am GMT\par \par \uc2 Ohio voters approve protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in state constitution\par \par \uc2 Voters in Ohio have approved a constitutional amendment that will protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the state, the Associated Press reports, despite the Republican-dominated legislature\u8217\'20\'19s has attempts to curtail the procedure since Roe v Wade was overturned last year.\par \par \uc2 The victory of Issue 1 in Ohio was the latest instance of voters in a red state deciding to maintain access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , following {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/nov/09/vermont-abortion-vote-first-us-state-constitution"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 similar elections last year}}}\uc2  in Kentucky and Kansas, as well as a host of Democratic-leaning states.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.01am GMT\par \par \uc2 Democrat Andy Beshear re-elected as Kentucky governor\par \par \uc2 Kentucky\u8217\'20\'19s Democratic governor, Andy Beshear, has won a second term as governor, the Associated Press confirms:\par \par \uc2 enltrBREAKING: Democrat Andy Beshear wins reelection for governor in Kentucky. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/APRaceCall?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #APRaceCall}}}\uc2  at 8:57 p.m. EST. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/BkpxdgZBfx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/BkpxdgZBfx}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 AP Politics (@AP_Politics) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/AP_Politics/status/1722071147653525660?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 8, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 Beshear\u8217\'20\'19s victory is a rare triumph for a Democrat in Kentucky, which has been a reliably red state in recent presidential elections.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.03am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.48am GMT\par \par \uc2 Democrats on course to keep control of Virginia senate, blocking Youngkin \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban\par \par \uc2 Democrats in Virginia are on track to again win a majority in the state senate, which would deny Republican governor Glenn Youngkin the ability to pass his agenda through the legislature, including a proposed ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Results of key senate races show a majority of Democrats triumphing over GOP candidates, meaning at least one of the two chambers in the general assembly will remain in the hands of Youngkin\u8217\'20\'19s opponents. From the Cook Political Report:\par \par \uc2 enltrIn VA Senate, Schuyler VanValkenburg (D) appears to have defeated Siobhan Dunnavant (R) in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/SD16?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #SD16}}}\uc2 . If Russet Perry's (D) narrow lead holds in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/SD31?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #SD31}}}\uc2 , Dems would be guaranteed to keep the majority.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Dave Wasserman (@Redistrict) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Redistrict/status/1722066259523608798?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 8, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 And Virginia-based political consultant Ben Tribbett :\par \par \uc2 enltr8:34PM PROJECTION: Russet Perry (D) defeats Juan Pablo Segura (R) in Senate District 31. Current Senate: 14 GOP, 20 DEM, 6 UND\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Ben Tribbett (@notlarrysabato) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/notlarrysabato/status/1722065040126140853?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 8, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 Democrats are also hoping to flip the house of delegates, which Republicans currently control by two seats. All positions in the two chambers are up for grabs in today\u8217\'20\'19s election.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.34am GMT\par \par \uc2 The Associated Press reports that Democrat Gabriel Amo has won a special election for an open House seat representing Rhode Island:\par \par \uc2 enltrBREAKING: Democrat Gabriel Amo wins special election to U.S. House in Rhode Island's 1st Congressional District. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/APRaceCall?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #APRaceCall}}}\uc2  at 8:10 p.m. EST. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/BkpxdgZBfx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/BkpxdgZBfx}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 AP Politics (@AP_Politics) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/AP_Politics/status/1722059143794593845?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 8, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 Amo\u8217\'20\'19s victory keeps the seat, which was previously occupied by Democrat \uc2 David Cicilline\uc2  until his resignation earlier this year, in the party\u8217\'20\'19s hands. The outcome will not change the balance of power in the House, where the GOP has a four-seat majority.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.29am GMT\par \par \uc2 The Cook Political Report reports that Issue 1, the Ohio ballot measure that will protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the state constitution, has been approved:\par \par \uc2 enltrI've seen enough: in a victory for the pro-choice side, Ohio Issue 1, a measure to establish a right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state constitution, passes.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Dave Wasserman (@Redistrict) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Redistrict/status/1722062934023483640?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 8, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 The Associated Press has not yet called the race.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.18am GMT\par \par \uc2 Polls have closed for election day in Ohio, and the mood at a watch party for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters seemed cautiously hopeful.\par \par \uc2 As voting finished at 7.30pm, dozens of people began to drink, chat and laugh at a downtown Columbus watch party for Ohioans United for Reproductive Rights, the coalition backing Issue 1, which would enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the state constitution. The lights were dimmed down, while speakers started to blast songs like More Than A Feeling and Come On Eileen.\par \par \uc2 Nobody paid much attention to the widescreen TV playing CNN, where reporters discussed early election results. In August, when Ohio held a special election, the race was called around 9pm.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists have reason to feel positive: an October poll found that 58% of Ohio residents planned to vote in favor of Issue 1. And last year, just months after the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade, multiple purple and red states voted to preserve \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Chlo\u233\'00\'E9 Wells, a regional organizer in north-east Ohio for Ohioans United for Reproductive Rights, said that she\u8217\'20\'19s been spending multiple days a week calling people and knocking on doors. There\u8217\'20\'19s been \u8220\'20\'1Coverwhelming support\u8221\'20\'1D, she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re targeting people who are obviously in our universe, but it\u8217\'20\'19s not like we\u8217\'20\'19re targeting people who are guaranteed \u8216\'20\'18yes\u8217\'20\'19es\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D, Wells said, a few hours before polls closed. \u8220\'20\'1CI still feel confident in it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.22am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.06am GMT\par \par \uc2 Polls close in Mississippi as voters consider second term for Republican governor Reeves\par \par \uc2 Voting just finished in most parts of Mississippi, where voters are being asked whether to give Republican Tate Reeves a second term in office, or replace him with Democrat Brandon Presley.\par \par \uc2 But polls are still open in Hinds county, the state\u8217\'20\'19s most populous, where local media reported some polling locations have run out of ballots:\par \par \uc2 enltr??BREAKING NEWS: A judge has ordered polling locations in Hinds County to stay open until 8pm.\par \par \uc2 If you are in line, stay in line. If you need assistance, call our Voter Protection Hotline at 601-203-4131.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Brandon Presley (@BrandonPresley) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BrandonPresley/status/1722050611720368430?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 8, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 Thus, results in this race, where Reeves is predicted to have the advantage, may be delayed.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.18am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.54am GMT\par \par \uc2 Ohio\u8217\'20\'19s Republican governor, Mike DeWine, went on CNN to argue against Issue 1, which would protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 The appearance resulted in a lengthy back and forth with the anchor Kaitlan Collins on DeWine\u8217\'20\'19s insistence its passage would allow abortions later in pregnancies, though those rarely happen.\par \par \uc2 Watch it below:\par \par \uc2 enltrWith polls set to close soon, Ohio's Republican Governor Mike DeWine argues against the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights amendment, warning about late term abortions. The CDC in 2020 less than 1% of abortions in the U.S. were performed at or after 21 weeks. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/3O79xSqv6a"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/3O79xSqv6a}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Kaitlan Collins (@kaitlancollins) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/kaitlancollins/status/1722033609186386147?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 7, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.16am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.35am GMT\par \par \uc2 In addition to a state constitutional amendment protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, Ohio voters are deciding whether to green-light recreational marijuana.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s more on that, from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/ohio-marijuana-legalization-election-2023-6d15efb27fdcd41e7364f2b7cd3177f4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Associated Press}}}\uc2  :\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/ohio-marijuana-legalization-election-e1c00516fec2185881c69fb0e811406a"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Issue 2}}}\uc2  on the statewide ballot would allow adults 21 and over to buy and possess up to 2.5 ounces of cannabis and to grow plants at home. A 10% tax would be imposed on purchases, with proceeds going to administrative costs, addiction treatment, municipalities with dispensaries, and social equity and jobs programs.\par \par \uc2 Ohio would become the 24th state to allow adult use cannabis for fun if the measure passes.\par \par \uc2 The conclusion of the vote follows a nearly two-year push by the Coalition to Regulate Marijuana Like Alcohol, which surmounted administrative, legal and legislative hurdles to put the citizen-initiated statute before voters.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.32am GMT\par \par \uc2 Polls close in Ohio, where protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights is on the ballot\par \par \uc2 Polls have just closed in Ohio, where voters are deciding whether to protect access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 In August, voters {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/aug/09/ohio-issue-1-abortion-rights-win"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overwhelmingly rejected}}}\uc2  an attempt by Republicans to make it more difficult to pass the sort of amendment being voted on today. We\u8217\'20\'19ll see if that is any predictor of how today\u8217\'20\'19s election turns out.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.27am GMT\par \par \uc2 Dave Wasserman, an elections analyst with the Cook Political Report, says Democrat Andy Beshear has been re-elected as governor of Kentucky:\par \par \uc2 enltrI've seen enough: Andy Beshear (D) wins reelection in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/KYGOV?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #KYGOV}}}\uc2 , defeating Daniel Cameron (R).\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Dave Wasserman (@Redistrict) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Redistrict/status/1722047246512832967?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 8, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 The Guardian relies on the Associated Press for race calls, and the news agency has not yet determined the result of the Kentucky gubernatorial election.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.18am GMT\par \par \uc2 Kentucky has been a reliably red state for more than 25 years, but Democratic governor Andy Beshear has what is predicted to be a good shot at winning a second term in office.\par \par \uc2 He is up against Daniel Cameron, the state\u8217\'20\'19s attorney general who, if elected, would be the first Black Republican governor in US history. Kentucky is among the southern states that banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the wake of Roe v Wade\u8217\'20\'19s overturning last year, but the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/kentucky-primary-governors-race-election-2023-e8df45cd3978ce5a1691ba447c84bafc"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Associated Press reports}}}\uc2  that reproductive rights are nonetheless expected to be a major factor in the contest.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s why:\par \par \uc2 While much of Beshear\u8217\'20\'19s first term was dominated by his response to a series of natural disasters and the pandemic, his re-election campaign was often focused on dire warnings about the future of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. He portrayed his Republican challenger, Daniel Cameron, as too extreme on the issue, pointing to his support for the state\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, which lacks exceptions for pregnancies caused by rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 The contest pits two former law firm colleagues against each other in one of this year\u8217\'20\'19s most high-profile elections. The Kentucky results will be closely watched for clues about whether voters remain energized by concerns about the future of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the US. Voters in other Republican-leaning states from Kansas to Ohio have already rebuffed other GOP-backed efforts to erode \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. A Beshear win would signal to President Joe Biden and other Democrats that they should continue to focus on the issue in 2024, when control of Congress and the White House are at stake.\par \par \uc2 Cameron, who would be the nation\u8217\'20\'19s first Black Republican to be elected governor, reaffirmed his support for the current Kentucky law, which bans all abortions except when carried out to save a pregnant woman\u8217\'20\'19s life or to prevent a disabling injury. He later signaled he\u8217\'20\'19d sign a bill adding rape and incest exceptions, but at another point, when confronted by someone claiming to seek reassurance about his position, indicated he\u8217\'20\'19d support such exceptions \u8220\'20\'1Cif the courts made us change that law\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.24am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.00am GMT\par \par \uc2 Polls close in Virginia as voters ponder whether to empower Republican governor Glenn Youngkin\par \par \uc2 Polls have just closed in Virginia, where voters are electing a new slate of legislators in both the state senate and house of delegates.\par \par \uc2 The Republican governor, Glenn Youngkin, is hoping his allies win majorities in both chambers and help him enact his priorities, including a ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Polls also closed in the final 41 counties in Kentucky, in the central time zone.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.14am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.47pm GMT\par \par \uc2 With only a few hours to go before the polls close in Ohio, activists on both sides of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate are scrambling to get more supporters to vote on Issue 1, a proposal to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDo you know that a vote \u8216\'20\'18yes\u8217\'20\'19 would legalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  through all nine months of pregnancy?\u8221\'20\'1D Jamie Curry, Ohio regional coordinator for the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group Students for Life, asked a student as he walked past her on the \uc2 Ohio State University\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s campus. \u8220\'20\'1CIs that something you can agree with or live with?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Issue 1 allows \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be prohibited past fetal viability, a benchmark that generally occurs around 24 weeks of pregnancy. The student, 20-year-old Christian Anguino, seemed unswayed by Curry\u8217\'20\'19s attempt to win him over to her cause.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn\u8217\'20\'19t up to a person like me, a man, to decide that,\u8221\'20\'1D Anguino said. \u8220\'20\'1CA pregnancy is a very, very hard thing to go through.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Curry, who organizes anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  students across Ohio, and a small group of local students had set up a table at a plaza on the \uc2 Ohio State University\uc2  campus, near a school polling station. Their table included numerous \u8220\'20\'1CVote No\u8221\'20\'1D stickers, plus a board that asked: \u8220\'20\'1CWHEN SHOULD THE PREBORN BE PROTECTED AGAINST \b \highlight22\uc2 ABORTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  VIOLENCE?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI definitely feel hopeful,\u8221\'20\'1D said Cheyanne Griffey, a 19-year-old at Columbus State, a community college near the \uc2 Ohio State University\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CJust on this campus alone, it does seem primarily \u8216\'20\'18vote yes,\u8217\'20\'19 but I think if they can read over both sides and really take the time to look, I have a lot of hope that I think they could make the decision to vote no in the end.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The outlook is grim for anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  forces: In an October poll, 58% of Ohio residents say they planned to approve Issue 1.\par \par \uc2 A group of activists who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights set up a duelling table on the other side of the plaza. Their bright-pink sign read: \u8220\'20\'1CKEEP PERSONAL DECISIONS OUT OF THE HANDS OF POLITICIANS.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve been doing organizing and grassroots work for at least the last four, five years,\u8221\'20\'1D said Amber Decker, a volunteer with Indivisible Central Ohio who supports Issue 1. \u8220\'20\'1CI have never felt the energy like we\u8217\'20\'19re feeling right now.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.50pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.16pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Polls have closed in parts of Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 The parts of Kentucky in the eastern time zone have closed their polls, while polls in parts of the state in the central time zone will remain open till the top of the hour.\par \par \uc2 Per the Kentucky secretary of state\u8217\'20\'19s office, voter turnout was about 45-45%.\par \par \uc2 The big race to watch here, again, is for governor. The Democratic incumbent, Andy Beshear, is fighting to hold his seat against Republican challenger Daniel Cameron in a deeply red state.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.21pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.48pm GMT\par \par \uc2 More than a year after the news first broke, the story of a 10-year-old rape victim who had to flee Ohio for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is motivating people to vote for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBasically, they believe if you\u8217\'20\'19re raped, you should have a rapist\u8217\'20\'19s baby,\u8221\'20\'1D said Bill Baldwin, a 70-year-old voter in Columbus, Ohio. \u8220\'20\'1COh, are you kidding?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Ohio is the only state in 2023 to vote directly on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as voters are being asked to decide on Tuesday whether to enshrine the right to the procedure in the state constitution. If \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters win, Ohio will be the first reliably red state to vote in favor of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights since the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade last year.\par \par \uc2 The story of the 10-year-old, who eventually got an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Indiana, made national news within days of Roe\u8217\'20\'19s overturning. At the time, Ohio had a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in effect. That law, which does not have exceptions for rape or incest and has since been frozen by a court, could again become the law of the land if \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters fail this election day.\par \par \uc2 In Akron, Ohio, about two hours north-east of Columbus, voter Lewis Short also brought up the story of the 10-year-old. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat if she had the baby and down the road, the daddy says he wants to see the baby?\u8221\'20\'1D Short told a Guardian reporter the weekend before election day. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s crazy. He raped her.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters are feeling cautiously optimistic about the vote. An October poll found that 58% of Ohioans plan to vote in favor of Issue 1, the proposal to amend the state constitution. And as activists knocked on doors in a Columbus neighborhood on election day, which was unseasonably warm, the people who answered the doors frequently said they planned to vote in favor of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights or already had.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese laws we\u8217\'20\'19re passing? Yeah, we\u8217\'20\'19ve got to stop that. That\u8217\'20\'19s the evil I see right now,\u8221\'20\'1D said Baldwin, who chatted with the activists in front of his house. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m pretty sure Ohio is going to stop that, today.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.53pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.41pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Poll closing times near for voters in Ohio, Virginia, Kentucky, Mississippi\par \par \uc2 Millions of voters in Ohio, Kentucky, Virginia and Mississippi are almost out of time to cast ballots in some of the most closely watched races being decided today.\par \par \uc2 In Kentucky, where Democratic governor Andy Beshear is standing for a second term against GOP candidate Daniel Cameron in the strongly Republican state, most polls close at 6pm eastern time, except for the 41 counties that are in the central time zone and close an hour later.\par \par \uc2 In Virginia, where all seats in the statehouse are up for grabs and will determine whether the Republican governor, Glenn Youngkin, is able to carry out his agenda \u8211\'20\'13 including passing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban \u8211\'20\'13 polls close at 7pm.\par \par \uc2 Voting in Ohio \u8211\'20\'13 where the marquee race is over an amendment to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the state constitution \u8211\'20\'13 halts at 7.30pm.\par \par \uc2 And in Mississippi, where the Republican governor, Tate Reeves, is up for a second term against Democrat Brandon Presley, the voting is over at 8pm.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.44pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.15pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The House will vote tomorrow on censuring Rashida Tlaib.\par \par \uc2 The resolution was proposed by Georgia Republican Rich McCormick, and accuses Tlaib of \u8220\'20\'1Cpromoting false narratives regarding the October 7, 2023, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  attack on Israel and for calling for the destruction of the state of Israel\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Democrats today tried to table the resolution, which would block its passage, but failed when 212 Republicans and one Democrat voted against doing so. It\u8217\'20\'19s unclear if that coalition will hold together tomorrow when the resolution itself is considered.\par \par \uc2 There is evidence that some Democrats are uncomfortable with Tlaib\u8217\'20\'19s statements, particularly her use of the slogan \u8220\'20\'1Cfrom the river to the sea\u8221\'20\'1D, which the resolution explicitly condemns. From {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.congress.gov/bill/118th-congress/house-resolution/845/text"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 its text}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 Whereas, on November 3, 2023, Representative Tlaib published on social media a video containing the phrase \u8220\'20\'1Cfrom the river to the sea\u8221\'20\'1D, which is widely recognized as a genocidal call to violence to destroy the state of Israel and its people to replace it with a Palestinian state extending from the Jordan River to the Mediterranean Sea;\par \par \uc2 Whereas Representative Tlaib doubled down on this call to violence by falsely describing \u8220\'20\'1Cfrom the river to the sea\u8221\'20\'1D as \u8220\'20\'1Can aspirational call for freedom, human rights, and peaceful coexistence\u8221\'20\'1D despite it clearly entailing Israel\u8217\'20\'19s destruction and denial of its fundamental right to exist\par \par \uc2 Earlier today, a group of House Democrats signed on to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://schneider.house.gov/sites/evo-subsites/schneider.house.gov/files/evo-media-document/from-the-river-to-the-sea.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a statement}}}\uc2  that condemned usage of the term, without mentioning Tlaib specifically:\par \par \uc2 We reject the use of the phrase \u8220\'20\'1Cfrom the river to the sea\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 a phrase used by many, including \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , as a rallying cry for the destruction of the State of Israel and genocide of the Jewish people. We all feel deep anguish for the human suffering caused by the war in \uc2 Gaza.Hamas\uc2  started this war with a barbaric terrorist attack on October 7, 2023, and neither the Palestinian nor Israeli people can have peace as long as \uc2 Hamas\uc2  still rules over Gaza and threatens Israel. This war is tragic and deeply painful for everyone, especially those who identify with the land and the people \u8211\'20\'13 Christians, Jews, and Muslims alike. Every civilian killed, every family torn apart, Palestinian and Israeli, is a tragedy. Every human being deserves dignity and respect, and each of us must do all we can to always see the humanity of the innocent people caught in the middle of this war.\par \par \uc2 Illinois\u8217\'20\'19s Brad Schneider was one of the organizers of the statement, and also the only Democrat to vote against tabling the resolution to censure Tlaib.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.20pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.51pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Top House Democrat Jeffries decries 'ad hominem attacks against colleagues' in statement on one-month anniversary of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  attack\par \par \uc2 The House\u8217\'20\'19s Democratic minority leader, \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 , has released a statement marking a month since \uc2 Hamas\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s deadly attack on Israel that also weighs in on the upcoming vote to censure Rashida Tlaib.\par \par \uc2 Tlaib is the sole Palestinian American in the House, and has been outspoken against Israel\u8217\'20\'19s retaliatory invasion of the Gaza Strip. Republicans have moved to censure Tlaib for comments they say promote the destruction of Israel, and which have also attracted criticism from some Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Jeffries does not mention Tlaib specifically in the statement, but instead recommends that Democrats agree to disagree when it comes to Israel:\par \par \uc2 As public officials serving in Congress, the words we choose matter. It is my strong belief that we must all take care to respect each other personally, even when strongly disagreeing on matters of policy or legislation. We should be able to agree to disagree on domestic or foreign policy issues, without being disagreeable with each other or the President of the United States. If the end goal following the defeat of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and safe return of all hostages is a just and lasting peace between Israel and the Palestinian people, as I believe it should be, ad hominem attacks against colleagues will never accomplish that objective.\par \par \uc2 The searing moment of turbulence in our society and throughout the world calls for us to tackle the challenges we confront in a serious, sober and substantive manner. Let us all recommit to doing just that for the good of everyone.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.52pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.25pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The day so far\par \par \uc2 Voters in many states across the country are casting ballots in off-year elections that could serve as important bellwethers ahead of the 2024 presidential vote. There is no shortage of races to cover, but we\u8217\'20\'19ll be paying particularly close attention to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2023/nov/07/election-day-latest-voting-abortion-virginia-kentucky-ohio-live-updates?page=with:block-654a40058f08a667a2c269ee#block-654a40058f08a667a2c269ee"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Virginia}}}\uc2 , where Republican governor Glenn Youngkin is hoping his allies take control of the legislature so he can enact an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2023/nov/07/election-day-latest-voting-abortion-virginia-kentucky-ohio-live-updates?page=with:block-654a4e408f08c85ed0cfabd0#block-654a4e408f08c85ed0cfabd0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ohio}}}\uc2 , a Republican-leaning state where voters are deciding where to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the state constitution. In red state Kentucky, Democratic governor Andy Beshear is fighting for a second term, while in Mississippi, voters are deciding whether to send Republican Tate Reeves to the governor\u8217\'20\'19s mansion again, or replace him with Democrat Brandon Presley \u8211\'20\'13 a cousin of Elvis Presley.\par \par \uc2 But that\u8217\'20\'19s not all the news that has happened today:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The House is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2023/nov/07/election-day-latest-voting-abortion-virginia-kentucky-ohio-live-updates?page=with:block-654a932e8f08c85ed0cfaf85#block-654a932e8f08c85ed0cfaf85"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 moving forward}}}\uc2  with a resolution to censure progressive Democrat Rashida Tlaib over comments criticizing Israel and supporting the Palestinian cause. In {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2023/nov/07/election-day-latest-voting-abortion-virginia-kentucky-ohio-live-updates?page=with:block-654a9b928f08a667a2c26e9c#block-654a9b928f08a667a2c26e9c"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a speech}}}\uc2 , Tlaib said she would continue calling for a ceasefire in the ongoing invasion of Gaza.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 David Weiss, the special counsel investigating Hunter Biden, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2023/nov/07/election-day-latest-voting-abortion-virginia-kentucky-ohio-live-updates?page=with:block-654a89bc8f081cf924385969#block-654a89bc8f081cf924385969"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 defended his independence}}}\uc2  in an unusual behind-closed-doors appearance before the House judiciary committee.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Iowa \u8217\'20\'19s Republican governor Kim Reynolds{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2023/nov/07/election-day-latest-voting-abortion-virginia-kentucky-ohio-live-updates?page=with:block-654a7b178f081cf9243858d5#block-654a7b178f081cf9243858d5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 endorsed}}}\uc2  her Florida counterpart Ron DeSantis for president, saying she does not think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  can win next year.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.01pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Maryland\u8217\'20\'19s Jamie Raskin led the Democratic defense in the just-concluded floor debate over censuring Rashida Tlaib.\par \par \uc2 He argued that punishing Tlaib for her criticism of Israel would undercut speech freedom:\par \par \uc2 enltr\u8220\'20\'1CThis resolution is about one thing and one thing only: the punishment of speech.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8211\'20\'13 Rep. Jamie Raskin (D-MD) speaks in opposition to a resolution to censure Rep. Rashida Tlaib (D-MI) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/XSU6kMUaeH"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/XSU6kMUaeH}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 The Recount (@therecount) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/therecount/status/1721985884092858728?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 7, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 The House just suspended its consideration of the resolution to censure Tlaib, and is expected to vote on it tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.38pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s video of the first half of Rashida Tlaib\u8217\'20\'19s speech on the House floor defending her comments on Israel\u8217\'20\'19s invasion of Gaza:\par \par \uc2 enltrRep. Rashida Tlaib (D-MI) chokes up while condemning the resolution to censure her that the House is considering:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSpeaking up to save lives, Mr. Chair, no matter faith, no matter ethnicity, should not be controversial in this chamber.\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/4ob7W6NZIB"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/4ob7W6NZIB}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 The Recount (@therecount) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/therecount/status/1721989360730444271?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 7, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.27pm GMT\par \par \uc2 'We cannot lose our shared humanity' Tlaib says, as House leads towards censure\par \par \uc2 In remarks on the House floor minutes after Democrats failed to block an effort to censure her for remarks her detractors say disparaged Israel, progressive Rashida Tlaib defended her criticism of the country and urged lawmakers to join in calling for a ceasefire in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI will not be silenced and I will not let you distort my words,\u8221\'20\'1D Tlaib said. \u8220\'20\'1CNo government is beyond criticism. The idea that criticizing the government of Israel is antisemitic sets a very dangerous precedent, and it\u8217\'20\'19s been used to silence diverse voices speaking up for human rights across our nation.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Tlaib, who was first elected in 2018 and is a prominent member of \u8220\'20\'1CThe Squad\u8221\'20\'1D of progressive female lawmakers, grew emotional as she said, \u8220\'20\'1CI can\u8217\'20\'19t believe I have to say this, but Palestinian people are not disposable.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She continued by saying she was against attacks on both Israeli and Palestinian civilians alike:\par \par \uc2 The cries of the Palestinian and Israeli children sound no different to me. What I don\u8217\'20\'19t understand is why the cries of Palestinians sound different to you all. We cannot lose our shared humanity, Mr. Chair. I hear the voices of advocates in Israel and Palestine across America and around the world for peace.\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m inspired by \u8230\'20\'26 the courageous survivors in Israel who have lost loved ones, yet are calling for a ceasefire and the end to violence. I am grateful to the people in the streets for the peace movement with countless Jewish Americans across the country standing up and lovingly saying \u8216\'20\'18not in our name\u8217\'20\'19.\par \par \uc2 We will continue to call for a ceasefire, Mr. Chair, for the immediate delivery of critical humanitarian aid to Gaza, for the release of all hostages and those arbitrarily detained and for every American to come home. We will continue to work for real, lasting peace that uphold human rights and dignity of all people and centers \u8230\'20\'26 peaceful coexistence between Israelis and Palestinians and censures no one \u8211\'20\'13 no one \u8211\'20\'13 and ensures that no person, no child has to suffer or live in fear of violence.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.06pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Progressive Democrat Rashida Tlaib has long been outspoken against Israel\u8217\'20\'19s policies towards Palestinians, but provoked a firestorm of criticism last week by defending the controversial slogan \u8220\'20\'1Cfrom the river to the sea\u8221\'20\'1D:\par \par \uc2 enltrFrom the river to the sea is an aspirational call for freedom, human rights, and peaceful coexistence, not death, destruction, or hate. My work and advocacy is always centered in justice and dignity for all people no matter faith or ethnicity.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Rashida Tlaib (@RashidaTlaib) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/RashidaTlaib/status/1720574880557539763?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 3, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 What makes that slogan so controversial? Here\u8217\'20\'19s the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/daniel-boffey"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Daniel Boffey}}}\uc2  with the answer:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe won\u8217\'20\'19t rest until we have justice, until all people, Israelis and Palestinians, between the river and the sea can live in peaceful liberty,\u8221\'20\'1D said Andy McDonald, a Labour MP, at a protest in London organised by the Palestine Solidarity Campaign at the weekend.\par \par \uc2 Three days later, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.sky.com/story/andy-mcdonald-senior-labour-mp-suspended-over-deeply-offensive-comments-at-pro-palestine-rally-12996759"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 McDonald was suspended from the party}}}\uc2  pending an investigation, leaving the former shadow cabinet minister sitting as an independent for now.\par \par \uc2 Some feel the decision was heavy handed while others see it as a sign of strong leadership from Keir Starmer as the Labour leader tries to draw a clear line between himself and his predecessor, Jeremy Corbyn.\par \par \uc2 The key to understanding why the party reacted so strongly is six words from McDonald\u8217\'20\'19s speech \u8211\'20\'13 and the context in with they were spoken.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBetween the river and the sea\u8221\'20\'1D is a fragment from a slogan used since the 1960s by a variety of people with a host of purposes. And it is open to an array of interpretations, from the genocidal to the democratic.\par \par \uc2 The full saying goes: \u8220\'20\'1CFrom the river to the sea, Palestine will be free\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 a reference to the land between the Jordan River, which borders eastern Israel, and the Mediterranean Sea to the west.\par \par \uc2 The question then is what that means for Israel and the Jewish people.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/oct/31/from-the-river-to-the-sea-where-does-the-slogan-come-from-and-what-does-it-mean-israel-palestine"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18From the river to the sea\u8217\'20\'19: where does the slogan come from and what does it mean?}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.47pm GMT\par \par \uc2 House votes to move ahead with censuring progressive Democrat Rashida Tlaib over Israel comments\par \par \uc2 The House of Representatives just rejected an attempt to block a resolution censuring progressive Democrat Rashida Tlaib over her criticism of Israel.\par \par \uc2 The vote was 213 opposed to tabling the resolution, 208 in favor and one voting present.\par \par \uc2 The resolution accuses Tlaib, the only Palestinian American in Congress, of \u8220\'20\'1Cpromoting false narratives\u8221\'20\'1D regarding \uc2 Hamas\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s 7 October terrorist attack against Israel, and \u8220\'20\'1Ccalling for destruction for the State of Israel.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The House is expected to vote later today on the passing the resolution. Lawmakers are currently on the floor debating Tlaib\u8217\'20\'19s comments.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.33pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Voters across Virginia are in the middle of casting ballots for state senate and assembly seats \u8211\'20\'13 all of which are up for grabs. Beyond just determining control of the legislature, today\u8217\'20\'19s election could decide whether Republican governor Glenn Youngkin is able to pass a ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in one of the few southern states where accessing the procedure is still possible. Here\u8217\'20\'19s more on today\u8217\'20\'19s election, from the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/joan-greve"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Joan E Greve}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 As he approached another door in Fredericksburg, walking past Halloween decorations and trees starting to lose their autumn leaves, Muhammad Khan prepared his pitch to voters. Over the past several weeks, Khan has spoken to many of his {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/virginia"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Virginia}}}\uc2  neighbors, stressing to them that the upcoming legislative elections will determine the future of their state.\par \par \uc2 Addressing fellow union organizers on Friday morning, Khan said: \u8220\'20\'1CWe really need to fight, and we need Virginia blue.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Members of \uc2 Unite Here\uc2 , a hospitality workers\u8217\'20\'19 union, have knocked on 230,000 doors on behalf of Democratic candidates in Virginia ahead of Tuesday, when all 140 legislative seats in the battleground state will be up for grabs.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are looking to maintain their narrow majority in the house of delegates and flip control of the state senate, which would clear the way for the governor, Glenn Youngkin, to enact his policy agenda. But Democrats warn that Republicans would use their legislative trifecta in Richmond to enact a 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban and roll back access to the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 The results in Virginia carry national implications.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/nov/07/virginia-elections-legislative-seats-republicans-democrats"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  Virginians vote as all 140 legislative seats in battleground state up for grabs}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.07pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Special counsel told Congress 'I am... the decision-maker' in Hunter Biden case - report\par \par \uc2 In his testimony before the House Judiciary Committee today, David Weiss, the justice department special counsel handling the prosecution of Hunter Biden, assured lawmakers he has full control over the case, Politico reports.\par \par \uc2 Republicans have alleged political interference in the investigation of the president\u8217\'20\'19s son, which centers around allegations Biden failed to pay taxes on income from his overseas business dealings, and lied about using drugs on a background check to buy a firearm. Special prosecutors usually testify to Congress only after finishing their investigation, but the justice department and Weiss agreed to a behind-closed-doors session with the GOP-controlled committee.\par \par \uc2 Politico obtained part of Weiss\u8217\'20\'19s testimony, and here\u8217\'20\'19s what it had to say:\par \par \uc2 enltrSpecial Counsel David Weiss told members of the Judiciary Committee (and their staff) behind closed doors today that he is "the decision-maker" in Hunter Biden case.\par \par \uc2 Reiterates he first requested special counsel status in August. More ?? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/wl814o38hD"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/wl814o38hD}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Jordain Carney (@jordainc) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/jordainc/status/1721948187785580765?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 7, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.43pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Among the many cities and states voting today is New York City, where voters are poised to send to the city council a man who was caught up in one of its most high-profile instances of wrongful convictions, the Associated Press reports:\par \par \uc2 The exonerated \u8220\'20\'1CCentral Park Five\u8221\'20\'1D member Yusef Salaam is poised to win a seat Tuesday on the New York City council, marking a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/general-news-36687d63802d44c693798717b8f3ae1f"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 stunning reversal of fortune}}}\uc2  for a political newcomer who was wrongly imprisoned as a teenager in the infamous rape case.\par \par \uc2 Salaam, a Democrat, will represent a central Harlem district on the city council, having run unopposed for the seat in one of many local elections playing out across New York state on Tuesday. He {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/salaam-central-park-new-york-council-primary-508d317b9dce37fd5635ae6128ceec2d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 won his primary election}}}\uc2  in a landslide.\par \par \uc2 The victory will come more than two decades after DNA evidence was used to overturn the convictions of Salaam and four other Black and Latino men in the 1989 rape and beating of a white jogger in Central Park. Salaam was imprisoned for almost seven years.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFor me, this means that we can really become our ancestors\u8217\'20\'19 wildest dreams,\u8221\'20\'1D Salaam said in an interview before the election.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/nov/07/yusef-salaam-nyc-council-seat-central-park-five"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Exonerated \u8216\'20\'18Central Park Five\u8217\'20\'19 member poised to win New York City council seat}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.25pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Less than two weeks after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/oct/27/who-are-victims-maine-shooting"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 18 people}}}\uc2  were killed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/maine-shootings"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 by a gunman}}}\uc2  in their small New England city, residents headed gingerly to polling places there today.\par \par \uc2 The mood was somber. Several shooting survivors remained hospitalized, flags flew at half-staff, and funerals were being held this week for those who died in the attack, the Associated Press reports.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a necessity. We have to do this [vote]. So we can\u8217\'20\'19t neglect it even though we\u8217\'20\'19ve been through a terrible tragedy,\u8221\'20\'1D said James Scribner, 79, a retired teacher and Marine veteran, who was joined by his wife at local school that was transformed into a polling place.\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/maine-shootings"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shootings}}}\uc2  on October 25 at a bar and a bowling alley in Lewiston, Maine, forced tens of thousands of residents to shelter in place for several days. Grocery stores, gas stations and restaurants were closed. The gunman was later found dead from a self-inflicted gunshot wound in a nearby town.\par \par \uc2 Local candidates paused their campaigns for a week after the shootings, and campaigning was different when it resumed, said Jon Connor, a candidate for mayor.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen we restarted campaigning, I was knocking on doors to see how people are doing,\u8221\'20\'1D said Connor, who was greeting voters earlier today. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re meeting people where they are. We want to be respectful.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Lewiston voters were choosing a mayor and filling seven city council and eight school board seats. Some election workers stayed home, either out of safety concerns or to focus on mourning, city clerk Kathy Montejo said.\par \par \uc2 Voter turnout appeared slow but steady.\par \par \uc2 It seems a little quieter, a little more subdued, a little more somber,\u8221\'20\'1D Montejo said.\par \par \uc2 Some voters overcame feelings of vulnerability to get to the polls.\par \par \uc2 It still stays in the back of my mind. But I also can\u8217\'20\'19t let one person make me stay in my house all by myself. I\u8217\'20\'19m still sad. But I had to do my civic duty,\u8221\'20\'1D voter Lori Hallett said.\par \par \uc2 A voter signing a petition in Lewiston, Maine, today. The bulletin board carries a message of support for the community as it recovers from the mass shooting that killed 18 people less than two weeks ago. Photograph: Robert F Bukaty/AP\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.08pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The Iowa governor, Kim Reynolds, broke her neutrality in the Republican primary and endorsed Ron DeSantis for president on Monday, saying she does not believe {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2  can win the general election.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI believe he can\u8217\'20\'19t win,\u8221\'20\'1D Reynolds said {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2024-election/iowa-gov-kim-reynolds-says-trump-cant-win-2024-endorses-ron-desantis-rcna123788"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in an interview with NBC}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CAnd I believe that Ron can.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The endorsement gives DeSantis the support of a deeply popular governor (she has an 81% approval rating among likely caucus-goers, according to a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/iowa-poll/caucus/2023/08/21/iowa-poll-donald-trump-holds-commanding-lead-5-months-to-caucuses/70624481007/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Des Moines Register/NBC poll}}}\uc2  ). It also gives him fuel as he tries to close a significant gap with the former president in polling, both in Iowa and across the US. Trump is currently polling at 45.6% in Iowa, according to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://projects.fivethirtyeight.com/polls/president-primary-r/2024/iowa/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 FiveThirtyEight average of polls}}}\uc2 , while DeSantis is at 17.1%. The Florida governor is also trying to break away from Nikki Haley, with whom he is battling for second place in the race.\par \par \uc2 DeSantis is betting his presidential campaign {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.tampabay.com/news/florida-politics/2023/09/21/ron-desantis-iowa-strategy-risk-donald-trump-president-2024-caucus/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 on a strong showing in Iowa}}}\uc2 , which will hold its caucuses for the GOP nomination on 15 January.\par \par \uc2 Iowa has long held the first caucuses in the presidential nominating contests and its governors do not typically endorse candidates. Reynolds had previously told others, including Trump, she would stay neutral in the contest, the New York Times {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/07/08/us/politics/trump-desantis-iowa-governor.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  reported in July}}}\uc2 . She reversed that on Monday.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs a mother and as a grandmother and as an American, I just felt like I couldn\u8217\'20\'19t stand on the sidelines any longer,\u8221\'20\'1D she said on Monday, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/elections/presidential/caucus/2023/11/06/iowa-gov-kim-reynolds-endorses-ron-desantis-says-trump-cant-win/71473895007/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  according to the Des Moines Register}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CWe have too much at stake. Our country is in a world of hurt. The world is a powder keg. And I think it\u8217\'20\'19s just really important that we put the right person in office.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Full report {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/nov/07/iowa-governor-kim-reynolds-endorses-ron-desantis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.44pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Voters in Houston are heading to the polls today to elect the next mayor of the nation\u8217\'20\'19s fourth largest city, choosing from a crowded field that includes US congresswoman {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/tv-and-radio/2023/jan/16/robin-rue-simmons-the-big-payback-documentary-pbs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sheila Jackson Lee}}}\uc2  and state senator {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/774e30a3ea8a4f718d78c0b133711751"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 John Whitmire}}}\uc2 , two longtime Democratic lawmakers, the Associated Press writes.\par \par \uc2 Jackson Lee and Whitmire have dominated an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/election-houston-mayor-jackson-lee-whitmire-e3915f48429986359f51c92ab3eba9f4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 open mayoral race}}}\uc2  that drew 17 candidates to the ballot in the Texan metropolis and one write-in candidate, and that has been focused on issues of crime, crumbling infrastructure and potential budget shortfalls.\par \par \uc2 If elected, Jackson Lee would be Houston\u8217\'20\'19s first Black female mayor. Since 1995, she has represented Houston in Congress. Whitmire has spent five decades in the Texas legislature, where he has helped drive policies that were tough on crime while casting himself as a reformer.\par \par \uc2 If no candidate manages to get more than half of the vote today, the top two will head to a runoff, which would be held December 9.\par \par \uc2 Jackson Lee, 73, and Whitmire, 74, have touted their experience in a race to lead one of the youngest major cities in the US.\par \par \uc2 About two weeks before the election, Jackson Lee\u8217\'20\'19s campaign had to contend with the release of an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/election-houston-mayor-jackson-lee-recording-21a6c108d82cf1cb72999bd0e0295145"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unverified audio recording}}}\uc2 , which is purported to capture her berating staff members with a barrage of expletives.\par \par \uc2 Booming growth over the last decade in Houston has caused municipal headaches but has also turned the area into an expanding stronghold for Texas Democrats. Although the mayoral race is nonpartisan, most of the candidates are Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Whitmire and Jackson Lee are seeking to replace Mayor Sylvester Turner, who has served eight years and can\u8217\'20\'19t run again because of term limits.\par \par \uc2 File pic: Democratic Texas congresswoman Sheila Jackson Lee on Capitol Hill. Photograph: J Scott Applewhite/AP\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.07pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Supreme court leans toward domestic-violence gun curbs\par \par \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  justices on today appeared inclined to uphold the legality of a federal law that makes it a crime for people under domestic violence restraining orders to have guns in the latest major case to test the willingness of its conservative majority to further expand gun rights, Reuters reports.\par \par \uc2 The justices heard arguments in an appeal by Joe Biden\u8217\'20\'19s administration of a lower court\u8217\'20\'19s ruling striking down the law \u8211\'20\'13 intended to protect victims of domestic abuse - as a violation of the US constitution\u8217\'20\'19s second amendment right to \u8220\'20\'1Ckeep and bear arms\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The New Orleans-based fifth US circuit court of appeals concluded that the measure failed a stringent test set by the supreme court in a 2022 ruling that required gun laws to be \u8220\'20\'1Cconsistent with the nation\u8217\'20\'19s historical tradition of firearm regulation\u8221\'20\'1D in order to survive a Ssecond amendment challenge.\par \par \uc2 Some of the conservative justices questioned the scope of the administration\u8217\'20\'19s argument that, under the second amendment, people who are not law-abiding and responsible \u8211\'20\'13 categories that include domestic abusers \u8211\'20\'13 may be barred from possessing firearms.\par \par \uc2 Some of their questions, however, signaled openness to a standard that would permit laws that disarm people deemed dangerous, as opposed to merely irresponsible.\par \par \uc2 Chief justice John Roberts, who leans conservative, focused on the word \u8220\'20\'1Cresponsible\u8221\'20\'1D, suggesting that it was too broad.\par \par \uc2 I mean, not taking your recycling to the curb on Thursdays, if it\u8217\'20\'19s a serious problem it\u8217\'20\'19s irresponsible,\u8221\'20\'1D Roberts said.\par \par \uc2 He added that:\par \par \uc2 What seems irresponsible to some people might seem like, well, it\u8217\'20\'19s not a big deal to others.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He also cited examples of a person who gets in a fist fight at a sports event or drives a small amount over the speed limit.\par \par \uc2 Solicitor General Elizabeth Prelogar, defending the law on behalf of the Biden administration, told Roberts that she was not using the term \u8220\'20\'1Cnot responsible\u8221\'20\'1D in a colloquial sense, instead asserting \u8220\'20\'1Cthe principle of responsibility as being intrinsically tied to the danger you would present if you had access to firearms\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 And the reason that we use the term \u8216\'20\'18not responsible\u8217\'20\'19 is because it is the standard this court has articulated\u8221\'20\'1D in its three major gun rights rulings in the past 15 years, Prelogar said.\par \par \uc2 Prelogar also told the justices that the law fits within the nation\u8217\'20\'19s tradition of taking guns from people deemed dangerous, thus meeting the standard the court itself has established for withstanding a second amendment challenge.\par \par \uc2 The supreme court justices in October 2022. Photograph: Evelyn Hockstein/Reuters\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.03pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.36pm GMT\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/hunter-biden"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Hunter Biden}}}\uc2  pleaded not guilty last month to three federal gun charges \u8211\'20\'13 in the politically fraught case that could go to trial in the heat of US president Joe Biden\u8217\'20\'19s 2024 re-election campaign.\par \par \uc2 Hunter Biden, 53, the first child of a sitting president to be criminally prosecuted, was arraigned at a court in Wilmington, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/delaware"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Delaware}}}\uc2 , in early October, accused of unlawfully possessing a gun as an illegal drug user and lying about his drug use on a background check form when he bought a Colt Cobra revolver in 2018.\par \par \uc2 The indictment was secured in September by the special counsel, David Weiss (see previous post) , after a plea agreement between Biden and prosecutors collapsed in August. Under that deal, Biden had agreed to plead guilty to misdemeanor tax violations and would avoid punishment on the gun charges if for two years he did not possess a firearm and refrained from using illegal drugs and alcohol.\par \par \uc2 The US justice department has not said whether it will also bring an indictment over separate tax misdemeanour charges but the special counsel has indicated they could come in Washington or in California, where Hunter lives.\par \par \uc2 In Congress, House Republicans \u8211\'20\'13 eager to divert attention from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/donald-trump-investigations-other-charges-b8b064a00caad4306fb54d2f6a320468"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the multiple criminal indictments}}}\uc2  faced by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 are seeking to link Hunter Biden\u8217\'20\'19s dealings to his father\u8217\'20\'19s through an impeachment inquiry. Republicans have been investigating Hunter since his father was Barack Obama \u8217\'20\'19s vice-president.\par \par \uc2 While questions have arisen about the ethics surrounding the Biden family\u8217\'20\'19s international business, no evidence has emerged so far to prove that Biden, in his current or previous office, abused his role or accepted bribes.\par \par \uc2 File pic: Joe and Hunter Biden arrive in Syracuse, New York, on February 4, 2023. Photograph: Andrew Caballero-Reynolds/AFP/Getty Images\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.24pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Hunter Biden prosecutor testifies before Congress in unique event\par \par \uc2 The prosecutor overseeing the investigation into {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/hunter-biden"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Hunter Biden}}}\uc2 , the son of US president {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/joebiden"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Joe Biden}}}\uc2 , is testifying today, in a situation marking the first time a special counsel will appear before the \uc2 US Congress\uc2  in the middle of a case, the Associated Press reports.\par \par \uc2 Special counsel David Weiss is appearing for a transcribed interview before members of the House Judiciary Committee as the US attorney battles Republican allegations that he did not have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/hunter-biden-irs-justice-department-congress-republicans-32d9f33fb0d05c483d76926504d45bef"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 full authority}}}\uc2  in the years-long case into the younger Biden.\par \par \uc2 Mr Weiss is prepared to take this unprecedented step of testifying before the conclusion of his investigation to make clear that he\u8217\'20\'19s had and continues to have full authority over his investigation and to bring charges in any jurisdiction,\u8221\'20\'1D Wyn Hornbuckle, a spokesperson for Weiss, said in a statement issued yesterday.\par \par \uc2 The rare move by the Justice Department to allow a special counsel or any federal prosecutor to face questioning before the conclusion of an investigation indicates just how seriously the department is taking accusations of interference.\par \par \uc2 Weiss\u8217\'20\'19 appearance comes after months of back-and-forth negotiations between Republicans on the Judiciary Committee and the Justice Department as lawmakers subpoenaed several investigators and attorneys involved in the Hunter Biden case.\par \par \uc2 The interview today is expected to focus on testimony from an \uc2 Internal Revenue Service\uc2  agent who claimed that under Weiss, the investigation into the president\u8217\'20\'19s son was \u8220\'20\'1Cslow-walked\u8221\'20\'1D and mishandled.\par \par \uc2 What information, if any, Weiss will be able to provide to Congress is unclear as under Justice Department policy and the law, he will be unable to address the specifics of his investigation.\par \par \uc2 Some more background on this in our next post\u8230\'20\'26.\par \par \uc2 US attorney David Weiss arrives for an interview before members of the House Judiciary Committee today, on Capitol Hill, Washington, DC. Photograph: Jos\u233\'00\'E9 Luis Maga\u241\'00\'F1a/AP\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.09pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Millions are voting in almost 40 states across the country today, in the biggest election between now and the decision on the White House next November.\par \par \uc2 Here are some scenes:\par \par \uc2 Virginia Governor Glenn Youngkin, left, is not on the ballot himself, but his agenda and prospects pretty much are. Here he is with 24th District state Senate candidate Danny Diggs speaking with supporters following an early voting rally in September, in Newport News. Virginia\u8217\'20\'19s voters decide today whether to empower Republicans with full state government control or let Democrats keep serving as a bulwark (with state senate control) against Youngkin\u8217\'20\'19s agenda. Photograph: Kendall Warner/AP\par \par \uc2 Here in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, the mayoral seat is up for grabs.\par \par \uc2 Democratic mayoral candidate Cherelle Parker steps from the voting booth after casting her ballot in Philadelphia today. Photograph: Ryan Collerd/AP\par \par \uc2 Reproductive rights are literally and figuratively Issue 1 in Ohio today.\par \par \uc2 A woman wears a pro-choice button to a canvassing meeting ahead of the election in Columbus, Ohio. Reproductive rights are directly on the ballot in the state today. Photograph: Megan Jelinger/AFP/Getty Images\par \par \uc2 Something else important is on the ballot in Ohio, too.\par \par \uc2 In Ohio, after Issue 1 comes Issue 2 on the ballot: Nikko Griffin, left, and Tyra Patterson, call out to early voters in Cincinnati last week. They urge people to vote for different issues, including Issue 2, which would allow adult-use sale, purchase, and possession of cannabis for Ohioans who are 21 and older. Photograph: Carolyn Kaster/AP\par \par \uc2 Fierce fight in Kentucky gubernatorial contest.\par \par \uc2 A woman walks into the Plandome Village Hall polling place in Plandome, Long Island, New York today. Photograph: Shannon Stapleton/Reuters\par \par \uc2 And here\par \par \uc2 Kentucky Attorney General and Republican gubernatorial candidate Daniel Cameron campaigning at the weekend. He hopes to stop popular Democratic governor Andy Beshear from winning re-election. Photograph: Greg Eans/AP\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.24pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.31pm GMT\par \par \uc2 In the year since the \uc2 US supreme court{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/24/roe-v-wade-overturned-abortion-summary-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturned}}}\uc2  Roe v Wade, a version of this conversation has played out across the country \u8211\'20\'13 and at the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 In 2022, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/11/abortion-midterm-elections-republicans-women-voters"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 won}}}\uc2  every \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related referendums put to voters. This year, Ohio will become the first reliably red state to vote on whether to explicitly add \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights to the state constitution since the fall of Roe.\par \par \uc2 Millions of dollars have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://19thnews.org/2023/11/ohio-issue-1-voter-turnout-spending-outcome-abortion-measure/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 flooded into Ohio}}}\uc2  in the last several weeks. Recent filings show that Ohioans United for Reproductive Rights, a name for the coalition backing Issue 1, has reported bringing in nearly $30m since August, including $3.5m from a group linked to billionaire George Soros.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s far more than the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  side has raised: Protect Women Ohio, a coalition that\u8217\'20\'19s leading the charge against Issue 1, reported raising just shy of $10m. Money flowed into the coalition through its action fund, which received donations from the influential anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group SBA Pro-Life America and the Concord Fund, an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/oct/25/abortion-ban-dark-money-roe-v-wade-right-wing"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 organization linked}}}\uc2  to conservative power broker Leonard Leo.\par \par \uc2 Issue 1 lawn signs are visible across the state, with \u8220\'20\'1CVote Yes\u8221\'20\'1D signs often placed right beside signs urging people to \u8220\'20\'1CVote No\u8221\'20\'1D. Ohio residents reported being bombarded with ads for one side or the other.\par \par \uc2 But the barrage of information about the vote has also left many Ohioans bewildered. Some of the confusion stems from the fact that this is the second time Ohioans have gone to the polls this year to vote on an \u8220\'20\'1CIssue 1\u8221\'20\'1D. In August, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/aug/08/ohio-issue-1-rejected-state-constitution"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ohioans voted on a measure}}}\uc2  that would have made it harder to amend the state constitution. At that time, people who supported \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights were urged to vote \u8220\'20\'1Cno\u8221\'20\'1D. Now, they have to vote \u8220\'20\'1Cyes\u8221\'20\'1D to protect the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Full report {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/nov/07/ohio-vote-abortion-rights-ban-what-is-issue-1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Pamphlets lay on a table during a pro-choice canvassing meeting on November 5, 2023, in Ohio. As the clock ticks down on a high-stakes vote in the US state of Ohio, activists are out in force urging voters to decide whether to explicitly enshrine the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state constitution. Photograph: Megan Jelinger/AFP/Getty Images\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, here is the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2022/jun/28/tracking-where-abortion-laws-stand-in-every-state"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 piece}}}\uc2 , tracking where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws stand in every state. Almost 17 months after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/24/roe-v-wade-overturned-abortion-summary-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Roe fell}}}\uc2  at the hands of a hard-right controlled Supreme Court, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now nearly completely banned in 14 US states.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2022/jun/28/tracking-where-abortion-laws-stand-in-every-state"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tracking where \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  laws stand in every state}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.14pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/nov/07/ohio-vote-abortion-rights-ban-what-is-issue-1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 vote on Issue 1 in Ohio today}}}\uc2  will have both political and medical consequences that stretch beyond the state. Issue 1 is the only \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related referendum this year, and the results will indicate whether the backlash to the demise of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Roe v Wade}}}\uc2  will continue to translate into wins at the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 If Issue 1 fails, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ohio"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ohio}}}\uc2  state supreme court will be free to reinstate a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, outlawing the procedure before many people even know they\u8217\'20\'19re pregnant. Ohioans who want abortions will need to flee to other states for the procedure, contributing to pressure on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics throughout the country.\par \par \uc2 Supporters of Issue 1 are cautiously optimistic: an October poll {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bw.edu/assets/community-research-institute/october_ohio_issues_poll-final.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 found}}}\uc2  that 58% of Ohio residents planned to vote yes. \u8220\'20\'1CYou just start going through these doomsday scenarios of what happens if we have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban,\u8221\'20\'1D said Representative Emilia Sykes, a Democrat whose district includes Akron, Ohio. \u8220\'20\'1CWe are going to prevail on Tuesday, and hopefully continue to send the message to leave us alone. Really, just leave us alone.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Over the last several weeks, Parinita Singh, 32, has spent roughly 30 hours canvassing houses in support of Issue 1.\par \par \uc2 At one home, with a sign out front reading \u8220\'20\'1CBe a Patriot: VOTE PRO-LIFE\u8221\'20\'1D, she entered into a cordial but spirited verbal sparring match with a man in his 80s. The pair touched on seemingly every argument in the US \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate: is a fetus a person or a clump of cells? Can\u8217\'20\'19t people just choose to give up their kids for adoption? Should men get a say? What about religion?\par \par \uc2 The man, who declined to give his name to the Guardian, tried to press Bible pamphlets into Singh\u8217\'20\'19s hands.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CGod doesn\u8217\'20\'19t force his opinions on anybody,\u8221\'20\'1D he told her.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSounds like he is, if he\u8217\'20\'19s telling women not to have abortions,\u8221\'20\'1D Singh replied.\par \par \uc2 Pro-Choice canvasser Summer McLain, 27, and her mother Lorie McLain, 61, look at a map of a neighborhood on November 5, before canvassing ahead of the general election in Columbus, Ohio. Photograph: Megan Jelinger/AFP/Getty Images\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.58pm GMT\par \par \uc2 In Ohio today, voters will decide whether to pass a constitutional amendment guaranteeing an individual right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other forms of reproductive healthcare, the Associated Press reports.\par \par \uc2 Ohio is the only state to consider a statewide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights question this year, fueling tens of millions of dollars in campaign spending, boisterous rallies for and against the amendment, and months of advertising and social media messaging, some of it {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/abortion-ohio-constitutional-amendment-election-misinformation-d7b3d8273389a432b011964c14959789"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 misleading}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 With a single spotlight on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights this year, advocates on both sides of the issue are watching the outcome for signs of voter sentiment heading into 2024, when \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights supporters are planning to put measures on the ballot in several other states, including {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/abortion-arizona-ballot-voters-2024-a7ea711840cbe2103a9c75c4c4dd4ef3"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Arizona}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/missouri-abortion-68362de7c0c0f6344543308ec4569b5b"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Missouri}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/abortion-ashley-moody-florida-politics-0db0a1a4deb47fa1b82410b7670b16e3"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Florida}}}\uc2 . Early {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/election-2023-ohio-abortion-marijuana-early-voting-eaf1fce0cac0a309efd487e0f594ea8f"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voter turnout}}}\uc2  has also been robust.\par \par \uc2 Public polling {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/abortion-poll-roe-dobbs-ban-opinion-fcfdfc5a799ac3be617d99999e92eabe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shows}}}\uc2  about two-thirds of Americans say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should generally be legal in the earliest stages of pregnancy, a sentiment that has been underscored in half a dozen states since the Supreme Court\u8217\'20\'19s decision {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/abortion-supreme-court-decision-854f60302f21c2c35129e58cf8d8a7b0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reversing Roe v. Wade}}}\uc2  in June 2022.\par \par \uc2 In both {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/2022-midterm-elections-abortion-us-supreme-court-health-michigan-23bfba4a07de57b175d9de1281ecace6"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Democratic and deeply Republican states}}}\uc2  \u8212\'20\'14 California, Kansas, Kentucky, Michigan, Montana and Vermont \u8212\'20\'14 voters have either affirmed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access or turned back attempts to undermine the right.\par \par \uc2 Voter approval of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/election-2023-abortion-rights-ohio-e3edc16a1bfaa22b9e72bc1d6fb76dd0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 constitutional amendment in Ohio}}}\uc2 , known as Issue 1, would undo a 2019 state law passed by Republicans that bans most abortions at around six weeks into pregnancy, with no exceptions for rape and incest. That law, currently on hold because of court challenges, is one of roughly two dozen restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  the Ohio Legislature has passed in recent years.\par \par \uc2 Lauren Miracle, right, holds her son Dawson, 1, as she helps her daughter Oaklynn, 3, fill out a child's practice ballot before voting herself at a polling location in the Washington Township House in Oregonia, Ohio, Tuesday, Nov. 7. Photograph: Carolyn Kaster/AP\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.39pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Supreme court to weigh legality of gun ownership of people under domestic violence restraining orders\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  is set to weigh the legality of a federal law that makes it a crime for people under domestic violence restraining orders to have guns, Reuters reports.\par \par \uc2 This is the latest major case to test the willingness of the court\u8217\'20\'19s conservative majority to further expand gun rights.\par \par \uc2 Oral arguments are scheduled today in an appeal by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/joebiden"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Joe Biden}}}\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s administration of a lower court \u8217\'20\'19s ruling striking down the law - intended to protect victims of domestic abuse - as a violation of the US Constitution\u8217\'20\'19s Second Amendment right to \u8220\'20\'1Ckeep and bear arms.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The New Orleans-based 5th US Circuit Court of Appeals concluded that the measure failed a stringent test set by the Supreme Court in a 2022 ruling that required gun laws to be \u8220\'20\'1Cconsistent with the nation\u8217\'20\'19s historical tradition of firearm regulation\u8221\'20\'1D in order to survive a Second Amendment challenge.\par \par \uc2 Advocacy groups for victims of domestic violence have warned of the grave danger posed by armed abusers, citing studies that show that the presence of guns increases the chances that an abused intimate partner will die.\par \par \uc2 In a nation bitterly divided over how to address firearms violence including frequent mass shootings, the court \u8217\'20\'19s 6-3 conservative majority has taken an expansive view of the Second Amendment and has broadened gun rights in three landmark rulings since 2008.\par \par \uc2 Its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/23/us-supreme-court-new-york-law-gun-control"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2022 ruling}}}\uc2  in a case called New York State Rifle and Pistol Association v Bruen recognized a constitutional right to carry a handgun in public for self defense, striking down a New York state law.\par \par \uc2 The current case involves Zackey Rahimi, a Texas man who pleaded guilty to illegally possessing guns in violation of the law at issue on Tuesday while he was subject to a restraining order for assaulting his girlfriend in a parking lot and later threatening to shoot her, leading to a series of court decisions and appeals.\par \par \uc2 Biden\u8217\'20\'19s administration has said the law should survive because of the long tradition in the US of taking guns from people deemed dangerous.\par \par \uc2 Supporters of Rahimi have argued that judges too easily issue restraining orders in an unfair process that results in the deprivation of the constitutional gun rights of accused abusers.\par \par \uc2 A ruling is expected by the end of June.\par \par \uc2 Executive Director of Stand Up America Christina Harvey (C) delivers remarks during a press conference calling on the Senate leadership to investigate Justices \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  and \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  as well as to pass a binding code of ethics for Supreme Court justices in front of the Supreme Court building in Washington, DC, USA, 30 October 2023. Photograph: Shawn Thew/EPA\par \par \uc2 The supreme court itself is under pressure to institute ethics rules, in the wake of scandals chiefly involving its most conservative judges.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.43pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.13pm GMT\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, Biden weakness key issues for voters in national elections\par \par \uc2 Voters go to the polls in almost 40 states today in crucial elections that will prove a major test of sentiment on Democratic and Republican leadership and key policies less than one year out from the presidential election.\par \par \uc2 Ohio has put the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly on the ballot, asking voters to choose whether to include the right in the state constitution. Such measures have had success in several states that have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/nov/09/vermont-abortion-vote-first-us-state-constitution"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tested them}}}\uc2  since Roe v Wade was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/24/roe-v-wade-overturned-abortion-summary-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturned}}}\uc2  in June 2022, including red state Kansas and swing state Michigan.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also a major voting issue in Virginia today, where results are expected to prove a bellwether for how things could go across the country next year, too, on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and on Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 appeal to voters.\par \par \uc2 Virginia Republican governor Glenn Youngkin, a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/oct/30/hes-pretty-smooth-glenn-youngkin-of-virginia-may-be-a-challenger-for-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rising GOP star}}}\uc2 , wants to pass a law limiting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights to 15 weeks, as what the party hopes will be a kind of compromise between the harsh bans enacted elsewhere and the Roe-era right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to fetal viability of around 25 weeks. Democrats narrowly control the state senate while Republicans control the lower chamber, the house of delegates, in the state\u8217\'20\'19s general assembly. If the GOP can flip the house today, it\u8217\'20\'19s all systems go for Youngkin, raising his national profile as a moderate (by today\u8217\'20\'19s hard right standards) Republican even higher.\par \par \uc2 If Democrats cling onto control in the Virginia senate and then Democratic governor Andy Beshear is re-elected in the strongly pro-Trump state of Kentucky, then the party will take huge cheer from either or both of those victories.\par \par \uc2 Beshear has been trying to avoid being roped in with Joe Biden, during his election campaign, as the US president is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/nov/07/donald-trump-joe-biden-state-poll-2024-president-election"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 polling poorly}}}\uc2  across the board but particularly with the kind of independents and moderate conservatives he lured away from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2020 and, especially after Roe, in the midterm elections last year.\par \par \uc2 Virginia Governor Glenn Youngkin serves coffee during a visit to a diner on Election Day in Manassas, Virginia, today. Photograph: Kevin Lamarque/Reuters\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.54pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Election day puts \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  top of agenda for many voters\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Tuesday is the biggest voting day in America before the presidential election next November and while millions of voters go to the polls in almost 40 states there are some key races and issues that everyone is watching.\par \par \uc2 The polls are open in many places already and we\u8217\'20\'19ll bring you the news as it happens during the day \u8211\'20\'13 and also tonight when polls close and we start to see which way things are leaning.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s what\u8217\'20\'19s afoot:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be a leading issue in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/nov/07/elections-virginia-new-jersey-ohio-abortion-biden"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voting today}}}\uc2  across the country, as it was in the midterms last November and will be in 2024, after the \uc2 US supreme court{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/24/roe-v-wade-overturned-abortion-summary-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturned}}}\uc2  Roe v Wade in June 2022, ending the federal right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It\u8217\'20\'19s directly on the agenda in Ohio today, with a ballot measure to enshrine the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state constitution. And it\u8217\'20\'19s front and center in Virginia, where the governor wants to introduce a 15-week ban on the procedure.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Virginia is one to watch as all 140 seats in the state\u8217\'20\'19s general assembly are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/nov/07/virginia-elections-legislative-seats-republicans-democrats"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 up for grabs}}}\uc2 . The GOP control the lower house of delegates and want to flip the upper chamber, the Democrat-majority senate and hand {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/oct/30/hes-pretty-smooth-glenn-youngkin-of-virginia-may-be-a-challenger-for-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 high-profile}}}\uc2  Republican governor Glenn Youngkin the trifecta. That would smooth passage of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/nov/01/republicans-abortion-ads-virginia-election"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 litmus-test}}}\uc2  15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  limit he wants to pass.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 In two big gubernatorial races, Kentucky \u8217\'20\'19s popular Democratic governor {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2019/dec/12/kentucky-ex-felons-vote-democratic-governor-andy-beshear"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Andy Beshear}}}\uc2  is running for re-election against Republican state attorney general Daniel Cameron and in Mississippi, Republican governor Tate Reeves, is running for re-election against the Democrat {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/oct/29/brandon-presley-elvis-presley-mississippi-governor-tate-reeves"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Brandon Presley}}}\uc2 , a former small-town mayor who\u8217\'20\'19s a cousin of Elvis Presley.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Election day means that the court is not sitting in New York where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his business empire are going through a $250m fraud trial that threatens to end his business career in the state where it all began. After an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/nov/06/trump-fraud-trial-speeches-grandstanding"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 uproarious stint}}}\uc2  on the stand yesterday, Trump\u8217\'20\'19s back in Florida for a rally tomorrow while daughter Ivanka testifies as a witness at the civil trial.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  is set to hear oral arguments in a key case that tests the legality of a federal law that makes it a crime for people under domestic-violence restraining orders to have guns.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231107ejb700234\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
