\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Ohio votes on  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rights in test of post-Roe backlash\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman in Akron, Ohio \par \uc2 1,029 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 November 2023\par 19:50 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Vote will have both political and medical consequences that go beyond Ohio as supporters of Issue 1 are cautiously optimistic\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are back on the ballot on Tuesday, with voters in Ohio heading to the polls to vote on Issue 1, a proposal to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 The vote will have both political and medical consequences that stretch beyond Ohio. Issue 1 is the only \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related referendum this year, and the results will indicate whether the backlash to the demise of Roe v Wade will continue to translate into wins at the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 If Issue 1 fails, the Ohio state supreme court will be free to reinstate a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, outlawing the procedure before many people even know they are pregnant. Ohioans who want abortions will need to flee to other states for the procedure, contributing to pressure on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics throughout the country.\par \par \uc2 Supporters of Issue 1 are cautiously optimistic: an October poll {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bw.edu/assets/community-research-institute/october_ohio_issues_poll-final.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 found}}}\uc2  that 58% of Ohio residents planned to vote yes. \u8220\'20\'1CYou just start going through these doomsday scenarios of what happens if we have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban,\u8221\'20\'1D said Representative Emilia Sykes, a Democrat whose district includes Akron, Ohio. \u8220\'20\'1CWe are going to prevail on Tuesday, and hopefully continue to send the message to leave us alone. Really, just leave us alone.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Over the last several weeks, Parinita Singh, 32, has spent roughly 30 hours canvassing houses in support of Issue 1.\par \par \uc2 At one home, with a sign out front reading \u8220\'20\'1CBe a Patriot: VOTE PRO-LIFE\u8221\'20\'1D, she entered into a cordial but spirited verbal sparring match with a man in his 80s. The pair touched on seemingly every argument in the US \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate: is a fetus a person or a clump of cells? Can\u8217\'20\'19t people just choose to give up their kids for adoption? Should men get a say? What about religion?\par \par \uc2 The man, who declined to give his name to the Guardian, tried to press Bible pamphlets into Singh\u8217\'20\'19s hands.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CGod doesn\u8217\'20\'19t force his opinions on anybody,\u8221\'20\'1D he told her.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSounds like he is, if he\u8217\'20\'19s telling women not to have abortions,\u8221\'20\'1D Singh replied.\par \par \uc2 In the year since the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade, a version of this conversation has played out across the country \u8211\'20\'13 and at the ballot box. In 2022, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/11/abortion-midterm-elections-republicans-women-voters"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 won}}}\uc2  every \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related referendum put to voters. This year, Ohio will become the first reliably red state to vote on whether to explicitly add \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights to the state constitution since the fall of Roe.\par \par \uc2 Millions of dollars have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://19thnews.org/2023/11/ohio-issue-1-voter-turnout-spending-outcome-abortion-measure/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 flooded into Ohio}}}\uc2  in the last several weeks. Recent filings show that Ohioans United for Reproductive Rights, a name for the coalition backing Issue 1, has reported bringing in nearly $30m since August, including $3.5m from a group linked to the billionaire \uc2 George Soros\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s far more than the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  side has raised: Protect Women Ohio, a coalition that\u8217\'20\'19s leading the charge against Issue 1, reported raising just shy of $10m. Money flowed into the coalition through its action fund, which received donations from the influential anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group SBA Pro-Life America and the Concord Fund, an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/oct/25/abortion-ban-dark-money-roe-v-wade-right-wing"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 organization linked}}}\uc2  to the conservative power broker Leonard Leo.\par \par \uc2 Issue 1 lawn signs are visible across the state, with \u8220\'20\'1CVote Yes\u8221\'20\'1D signs often placed right beside signs urging people to \u8220\'20\'1CVote No\u8221\'20\'1D. Ohio residents reported being bombarded with ads for one side or the other.\par \par \uc2 But the barrage of information about the vote has also left many Ohioans bewildered. Some of the confusion stems from the fact that this is the second time Ohioans have gone to the polls this year to vote on an \u8220\'20\'1CIssue 1\u8221\'20\'1D. In August, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/aug/08/ohio-issue-1-rejected-state-constitution"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ohioans voted on a measure}}}\uc2  that would have made it harder to amend the state constitution. At that time, people who supported \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights were urged to vote \u8220\'20\'1Cno\u8221\'20\'1D. Now, they have to vote \u8220\'20\'1Cyes\u8221\'20\'1D to protect the procedure.\par \par \uc2 That swap has been a \u8220\'20\'1Cdisaster\u8221\'20\'1D, said one \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights coalition worker. Marian Carcel, a 71-year-old who lives near Akron and has been knocking on doors to support Issue 1, says she keeps meeting people who don\u8217\'20\'19t know how to vote for what they want.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSome people thought, \u8216\'20\'18Oh, well I\u8217\'20\'19m done. I\u8217\'20\'19ve already done what I\u8217\'20\'19m supposed to do, I voted no in August,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D Carcel said. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd then they\u8217\'20\'19re like, \u8216\'20\'18Well, I voted no in August, aren\u8217\'20\'19t I supposed to vote no now?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  forces have also tried to seed opposition to the measure by arguing that Issue 1 would let minors get abortions and even gender-affirming care without their parents\u8217\'20\'19 consent \u8211\'20\'13 a dubious suggestion, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/nov/03/ohio-abortion-vote-misinformation-warning"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 legal experts told the Guardian}}}\uc2 . Still, anti-Issue 1 signs often urge people to vote to \u8220\'20\'1Cprotect children\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cprotect parents\u8217\'20\'19 rights\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 At a pro-Issue 1 rally on Saturday, dozens of attendees carried signs with messages like \u8220\'20\'1CMind Your Own Uterus\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1CGirls Just Want to Have Fundamental Rights\u8221\'20\'1D. One young girl carried a neon pink sign that read, in a childish scrawl, \u8220\'20\'1CVote Yes on One,\u8221\'20\'1D complete with nine shaky exclamation points.\par \par \uc2 But not all Ohioans trust the polls.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf we went off polls, Donald J Trump would have never been president,\u8221\'20\'1D said Singh, the canvasser. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m anxious AF. I haven\u8217\'20\'19t had a good night\u8217\'20\'19s sleep in over a month right now, because I\u8217\'20\'19m just like, \u8216\'20\'18What\u8217\'20\'19s gonna happen?\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As Singh canvassed an Akron neighborhood, she met several men who said they planned to vote for Issue 1, especially because they worried about rape victims being forced to carry pregnancies to term. Ohio\u8217\'20\'19s six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban does not include exceptions for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI believe in people having the ability to do what they want, having the freedom to make their own choices,\u8221\'20\'1D said David Lewis, as he watched his two daughters play on his porch. \u8220\'20\'1CI wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t want their rights to be taken away in the future.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231107ejb7001jm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
