\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Anti- \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  misinformation mounting ahead of key Ohio vote, experts warn\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 955 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 November 2023\par 11:18 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Mistruths and outright lies abound in Republican advertising as the state gears up for crucial vote on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, say criticsOhio purged 26,000 voters days before referendum deadline\par \par \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s ad left Shyla Thurston infuriated.\par \par \uc2 In the ad, DeWine, Ohio\u8217\'20\'19s Republican governor, stands next to his wife, Fran DeWine, as they speak directly to the camera. They assure voters that they have \u8220\'20\'1Ccarefully studied\u8221\'20\'1D Issue 1, the name of a November ballot initiative to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in Ohio\u8217\'20\'19s state constitution. And, the couple say, they have realized it goes too far.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIssue 1 would allow an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at any time during a pregnancy, and it would deny parents the right to be involved when their daughter is making the most important decision of her life,\u8221\'20\'1D Fran DeWine tells voters.\par \par \uc2 But Issue 1 would only allow abortions past fetal viability \u8211\'20\'13 a benchmark that generally occurs around 24 weeks of pregnancy \u8211\'20\'13 in certain cases. Legal experts say that the amendment is also unlikely to rewrite the existing Ohio law that already requires minors to get permission from their parents before they get abortions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey want to confuse you, so that you don\u8217\'20\'19t vote for it. Instead of putting factual information out, it\u8217\'20\'19s lies being put out there by elected officials that people trusted to vote in, and they\u8217\'20\'19re there leading by lying,\u8221\'20\'1D said Thurston, a 37-year-old office manager who lives outside Columbus.\par \par \uc2 They want to confuse you, so that you don\u8217\'20\'19t vote for it... It\u8217\'20\'19s lies being put out there by elected officials\par \par \uc2 Columbus resident Shyla Thurston\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, Ohioans will vote on whether to amend the state constitution to enshrine the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But powerful anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  forces are arguing that the amendment will go beyond guaranteeing access to the procedure to imperil parents\u8217\'20\'19 rights when it comes to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and even gender-affirming care. Experts and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters say these claims are spreading misinformation about the proposed amendment.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not about medical treatment. It\u8217\'20\'19s not about gender identity. It is about reproductive freedom. It\u8217\'20\'19s about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other reproductive rights. It\u8217\'20\'19s about adults,\u8221\'20\'1D said Tracy Thomas, director of the Center for Constitutional Law at the University of Akron School of Law. \u8220\'20\'1CThese are just arguments trying to say this amendment is about something that it\u8217\'20\'19s not.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The DeWine ad is the work of a coalition known as Protect Women Ohio. Over the course of the campaign, Protect Women Ohio and affiliated groups have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/oct/25/abortion-ban-dark-money-roe-v-wade-right-wing"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 received millions}}}\uc2  from the influential anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group Susan B Anthony Pro-Life America, which operates out of Washington DC, as well as from the Concord Fund, an advocacy group linked to Leonard Leo, a behind-the-scenes power broker credited with helping to shape the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s current conservative majority. (\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters are swimming in out-of-state money, too, with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/ohio-abortion-amendment-election-campaign-fundraising-f14d984b5b22ce64a2cfa8f53ac4ce24"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 millions pouring in from billionaires}}}\uc2  and groups that are associated with them.)\par \par \uc2 Another ad paid for by Protect Women Ohio argues that if the amendment passes, children will be able to get gender-affirming care without their parents\u8217\'20\'19 permission.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYour daughter is young, vulnerable, online. Pushed to change her sex, or to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . You have some right to help her through this, but activists want to take all of that away,\u8221\'20\'1D one ad\u8217\'20\'19s narrator declares, before the sound of a door slamming shut. \u8220\'20\'1CUnder their proposed amendment to the Ohio constitution, the state shall not interfere with individuals getting abortions or sex changes.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 However, Issue 1 says nothing about gender-affirming care. If it passes, it would protect reproductive decisions \u8220\'20\'1Cincluding but not limited to\u8221\'20\'1D the right to access contraception and fertility treatments, continue or end a pregnancy, and more.\par \par \uc2 While some forms of gender-affirming care can affect people\u8217\'20\'19s ability to reproduce, some experts say the link is a stretch.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are a lot of things that we do that might have some bearing on our ability to reproduce. That doesn\u8217\'20\'19t mean that all of those things are going to be protected,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jonathan Entin, an emeritus professor at Case Western Reserve University School of Law. \u8220\'20\'1CI get why people are focusing on the gender-affirming care issue. It\u8217\'20\'19s a big, contentious issue. And it raises all sorts of questions that people have really strong views about. But just because it\u8217\'20\'19s a contentious issue doesn\u8217\'20\'19t necessarily mean that Issue 1 can sensibly be interpreted as protecting a way to gender-affirming care.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 If something is supported by the majority of people, it\u8217\'20\'19s not actually a radical position\par \par \uc2 Akron resident Matthew Balsinger\par \par \uc2 If the amendment fails, Ohio may find itself under a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, which briefly took effect after Roe v Wade was overturned but has since been paused by a state court. If the state\u8217\'20\'19s supreme court decides to reinstate the ban, the consequences could reverberate across the midwest, since already-overwhelmed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics in the rest of the country will have to handle the onslaught of patients fleeing Ohio.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey don\u8217\'20\'19t seem to be focused on making an argument on the rhetoric \u8211\'20\'13 like \u8216\'20\'18Hey, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  destroys lives\u8217\'20\'19 or \u8216\'20\'18We support pro-life,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D said Matthew Balsinger, a 34-year-old high school science teacher who lives in the Akron area. \u8220\'20\'1CThey don\u8217\'20\'19t seem to be focusing on that message. They instead want to portray it as this cultural war: \u8216\'20\'18Look how radical the people who are putting this forward are.\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf something is supported by the majority of people, it\u8217\'20\'19s not actually a radical position,\u8221\'20\'1D Balsinger added.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231103ejb3001uu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
